INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5301,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10335411','Role of intraoperative processes of care during major upper gastrointestinal oncological resection in postoperative outcomes: a scoping review protocol.','Rajendran L, Hopkins A, Hallet J, Sinha R, Tanwani J, Kao MM, Eskander A, Barabash V, Idestrup C, Perez P, Jerath A.','2023','oesophagectomy hepatectomy oesophagectomy hepatectomy charted 392ug 392ug regionalisation oesophagectomy hepatectomy endarterectomy 068339supp001 oesophagectomy hepatectomy preselected oesophagectomy hepatectomy hepatectomy oesophagectomy hepactectomy leaderships sunnybrook lucksheee pperezde bagante ruzzenente beal ceppa nakeeb pearse leapfroggroup ejh esophagectomy moonesinghe boney manecke asemota michard rashidi kobewka djt vonlanthen slankamenac breitenstein 0b013e31821d4a43 bilimoria bentrem feinglass birkmeyer stukel siewers nejmsa035205 finks birkmeyer nejmsa1010705 alabaster ashiku esophagectomy jtd gawande semel esophagectomy 0b013e318213862f pucher allgood dikken dassen lemmens vep lingsma klazinga oesophago e0183955 gasper glidden 0b013e3181b47c79 simunovic urbach niemeyer seshadri coolsen mme lun hing rosato sauter leapfroggroup behman coburn esw hallet durkin schisler lohser esophagectomy baar glatz thoracotomic esophagectomy s12871 hallet jerath turgeon mcglynn nejm199609263351311 hannan popp feustel endarterectomy hs1201 lilley lindvall lillemoe skelhorne nenshi jerath e1026 cmajo birkmeyer dimick birkmeyer njo levac levac lillie 068339supp001','Abstract Introduction Optimal delivery and organisation of care is critical for surgical outcomes and healthcare systems efficiency. Anaesthesia volumes have been recently associated with improved postoperative recovery outcomes; however, the mechanism is unclear. Understanding the individual processes of care (interventions received by the patient) is important to design effective systems that leverage the volume-outcome association to improve patient care. The primary objective of this scoping review is to systematically map the evidence regarding intraoperative processes of care for upper gastrointestinal cancer surgery. We aim to synthesise the quantity, type, and scope of studies on intraoperative processes of care in adults who undergo major upper gastrointestinal cancer surgeries (oesophagectomy, hepatectomy, pancreaticoduodenectomy, and gastrectomy) to better understand the volume-outcome relationship for anaesthesiology care. Methods and analysis This scoping review will follow the Arksey and O’Malley framework and Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) extension framework for scoping reviews. We will systematically search MEDLINE, Embase and Cochrane databases for original research articles published after 2010 examining postoperative outcomes in adult patients undergoing either: oesophagectomy, hepatectomy, pancreaticoduodenectomy, or gastrectomy, which report at least one intraoperative processes of care (intervention or framework) applied by anaesthesia or surgery. The data from included studies will be extracted, charted, and summarised both quantitatively and qualitatively through descriptive statistics and narrative synthesis. Ethics and dissemination No ethics approval is required for this scoping review. Results will be disseminated through publication targeted at relevant stakeholders in anaesthesiology and cancer surgery. Trial registration number 10.17605/OSF.IO/392UG; https://archive.org/details/osf-registrations-392ug-v1 . Keywords: adult oncology, gastrointestinal tumours, adult anaesthesia, adult surgery Strengths and limitations of this study. Broad and comprehensive search of all recent literature from several academic databases on how higher volume anaesthesiology care leads to improved outcomes in adults undergoing upper gastrointestinal surgery. Systematic search strategy, study selection, and data extraction conforming to established standards for scoping reviews. Synthesis of literature with comprehensive list of anaesthesia, surgical, and shared intraoperative care processes and related outcome measures. Engagement of relative stakeholders in anaesthesiology, cancer surgery, policymakers, and patients in research and dissemination of findings. Quality assessment and grading of included articles will not be performed (as per standard approach for scoping reviews) limiting ability to provide evidence for recommendations. Introduction Upper gastrointestinal (GI) cancers refer to cancers affecting the upper GI tract (oesophagus, stomach, duodenum, liver, pancreas, and bile ducts) and are among the leading causes of cancer deaths. 1 Surgical resection is the cornerstone of curative management for these cancers, but is associated with high postoperative morbidity and mortality in up to 50% of patients. 2–6 Optimal care delivery and organisation is critical to minimise postoperative morbidity for patients with upper GI cancers, as well as for health systems’ and hospitals’ efficiency and sustainability. Indeed, postoperative morbidity significantly impacts recovery and patient experience, and results in long-term disability, which is a core perioperative outcome and priority for patients. 7 Furthermore, it is a major driver of increased healthcare costs (five times that of surgery without morbidity). 8–10 Higher hospital and surgeon procedural volumes are associated with improved short-term (morbidity and mortality) and long-term (recurrence and survival) outcomes for cancer surgery. 11–16 These data have already led to policy changes and regionalisation of complex cancer surgery. 17–22 Many efforts have been made to standardise hospital and surgeon volume, training and postoperative recovery pathways. 11–16 23 24 In particular, upper GI cancer surgery (oesophagectomy, hepatectomy, and pancreatectomy) are targeted by volume-outcome initiatives recommending minimum clinical volumes for both hospitals and surgeons. 25 These procedures also require unique intraoperative management that differs from other procedures and may impact postoperative outcomes, such as specific cardiorespiratory and end-organ perfusion goals of care, pain management, and long operating time, that make them sensitive to variation in experience and expertise of the perioperative care team. 26–29 More recently, a similar observation was made for anaesthesiology procedural volume in upper GI cancer surgery, which is independent of surgeon and hospital volume. 30 However, the volume-outcome relationship in anaesthesiology requires further examination, including better understanding of potentially complex underlying processes care. As outlined in the Donabedian framework for health services and quality of care, improvements in outcomes require an understanding of structures (organisational properties of the system—what is the structure of care for the patient?) and processes (the clinical processes—what is done to the patient?) of care. 31–33 Understanding the care processes that are linked to better outcomes is critical to design systems, which can effectively leverage the volume-outcome relation for the benefit of patients, hospitals, and health systems. For instance, identifying what processes of care are used by high-volume anaesthesiologists to yield better outcomes would inform care pathways and organisation. Processes of care are heterogeneous and can range from single interventions such as antimicrobial prophylaxis to multiphase interventions including implementation of protocols in fluid management or blood transfusion ( online supplemental data 1 ). Additionally, sets of processes of care are specific to a condition or procedure. One study identified a group of processes involved in carotid endarterectomy. 34 Other studies have investigated processes of care within palliative care surgery 35 or emergency general surgery. 36 However, there is limited data regarding what set of processes of care are essential to delivering high quality intraoperative care for patients undergoing GI cancer surgery. 37 Most studies in the literature do not clearly label ‘process of care’, making it difficult to identify the size and limitations of this body of evidence. In addition, there is a large divide between the surgical and anaesthetic literature, though both specialties are integral to intraoperative care. Documenting key processes of intraoperative care potentially associated with better outcomes is necessary to further study their role in volume-outcome relationships and provide best practice documents. Supplementary data bmjopen-2022-068339supp001.pdf (98.2KB, pdf) Our overall goals are to direct future research efforts, by establishing a comprehensive list of processes of care that can be examined towards understanding what underlies the volume-outcome relationship for anaesthesiology care. Hence the primary objective of this scoping review is to systematically map the evidence regarding intraoperative processes of care for upper GI cancer surgery. Specifically, we aim to identify the quantity, type, and scope of studies on intraoperative processes of care for upper GI cancer surgery (oesophagectomy, hepatectomy, pancreaticoduodenectomy, and gastrectomy). A scoping review will provide awareness of the current literature around intraoperative processes of care for upper GI cancer surgery and facilitate the synthesis and dissemination of what is currently known and identify gaps for further exploration. It is our hope that this review will support research to inform intraoperative care organisation and delivery, to further improve outcomes for patients undergoing surgery for upper GI cancers. Methods and analysis A scoping review methodology will be used to explore the literature describing intraoperative processes of care for upper GI cancer surgery. Scoping reviews are ideally suited to examine broad research questions that aim to describe research activity in a specific field. 38–40 We will follow the Arksey and O’Malley framework for scoping reviews, as well as the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) extension for scoping reviews checklist. 38–41 This protocol is registered with Open Science Framework. Identifying the research question This review focuses on areas of shared surgical-anaesthesia decision-making and anaesthesia processes of care; for example, haemostasis and fluid management. The scoping review will answer the following questions: What are the intraoperative processes of care that have been examined for upper GI cancer surgery? What outcomes have been measured to assess the effect of intraoperative processes of care for upper GI cancer surgery? What is the direction of the effect of studied intraoperative processes of care on outcomes for upper GI cancer surgery? The assessment will also be stratified by the type of procedure. These questions and the initial protocol were developed by the research team in an iterative process. The protocol will be revised as needed throughout the review process. Search strategy and information sources The search strategy was developed for MEDLINE in consultation with a health sciences librarian, guided by our research questions. The full search strategy can be found in online supplemental data 2 . A similar search will be conducted with Embase and Cochrane. A preliminary search in MEDLINE has identified potential citations. We will restrict the study to articles published from January 2010 to March 2021 to best capture contemporary processes of care that are currently used. We will also review medical society guidelines (including American College of Surgeons, American Society of Anesthesiologists, Canadian Association of General Surgeons, Canadian Anesthesiologists’ Society) and perform a search of cited references to identify any additional studies. Study selection Study selection will start by an initial review of titles and abstracts. In anticipation of a high volume of papers, 10% of the title and abstract review will be done independently by two reviewers, to ensure consensus agreement in the selection process. Subsequently, each remaining review will be independently conducted independently by a single reviewer. We will seek to obtain full texts for each study, including through library requests and direct contact with authors. The full texts will then be reviewed in a similar manner, applying the proposed preselected inclusion and exclusion criteria. Disagreements will be resolved by the two reviewers. If necessary, a third member of the research team will be consulted to reach consensus for a final decision on eligibility. The inclusion and exclusion criteria are summarised in table 1 , following the population, concept, and context categories for scoping reviews. We will include studies in any language. Table 1. Inclusion and exclusion criteria for selecting eligible studies Inclusion criteria Exclusion criteria Population Adults (18 years of age or older) undergoing elective, urgent or emergent upper GI cancer surgery (oesophagectomy, hepatectomy, pancreaticoduodenectomy, or gastrectomy). Upper GI cancer surgeries cannot be distinguished from the study population (not reported separately from larger cohort of patients). Non-cancer upper GI resection (such as live donor hepatectomy and cholecystectomy). Concept Any upper GI cancer surgery in adults (18 years of age or older). Studies reporting on intraoperative (in the operating room) processes of care (intervention or framework) applied by anaesthesiology or surgery. Studies where multiple interventions are reported, but a process of care must be reported separately. Studies where surgical technique is the only process studied (such as technique for lymph node dissection or margin resection). Context Articles published between January 2010 and March 2021. Original research articles (randomised controlled trials, quasi-randomised trials, observational and cross-sectional cohort studies). Systematic reviews and meta-analyses. Quantitative or qualitative studies. Opinion papers. Editorials. Conference abstracts. Case reports. Theses/dissertations. Narrative/qualitative reviews. Open in a new tab GI, gastrointestinal. Data abstraction A standardised data extraction form ( online supplemental data 3 ) will be developed by the research team. This form will facilitate the systematic collection and sorting of information from the included articles. Key data in the form will include: study characteristics (such as study location, year, design, aim, sample size), study population characteristics (such as age, sex or surgery type), and characteristics of the processes of care during surgery (ie, type of process, outcomes used to measure that process and the direction of that outcome). We will aim to summarise data across specific surgical procedures, where possible. The data extraction form will first be piloted by two independent reviewers. Differences in data abstraction will be resolved by a third member of the team. After the pilot, the results will be shared with the entire research team to determine whether the data extraction form effectively captures the information necessary to meet the study objectives and answer the research question. The form will be revised accordingly. Once the data extraction form is finalised, the extraction process for each study will be performed by a single reviewer and verified by a second reviewer. Data summary and reporting The results will be summarised quantitatively and qualitatively to describe the collected data. The data summary will be stratified by the surgical procedure (oesophagectomy, hepactectomy, pancreaticoduodenectomy and gastrectomy). Descriptive statistics (frequencies with dispersion measures) will be used to describe the number of studies reporting a process of care or measured outcome. The magnitude, direction of effect and a descriptive analysis for all recorded outcomes that are associated with a specific process of care in our patient population, will all be included. This avoids uncertainty or reporting bias. The narrative and qualitative thematic analysis of the intraoperative processes of care and their effects on oncological surgical care outcomes will also be reported. Data will be presented in tables, along with narrative synthesis of the findings. Consultation and dissemination of results We have, and will continue to, engaged relevant stakeholders in the anaesthesiology and cancer surgery community. Those include anaesthesiologists, surgeons, policymakers, departmental leaderships, and patients with GI cancer with lived experience of the surgical process. Those stakeholders will support data dissemination and knowledge translation. Specifically, the results will be disseminated via publication targeted at relevant stakeholders involved in the perioperative care of our patient population and via our clinical networks and those of our stakeholders and organisation partners. Furthermore, the results of this scoping review will be used to (1) conduct a Delphi-based expert consensus to determine which processes of care are most relevant for further study into delivering high quality of care delivery for patients undergoing upper GI cancer surgery and subsequently, (2) examine the use of those processes of care using population-based data. Through this, we aim to direct future research by identifying both high yield and potentially under-represented areas of the literature as it relates to GI oncological care. Patient and public involvement Patients and the public were not involved in the development of this protocol. We have planned for patient partners’ engagement in data interpretation and dissemination. Ethics and dissemination No ethics approval is required for this scoping review. Results will be disseminated through publication targeted at relevant stakeholders in anaesthesiology and cancer surgery. Supplementary Material Reviewer comments bmjopen-2022-068339.reviewer_comments.pdf (144.8KB, pdf) Author''s manuscript bmjopen-2022-068339.draft_revisions.pdf (1MB, pdf) Acknowledgments The authors would like to thank Henry Lam from the Sunnybrook Health Sciences Library for providing librarian services and consultation, in aiding the search for the scoping review. Footnotes Twitter: @lucksheee, @pperezde Contributors: LR, AH, JH, AE, VB and AJ were involved in conception of the project, literature review, developed the research questions and study methods and contributed to the drafting of the protocol. RS, JT, M-MK, CI and PP contributed meaningfully to the editing and critical review of this protocol and approved the final manuscript. Funding: This research will in part be funded by the Academic Health Sciences - Alternating Funding Plan. Competing interests: None declared. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were not involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Supplemental material: This content has been supplied by the author(s). It has not been vetted by BMJ Publishing Group Limited (BMJ) and may not have been peer-reviewed. Any opinions or recommendations discussed are solely those of the author(s) and are not endorsed by BMJ. BMJ disclaims all liability and responsibility arising from any reliance placed on the content. Where the content includes any translated material, BMJ does not warrant the accuracy and reliability of the translations (including but not limited to local regulations, clinical guidelines, terminology, drug names and drug dosages), and is not responsible for any error and/or omissions arising from translation and adaptation or otherwise. Ethics statements Patient consent for publication Not applicable. References 1. Bray F, Ferlay J, Soerjomataram I, et al. Global cancer Statistics 2018: GLOBOCAN es