aro C., Subira C., et al. Cognitive phenotypes 1 month after ICU discharge in mechanically ventilated patients: a prospective observational cohort study. Crit Care. 2020;24:618. doi: 10.1186/s13054-020-03334-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Navarra-Ventura G., Lopez-Aguilar J., Blanch L., Fernandez-Gonzalo S. Characterization and management of cognitive and emotional alterations in COVID-19 critically ill patients after ICU discharge. Med Intensiva (Engl Ed) 2022;46:112–113. doi: 10.1016/j.medine.2020.11.002. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',309,'2025-10-15 11:09:07','2025-10-15 11:09:07');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5326,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4100843','Emergency medicine clerkship directors: current workforce.','Wald DA, Khandelwal S, Manthey DE, Way DP, Ander DS, Thibodeau L.','2014','cdem cdem cdem inducted cdem cdem dpw enlist receptionist macy receptionist margo margo cdem aacem headway devoting headway affiliates cdem zun downey s15328015tlm1601_4 manthey kruus tripp amoroso heitz eyck fitch pangaro fincher bachicha s15328015tlm1503_12 pangaro mjm polan zun manthey manthey ander allianceforclinicaleducation margo gazewood jerpbak ephgrave margo 0b013e3181d2cdf1 allianceforclinicaleducation 2008aamcpanel pangaro gazewood margo jerpbak predoctoral','Abstract Introduction The emergency medicine clerkship director serves an important role in the education of medical students. The authors sought to update the demographic and academic profile of the emergency medicine clerkship director. Methods We developed and implemented a comprehensive questionnaire, and used it to survey all emergency medicine clerkship directors at United States allopathic medical schools accredited by the Liaison Committee on Medical Education. We analyzed and interpreted data using descriptive statistics. Results One hundred seven of 133 (80.4%) emergency medicine clerkship directors completed the survey. Clerkship Director’s mean age was 39.7 years (SD-7.2), they were more commonly male 68.2%, of Caucasian racial backgrounds and at the instructor or assistant professor (71.3%) level. The mean number of years of experience as clerkship director was 5.5 (SD-4.5). The mean amount of protected time for clerkship administration reported by respondents was 7.3 hours weekly (SD-5.1), with the majority (53.8%) reporting 6 or more hours of protected time per week. However, 32.7% of emergency medicine clerkship directors reported not having any protected time for clerkship administration. Most clerkship directors (91.6%) held additional teaching responsibilities beyond their clerkship and many were involved in educational research (49.5%). The majority (79.8%), reported being somewhat or very satisfied with their job as clerkship director. Conclusion Most clerkship directors were junior faculty at the instructor or assistant professor rank and were involved with a variety of educational endeavors beyond the clerkship. INTRODUCTION In the past three decades, emergency medicine (EM) as a distinct clinical specialty has undergone tremendous growth. Recent surveys report that close to 40% of United States (U.S.) medical schools offer a mandatory EM clerkship in the clinical years of medical school, with the majority being in the senior year. 1 – 3 Job responsibilities of the clerkship director (CD) typically involve clerkship administration, clinical and didactic teaching, and participation in scholarly activity. 4 In 2005, a study by Coates et al 5 provided the first insight into the demographics and characteristics of the EM CD. In that study, the authors reported that: 72% of the EM CDs were junior faculty (at the instructor or assistant professor level); EM CDs were only provided an average reduction of 2.7 hours per week from their clinical work to perform clerkship administration and teaching duties; and that most CDs (51%) received no reduction in clinical time for clerkship related duties. The lack of protected time afforded to EM CDs should be viewed in context of the national CD organizations that have recommended a clinical reduction of 0.25 FTE to perform clerkship administrative duties and up to 0.55 FTE for the additional time required for teaching and educational scholarship. 4 , 6 – 8 Since the publication of the Coates review (2005), a number of developments in the specialty of EM have occurred. In May 2007, the Clerkship Directors in Emergency Medicine (CDEM) was formed establishing a unified national voice for EM CDs and medical student educators to advance education, research and faculty development within the specialty. 9 In 2008, CDEM became the first academy within the Society for Academic Emergency Medicine (SAEM). In November, 2008, CDEM was inducted as a full voting member of the Alliance for Clinical Education (ACE), a multidisciplinary group formed to enhance the clinical education of medical students. 10 In addition, members of CDEM have worked closely with the Council of Emergency Medicine Residency Directors (CORD-EM) to develop the CDEM/Medical Student Educators Track at the CORD Annual Academic Assembly and with the SAEM Program Committee to increase the educational content at the SAEM Annual meeting. In light of all the recent changes to the emergency medicine profession, our objective for this study was to provide an updated demographic and academic profile of the EM CD including; general characteristics, participation in scholarly activities, perceived support from their home institution, and satisfaction with their job. METHODS Study Design and Population We conducted a survey of EM CDs at U.S. medical schools fully accredited by the Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME). A roster of medical schools was obtained from the Association of American Medical Colleges (AAMC) web site ( www.aamc.org ). EM CD’s names and contact information were obtained from the SAEM membership directory, individual medical school websites and through direct phone contact with the medical schools if the information was otherwise unavailable. The final roster included one representative identified as the EM CD at the primary clinical training site from each of the 133 targeted medical schools. The study met criteria for exemption from human subjects review and informed consent by the institutional review board at The Ohio State University College of Medicine. Survey Content and Administration Authors (DEM and SK) selected survey items and item formats based on the literature covering similar efforts to profile CDs. The initial draft of the survey instrument was primarily modeled off of one reported by Coates, et. al. who studied EM CDs specifically. All other authors, including one with formal training in survey development (DPW) reviewed survey items until a final survey draft was agreed upon by consensus opinion. Before implementation, the survey instrument was piloted by five EM CDs, not affiliated with the study. These individuals provided suggestions for improving clarity, readability, and comprehensiveness. Surveys were disseminated to the target population of U.S. medical school EM CDs using an electronic survey service (SurveyMonkey,™ Palo Alto, CA). An initial personalized email with a link to the electronic survey was sent to each EM CD in August, 2010. The email included an outline of the study and an assurance of confidentiality. Reminders and follow-up emails were sent to non-respondents monthly from September, 2010 – February, 2011. In April, 2011, an attempt was made to contact non-responders directly and a final survey was distributed. Over these 9 months, every medical school was directly contacted at least twice to enlist their participation in this study. Data Analysis Descriptive statistics were used to report general characteristics and demographics of the EM CD. Comparisons between medical schools which required students to take an EM clerkship and those which offered EM as an elective rotation were made when possible. All analyses were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics for Windows, Version 19. (IBM Corp. Released 2010. IBM SPSS Statistics for Windows, Version 19.0. Armonk, NY: IBM Corp). RESULTS One hundred seven (80.4%) of 133 EM CD’s completed the survey. Although most EM CDs (71.3%) were reported to be junior faculty, e.g. at the clinical instructor or assistant professor rank; over a quarter (27.8%) of EM CDs had achieved the rank of associate or full professor. The mean number of years that a faculty member had served as CD was 5.5 (SD-4.5). More than one third (35.2%) had been in their current role as CD for 6 or more years. Many EM CDs reported having held or were currently holding leadership positions in their department other than CD. These included: assistant/associate residency director (22.4%), assistant clerkship director (9.3%), clinical director/associate clinical director (9.3%), chairman/division chief (7.5%), director of undergraduate medical education (4.7%) and residency director (3.7%). The mean amount of protected time for clerkship administration reported by respondents was 7.3 hours weekly (SD-5.1), with the majority (53.8%) reporting 6 or more hours of protected time per week. However, 32.7% of EM CDs reported not having any protected time at all for clerkship administration. We asked respondents to report how much financial support they received from their departments, in dollars, for continuing medical education (CME). Most respondents (76.6%) said that they receive some level of financial support for CME, with the median amount reported as $2750 (semi-interquartile range= $1250). This means that 50% of the 82 respondents who said they received CME funding, reported receiving between $1500 and $4000 for CME support. Another form of financial support we asked about was support for professional development beyond CME through conference attendance. Many (45.8%) CDs report receiving this type of financial support from their departments. We asked CDs to tell us about the type of administrative or clerical support they receive for carrying out their clerkship duties. Responses varied widely with 15% (16 of 107) report having the support of a full-time coordinator, 24.3% (26 of 107) have a secretary/receptionist, while most (59.8%; 64 of 107) share a coordinator with other programs. Only 10 CDs (9.3%; 10 of 107) report that they have no clerical support. We also asked CDs to rate the level of general support they receive from their department using a Likert-type response set ranging from high (excellent) to low (poor). Forty two percent noted departmental support as excellent (19.6%; 21 of 107) or good (22.4%; 24 of 107). Almost a third (30.8%; 33 of 107) said that support was satisfactory, while a quarter (25.2%; 27 of 107) said that it was less than satisfactory (fair). Almost half (46.7%; 50 of 107) said that they had some formal preparation for their role as CD. More than one third of the faculty (35.5%; 38 of 107) reported receiving brief training from a senior faculty member; 11.2% (12 of 107) had extensive mentorship; 10.3% (11 of 107) received a written job description and another 10.3% (11 of 107) were given a handbook of clerkship guidelines. However, (53.3%; 57 of 107) of respondents said that they had no formal training. We also assessed how many CDs had formal training as educators. We found that more than one quarter of EM CDs (27.1%; 29 of 107) had completed the American College of Emergency Physicians (ACEP) Teaching Fellowship and 4.7% (5 of 107) had earned a Masters in Education degree. Additional faculty development programs completed by EM CDs included: the Harvard Macy Program (3.7%; 4 of 107), Medical Education Research Certificate/AAMC (2.8%; 3 of 107) and the Stanford Faculty Development Program (1.9%; 2 of 107). Additional information regarding general characteristics of the EM CD is presented in Table 1 . Table 1. General characteristics of the emergency medicine (EM) clerkship director. Characteristics/demographics Percentage (SD) Age 39.7 years (SD-7.2) Gender Male 68.2 Female 31.8 Ethnicity White/Caucasian 83.2 Asian/Pacific Islander 11.9 Hispanic/Latino 3.0 African American 2.0 Academic rank Instructor 2.0 Assistant professor 69.3 Associate professor 22.8 Professor 5.0 No academic appointment 1.0 Board certified/prepared in EM Yes 98.1 No 1.9 Years as clerkship director 5.4 (SD-4.5) <1 3.7 1–2 27.2 3–5 33.7 6–10 24.1 >10 11.1 Protected time for clerkship administration 7.3 (SD-5.1) < 5 hours 46.2 6–10 hours 33.8 >10 hours 20.0 Job satisfaction Very satisfied 35.6 Somewhat satisfied 45.2 Neither satisfied nor dissatisfied 2.9 Somewhat dissatisfied 7.7 Very dissatisfied 8.7 Career aspirations Assistant/associate dean 36.2 Clerkship director 21.6 Residency director 13.8 Chairman 11.2 Assistant/associate residency director 8.6 Vice chairman 5.2 Dean 2.6 Research director 0.9 Open in a new tab CDs also reported that they commonly engage in a wide range of teaching activities, with most (91.6%; 98 of 107) having teaching responsibilities beyond running the clerkship. Many of these activities involve pre-clinical medical students. CDs also engaged in formal academic scholarship with 79.4% (85 of 107) of CDs reporting having had peer reviewed publications and 72% (77 of 107) having published textbook chapters. Approximately one quarter (23.4%; 25 of 107) of CDs reported that they had previously applied for an educational grant, with 64% (16 of 25) of those applying having received grant support. Information regarding teaching outside of the clerkship and participation in other scholarly endeavors are reported in Table 2 . Table 2. Teaching outside of the clerkship and other scholarly endeavors of the emergency medicine (EM) clerkship director. Percentage (SD) Educational responsibilities beyond the clerkship within the medical school Yes 91.6 No 8.4 Specific educational responsibilities Simulation 45.8 Introduction to clinical medicine 37.4 Advanced EM elective 35.5 Procedural curriculum 26.2 Physical examination course 22.4 Preclinical curriculum 16.9 Basic science curriculum 15.9 Clinical assessment/problem solving course 15.0 Leadership role in addition to the clerkship Director of advanced EM elective 15.9 Director of simulation 14.0 Director of procedural curriculum 6.5 Director of introduction to clinical medicine 4.7 Director of clinical assessment/problem solving course 3.7 Director of physical examination course 3.7 Peer reviewed publications 4.7 (SD-5.9) None 20.6 1–5 53.3 >5 26.2 Peer reviewed educational topics 1.4 (SD-3.5) Textbook chapters 3.6 (SD-5.5) None 28.0 1–5 54.2 >5 17.8 Textbook educational topics 0.6 (SD-1.4) Involvement in educational research Yes 49.5 No 50.5 Involvement in non-educational research Yes 64.5 No 35.5 Open in a new tab SD , standard deviation When we looked at protected time, ratings of departmental support, level of clerical support and level of satisfaction with their job, we found that CDs whose medical school curriculum consist of “required” EM clerkships were slightly more likely to receive protected time for their CD position when compared to those whose schools only offered “elective” clerkships (75% (42 of 56) vs. 58.8% (30 of 51)). The associated Chi-Square test for this comparison was not considered statistically significant ( X 2 = 3.173; df=1; P =.099). With regard to perceived level of support from their departments, both groups (required v. elective) were virtually the same in their ratings, with CDs with required clerkships having a mean rating of 3.357 (SD of 1.09) on this 5 Likert-Type scale and CDs with elective clerkships having a mean rating of 3.388 (SD of 1.08). CDs from institutions with required clerkships received slightly more clerical support than their elective institution counter-parts. More CDs of required clerkships had full-time clerkship coordinators (12 of 56, 21.4%) vs. (4 of 51, 7.8%) and fewer of them had no support at all (3 of 56, 5.4%) vs. (7 of 51, 13.7%). The primary clerical support model is a shared duty or half-time coordinator. This was true of both required clerkships (33 of 56, 58.9%) and those with elective clerkships (31 of 51, 39.2%). Support in the form of a secretary or receptionist was reported by 13 of 56 (23.2%) institutions with required clerkships and 13 of 51 (25.5%) institutions with elective clerkships. Finally, ratings of job satisfaction were compared across the two institution types. Those CDs who have required clerkships rated their level of satisfaction with their job slightly higher than those who have electives, however this difference was not statistically significant. DISCUSSION In 2009, Margo et al 11 compared CD characteristics using data available from surveys published in 7 medical specialties. This study used the data that was previously reported by Coates in 2005. 5 At the time of the Margo study, EM CDs were younger, more likely to be junior faculty at the clinical instructor or assistant professor rank and had less protected time afforded to support their role as CD when compared to their counterparts in other specialties. 11 , 12 Little has changed over the 6 years since the Coates study. The mean age of the EM CD is roughly the same; 38.9 years (SD-7.0) v. 39.7 years (SD-7.2). This compares to the mean age of CDs in other core specialties which is; mean 46.7 years (mean range 45–47.7 years). Regarding gender, 68.2% of EM CD’s are male which is similar to the other core specialties (mean 62% males, range 50–75%). 11 When looking at the distribution of academic rank of the EM CD, in 2005, 72.1% of EM CDs were at the instructor or assistant professor level. Our more recent data shows that 71.3% of EM CD’s are currently at this level. When broken down by specific academic rank, we observed that more EM CDs are at the assistant professor level than in 2005; 69.3% v. 61.3%. When we look at CDs at the senior faculty level (associate professor or professor), there is a small but positive trend. Currently, more than one quarter (27.8%) of EM CDs are at the associate professor or professor rank compared to 21.6% in 2005. These comparisons alone may not fully reflect the changing faculty rank of EM CDs as institutional promotion and tenure committees vary in requirements across medical schools. Protected time for clerkship administration, teaching and participation in academic scholarship are necessary for CD success in fulfilling their role. A recent CDEM – Association of Academic Chairs of Emergency Medicine (AACEM) combined Taskforce publication outlines the expectations of the EM CD. 8 This document mirrors many of the same expectations set forth by other national CD organizations. 4 , 6 , 7 Despite the recognized importance of the role of the CD, the support afforded to the CD is variable across disciplines. 11 , 12 Regarding protected time afforded for clerkship administration, EM CDs have made some headway in recent years. Of the faculty reporting that they receive protected time for clerkship administration, the majority (53.8%) report receiving 6 or more hours of protected time weekly. However, less than one third (32.7%) report that they do not receive protected time for their role as CD as compared to 2005 when the majority of CD’s (51.4%) reported that they had zero release time to perform clerkship administration. 5 Despite this positive trend noted in the amount of protected time allotted, there appears to be a discrepancy between EM CDs and their counterparts in other specialties. 11 However, a direct comparison between EM and other clinical specialties is difficult to perform for a number of reasons. CDs in the other core specialties report their clinical workload as a combination of weekly outpatient clinic sessions and inpatient responsibilities. 13 In addition, there is significant variability regarding inpatient clinical responsibilities across specialties further confounding a direct comparison. With regards to time committed to the clerkship, the other core specialty CDs report devoting an average 33% (range 30 – 48%) of their professional time towards the clerkship. 13 Translating this to a typical 40 hour work week would mean that CDs devote approximately 13 hours per week to clerkship administration. Meanwhile, the average protected time for the EM CD is only 7.3 hours per week or 18.5% of time based on a 40 hour work week. This means that EM CDs are only being supported for about half of the time that CDs from other disciplines receive to run their clerkships and falls far short of the expectations outlined by various national organizations. 4 , 6 – 8 Rapid turnover in the CD position was reported as a common problem in 2005 by Coates, when approximately 45% of EM CDs reported being in their position for 2 years or less. More recently it appears that some headway has been made in addressing turnover, with just a little more than one third (36.5%) of EM CDs report being in their current position for 1–2 years. In addition, almost one third (32.0%) of CDs have been in their current position as CD for 6 or more years as compared to 22.4% in 2005. Currently, the mean number of years a CD has been in their current position is 5.4 years. In comparison, as reported in a multispecialty review published in 2010, the mean number of years as CD for all of the core clinical specialties was 6.8 years (range 5.5 to 7.5 years). 12 Administrative support for the clerkship is important as it has been reported to have a positive correlation with the academic productivity of the CD. 11 We found administrative support for the clerkship, to be quite variable, with more than half (59.8%) of EM CDs reporting a shared/half time coordinator and only 15% reporting a full time clerkship coordinator. This is far less administrative support than reported for other core clinical specialties. 14 , 15 Overall, the role of CD is viewed positively by the faculty who perform these duties. More than two thirds of CDs (70%) across multiple specialties have reported that the role has had a positive effect on their academic achievement (core clerkship directors). In addition, more than three quarters of these same CDs (90%) reported that being a CD enhanced their satisfaction with their professional work. As for EM CDs, our data support that the vast majority (80.8%) are satisfied in their current job. LIMITATIONS Our study fell short of profiling all EM CDs. We did not attempt to survey CDs from osteopathic medical schools nor did we attempt to survey CDs from community hospitals and secondary or regional affiliates of LCME accredited medical schools. We did not address many confounders such as year of training, length of rotation, and volume of students. CONCLUSION The majority of EM CD’s are still junior faculty at the clinical instructor or assistant professor rank. We found that CDs had 7.3 hours of protected time per week and that this has generally improved since 2005. However, this still falls below expectations set forth by CDEM EM CDs are engaged in a wide variety of teaching activities in addition to the clerkship, and many perform educational research and scholarship. The majority of EM CDs are satisfied in their current position. Footnotes Full text available through open access at http://escholarship.org/uc/uciem_westjem Conflicts of Interest : By the West JEM article submission agreement, all authors are required to disclose all affiliations, funding sources and financial or management relationships that could be perceived as potential sources of bias. The authors disclosed none. REFERENCES 1. Zun LS, Downey L. Is a third year clerkship in emergency medicine correlated with a career choice in emergency medicine? Teach Learn Med. 2004;16:14–17. doi: 10.1207/s15328015tlm1601_4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Wald DA, Manthey DE, Kruus L, Tripp M, Barrett J, Amoroso B. The state of the clerkship: a survey of emergency medicine clerkship directors. Acad Emerg Med. 2007;14:629–634. doi: 10.1197/j.aem.2007.02.035. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Heitz C, Eyck RT, Smith M, Fitch M. Simulation in medical student education: survey of clerkship directors in emergency medicine. West J Emerg Med. 2011;12:455–460. doi: 10.5811/westjem.2011.3.2107. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Pangaro L, Fincher RM, Bachicha J, et al. Expectations of and for clerkship directors: A collaborative statement from the Alliance for Clinical Education. Teach Learn Med. 2003;15:217–222. doi: 10.1207/S15328015TLM1503_12. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Coates WC, Gill AM, Jordan R. Emergency medicine clerkship directors: Defining the characteristics of the workforce. Ann Emerg Med. 2005;45:262–268. doi: 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2004.09.023. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Pangaro LN. Expectations of and for the medicine clerkship director. Am J Med. 1998;105:363–365. doi: 10.1016/s0002-9343(98)00335-0. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kuhn TW, Cohen MJM, Polan HJ, et al. Standards for Psychiatry Clerkship Directors. Acad Psych. 2002;26:31–37. doi: 10.1176/appi.ap.26.1.31. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Rogers RL, Wald DA, Lin M, Zun LS, Christopher T, Manthey DE. Expectations of an emergency medicine clerkship director. Acad Emerg Med. 2011;18:513–8. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2011.01063.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Wald DA, Manthey DE, Lin M, Ander DS, Fisher J. Clerkship directors in emergency medicine: statement of purpose. Acad Emerg Med. 2008;15:856–859. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2008.00207.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Alliance for Clinical Education. [Accessed May 21st 2013]. available at; http://www.allianceforclinicaleducation.org/ 11. Margo K, Gazewood J, Jerpbak C, et al. Clerkship director’s characteristics, scholarship and support: A summary of published surveys from seven medical specialties. Teach Learn Med. 2009;21:94–99. doi: 10.1080/10401330902791065. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Ephgrave K, Margo K, White C, et al. Core clerkship directors: Their current resources and the rewards of the role. Acad Med. 2010;85:710–715. doi: 10.1097/ACM.0b013e3181d2cdf1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Alliance for Clinical Education annual workshop in clinical education. 2008. [Last accessed May 21st 2013]. Available at; http://www.allianceforclinicaleducation.org/2008AAMCPanel.pdf . 14. Durning SJ, Papp KK, Pangaro LN, et al. Expectations of and for the internal medicine clerkship directors: How are we doing? Teach Learn Med. 2007;19:65–69. doi: 10.1080/10401330709336626. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Gazewood J, Margo K, Jerpbak C, et al. Predoctoral directors: Who are they and what do they do in these trying times? Fam Med. 2007;39:171–177. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',9,'2025-10-15 11:09:11','2025-10-15 11:09:11');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5327,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10437019','Effects of Cerebrovascular and Lewy Body Pathology on Parkinsonian Signs in Community-Dwelling Older Adults.','Agrawal S, Leurgans SE, Nag S, Oveisgharan S, Barnes LL, Bennett DA, Buchman AS, Schneider JA.','2023','bradykinesia bradykinesia nigrostriatal pseudoparkinsonism enrolls priests nuns chicagoland efigure1 c920 bradykinesia into1 slabs calcarine zymed wako bisecting c920 bradykinesia bradykinesia bradykinesia additively c920 c920 bradykinesia etables c920 ganglionic ganglionic copathologies copathologies aggravation s129 microhemorrhage r01ag022018 r01ag056352 r01ag047976 r01ag059732 r01ag017917 p30ag010161 p30ag072975 r01ag015819 r01ag022018 glv153 schupf mayeux schupf pringsheim frolkis steeves tdl e1821 e1830 vizcarra espay deconstructing s161 s189 epsilon4 aws234 manas aragones s40520 zagten lodder kessels ihara tomimoto ishizu s00702 oveisgharan arfanakis evia greffard verny fujishiro delledonne rosano brach longstreth reitz trenkwalder roesler eckardstein brickman archneurol bohnen aizenstein rosano e223 e229 oveisgharan e1976 e1984 manolio chs atv poppas moulding oxid longev kaimal dempsey vemuganti','Abstract Background and Objectives The roles of Lewy body (LB) and separately of cerebrovascular disease (CVD) pathologies in the severity of parkinsonian signs are well recognized in old age. We investigated whether the 2 pathologies act synergistically to further potentiate the severity of parkinsonism beyond their separate effects. Methods We used postmortem data of decedents from 3 longitudinal community-based studies of aging who underwent annual clinical evaluation to assess parkinsonian signs using 26 items of the motor portion of a modified Unified Parkinson Disease Rating Scale. A summary score was developed from each item score to construct a global parkinsonian score, with a higher score indicating more severe parkinsonism. A detailed neuropathologic evaluation was performed to identify LB, Alzheimer disease pathology, nigral neuronal loss, atherosclerosis, macroscopic infarcts, and other CVD pathologies (arteriolosclerosis, cerebral amyloid angiopathy, and microscopic infarcts). A series of regression models with terms for LB, CVD pathology, and the interaction of LB with CVD pathologies was fit for global parkinsonism proximate to death and for individual parkinsonian signs scores including, parkinsonian gait, rigidity, tremor, and bradykinesia. Results In 1,753 participants (mean age at death = 89 years; 68% women), LB was observed in 26% of participants, and CVD pathologies were present in more than two-thirds of participants. LB and 3 CVD pathologies (atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, and macroscopic infarcts) were each independently associated with the severity of global parkinsonism proximate to death (LB: β = 0.318, SE = 0.08, p < 0.001; atherosclerosis: β = 0.373, SE = 0.079, p < 0.001; arteriolosclerosis: β = 0.253, SE = 0.078, p = 0.001; macroscopic infarcts: β = 0.333, SE = 0.077, p < 0.001). A linear regression model adjusted for demographics, CVD, and neurodegenerative pathologies showed interaction between LB and macroscopic infarcts (β = 0.463, SE = 0.168, p = 0.006), with LBs being associated with worse global parkinsonism when macroinfarcts are present. Similar interactions were found for atherosclerosis and LBs (β = 0.371, SE = 0.173, p = 0.032) and for parkinsonian gait as the outcome (macroscopic infarcts: β = 0.662, SE = 0.239, p = 0.005; atherosclerosis: β = 0.509, SE = 0.246, p = 0.038). Findings were not affected when the 66 participants with a clinical diagnosis of Parkinson disease were excluded. By contrast, there were no interactions between LB and other CVD pathologies or between atherosclerosis and macroscopic infarcts for global parkinsonism proximate to death. Discussion These findings suggest that atherosclerosis and macroscopic infarcts interact with LB pathology to increase the severity of parkinsonism beyond their additive effects in older persons. Introduction Mild parkinsonism is a common and heterogeneous group of disorders found in older adults that can lead to negative health outcomes, including falls, disability, and mortality. 1 , 2 Approximately half of adults manifest mild parkinsonian signs including parkinsonian gait, rigidity, tremor, and bradykinesia by 85 years of age even without showing neurologic diseases. 3 - 6 The cause of most cases of parkinsonism in older adults is often unknown, especially where there are no underlying diseases or medication-related factors. Clinical Parkinson disease (PD) is the most common cause of parkinsonism, but the prevalence of PD in the community is less than 5%, 7 which is far less than the frequency of parkinsonism and parkinsonian signs in the community-dwelling older adults. 8 Movement disorder experts in the parkinsonism field have also debated the diagnosis and prevalence of vascular parkinsonism, with some suggesting that it is rare and caused by an ischemic or hemorrhagic event involving the substantia nigra or nigrostriatal pathway, while others believing that it is caused by other neurodegenerative diseases, including PD or progressive supranuclear palsy, or the symptoms to be pseudoparkinsonism symptoms. 9 Given that parkinsonism is a heterogeneous disorder of motor dysfunction in older adults, the extent to which PD and cerebrovascular disease pathology is linked to parkinsonism and parkinsonian signs remains controversial. Multiple postmortem and neuroimaging studies have shown that a wide range of cerebrovascular diseases (CVDs), specifically vessel pathology, vascular tissue injuries, and PD pathology, are associated with greater severity of parkinsonism in the aging brain 8 , 10 , 11 and are related with a faster rate of parkinsonism. 8 The relationship between vascular and Lewy body (LB) pathology remains complex, and it remains unclear whether cerebrovascular disease pathologies such as atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, cerebral amyloid angiopathy, and associated tissue injuries (e.g., macroscopic infarcts and microscopic infarcts) and LB pathology interact in individuals to potentiate the severity of parkinsonism in older persons beyond their additive effects. Elucidating interactions of these pathologies may improve our understanding of mechanisms underlying these pathologies in developing parkinsonism in old age. This study examined whether CVD and LB pathologies act synergistically to potentiate the severity of parkinsonism and parkinsonian signs. We first assessed the association of postmortem indices of LB and CVD pathologies with the severity of global parkinsonism. Next, we examined the interactions between CVD pathology and LB to determine whether these pathologies contribute independently or synergistically to the severity of global parkinsonism and to individual parkinsonian signs in the aging brain. Methods Study Participants Participants were recruited for 1 of 3 ongoing epidemiologic longitudinal clinical-pathologic cohort studies of aging: the Religious Orders Study (ROS), the Rush Memory and Aging Project (MAP), or the Minority Aging Research Study (MARS). 12 , 13 Each study was approved by the Rush University Medical Center institutional review board. The ROS enrolls older Catholic priests, nuns, and lay brothers from multiple sites across the United States, the MAP recruits older persons from across the Chicagoland and northeastern Illinois area, and the MARS recruits older individuals who identify themselves as African American. Eligibility in all 3 studies requires enrollment at age 65 years and older without known dementia and consent to undergo annual clinical evaluation. Written informed consent was obtained from both ROS and MAP study participants for brain donation at study enrollment, while brain donation was optional for the MARS study. During the analyses, 1,753 participants who had complete data on parkinsonian evaluation and neuropathology were included in this study. A flowchart representing the analytic sample is provided in the supplementary file (eFigure1, links.lww.com/WNL/C920 ). This study is reported following the Strengthening in Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology checklist. Clinical Evaluation for Parkinsonian Signs Four parkinsonian sign scores (parkinsonian gait, bradykinesia, rigidity, and tremor) were assessed annually by trained nurses using 26 items of the motor portion of a modified Unified Parkinson Disease Rating Scale. 10 As previously published, 14 trained nurse clinicians showed high interrater reliability with a neurologist with expertise in movement disorders and short-term stability with themselves both for the 4 parkinsonian sign scores and the global parkinsonian score. A score for each parkinsonian sign was calculated as the sum of the scores for the relevant items; the sum was then scaled so that the maximum score was 100. The global parkinsonism score was then calculated as the average of scores for the 4 parkinsonian signs, with a higher score indicating more severe parkinsonism. Postmortem Neuropathologic Evaluation A systematic and uniform neuropathologic evaluation was performed by senior neuropathologists and trained staff, blinded to demographic or clinical information. The average postmortem interval was 9.55 hours (median 6.9 hours; interquartile range [IQR] 2–115.7 hours). In brief, autopsied brains were removed by trained staff and cut into the hemispheres. One hemisphere was frozen for extraction of multi-omics data and the other was fixed in 4% paraformaldehyde and cut into1 cm coronal slabs. 15 , 16 Tissue from 11 standard brain regions including middle temporal, midfrontal, entorhinal with the amygdala, mid hippocampus, calcarine, inferior parietal, and anterior cingulate cortices, basal ganglia and anterior thalamus, midbrain, and cerebellum were taken for diagnostic evaluation. To confirm the age of each gross infarct (acute, subacute, and chronic), additional tissue was taken. Lewy Body Pathology Immunohistochemistry with alpha-synuclein (Zymed LB 509; 1:50; pSyn, 1:20 000; Wako Chemicals) was used to detect LB from the substantia nigra, amygdala, midfrontal, entorhinal, cingulate, middle temporal, and inferior parietal cortices. 17 LB pathology was documented as present if LBs were present in any assessed brain regions and treated as present or absent in these analyses. Atherosclerosis Atherosclerosis assessment was performed by visual inspection at the Circle of Willis and included evaluation of the basilar, posterior cerebral, middle cerebral, and anterior cerebral arteries and their proximal branches. The severity was determined by visual examination of the number of plaques, the extent of involvement of each artery by plaques, and the degree of vascular occlusion on bisecting vessels. 18 A dichotomous variable (none-to-mild vs moderate-to-severe) was used; where none-to-mild includes ≤7 plaques with or without <50% vessel occlusion while moderate-to-severe includes those with a frequent number of plaques (>7), involvement of >50% of vessel length, or >50% vessel occlusion. 18 Arteriolosclerosis Arteriolosclerosis was evaluated from the anterior basal ganglia. A semiquantitative grading system from 0 (none) to 6 (severe) was used to assess the severity of arteriolosclerosis. 18 A dichotomous variable (none-to-mild vs moderate-to-severe) was used for both descriptive and analytical analyses. 18 Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA was assessed from meningeal and parenchymal vessels from 4 neocortical region sections based on the burden of Aβ deposition in the vessels. 19 For analyses, CAA was scaled as none-to-mild vs moderate-severe. Macroscopic Infarcts Macroscopic infarcts were identified visually on gross examination, collected, and evaluated for age under the microscope. For this study, only chronic macroscopic infarcts were included. A dichotomous variable (present vs absent) for chronic macroscopic infarcts was considered in the analyses. 20 Macroscopic infarct variables were further defined into cortical or subcortical locations. Subcortical infarcts included both subcortical white matter and deep hemispheric gray matter structures. Microscopic Infarcts Chronic infarcts not visible to the naked eye and identified under the microscope were termed microscopic infarcts. Microscopic infarcts were assessed in abovementioned brain regions. Finally, a dichotomous variable (present vs absent) for chronic microscopic infarcts was used for analyses. 20 Other Brain Pathologies Details of the ex vivo WMH burden and other neurodegenerative pathology assessment are provided in the eMethods ( links.lww.com/WNL/C920 ). Other Covariates Methods for the assessment of other covariates (demographics, frailty, gait speed, clinical diagnosis of PD, history of stroke, and vascular risk factors) are provided in the eMethods. Statistical Analyses The global parkinsonian sign score and the parkinsonian gait score had positively skewed distributions and were subjected to a square root transformation, and the transformed scores were used in all analyses. Rigidity, bradykinesia, and tremor were relatively infrequent and were treated as present or absent in descriptive and analytical analyses. Initial bivariate associations of LB with CVD pathology were examined with logistic regression tests. We used a linear regression model without an interaction term to examine the main effects of LB, arteriolosclerosis, CAA, atherosclerosis, and macroscopic and microscopic infarcts with global parkinsonism proximate to death, adjusted for age at death, sex, and education. Next, to examine the interaction between macroscopic infarcts and LB pathology, we fitted a linear regression model with global parkinsonism as the outcome and terms for LB and macroscopic infarcts, an interaction term between LB and macroscopic infarcts, and terms to adjust for demographics (age at death, sex, and education). Next, we repeated the analyses after adjusting neurodegenerative pathologies (Alzheimer disease pathology and nigral neuronal loss) and other CVD pathologies. Similar analyses were repeated but replaced the macroscopic infarct term with atherosclerosis and replaced an interaction term between LB and macroscopic infarcts with an interaction term between atherosclerosis and LB. To examine the interaction of LB with the remaining 3 CVD pathologies (arteriolosclerosis, CAA, and microscopic infarcts), we repeated 3 separate analyses similar to the preceding analyses. For those CVD pathologies showing significant interaction with LB for the severity of parkinsonism, secondary regression analyses were applied to examine the interactions of LB with the CVD pathology for the 4 individual parkinsonian signs. Linear regression analyses were used for parkinsonian gait scores and logistic regression analyses for 3 outcomes: bradykinesia, rigidity, and tremor. Sensitivity analyses were performed by additional adjustment for potential confounders to assess the robustness of significant outcomes. In the first set, we repeated the fully adjusted models excluding the cases with clinical diagnosis of Parkinson disease (PD). In the second set, we repeated the models after adjusting vascular risk factors. All analyses were programmed in SAS/STAT, version 9.4 [SAS Institute Inc., Cary, NC]. Statistical significance was determined at nominal α level of 0.05. Data Availability All data included in this study are available by request through the Rush Alzheimer Disease Center Research Resource Sharing Hub radc.rush.edu/ . Results There were 1,753 participants included in this study, with a mean age at death of 89 years (SD = 6.7), and 68% were female participants. The mean (SD) square root–transformed global parkinsonian score was 3.77 (1.48) and was associated with age (estimate = 0.032; SE = 0.005, p < 0.001), but not with sex or education. Detailed information on demographic characteristics and individual parkinsonian signs is listed in Table 1 . Table 1. Demographic, Clinical, and Neuropathologic Characteristics Open in a new tab LBs were identified in approximately one-fourth (N = 449) of the brains in these older individuals. Almost 80% (N = 1,401) showed evidence of 1 or more indices of CVD pathology. Of them, 572 (33%) had moderate-to-severe atherosclerosis, 555 (32%) had moderate-to-severe arteriolosclerosis, 616 (35%) had moderate-to-severe CAA, 624 (36%) had macroscopic infarcts, and 535 (30%) had microscopic infarcts ( Table 1 ). CVD pathologies tend to occur with each other. Logistic regression analyses, after adjusting for age and sex, revealed that moderate-to-severe atherosclerosis increased the odds of having macroscopic infarcts (OR = 2.61, 95% CI = 2.12–3.22, p < 0.001) by more than 2.5-fold and was associated with 41% higher odds of microscopic infarcts (OR = 1.41, 95% CI = 1.44–1.75, p = 0.001). Those with moderate-to-severe arteriolosclerosis were more than twice as likely to have macroscopic infarcts (OR = 2.10, 95% CI = 1.70–2.59, p < 0.001) and were associated with 50% higher odds of microscopic infarcts (OR = 1.50, 95% CI = 1.20–1.86, p < 0.001). Those with CAA were not associated with having macroscopic infarcts or microscopic infarcts (both p values > 0.676). Logistic regression revealed that the presence of LB was not associated with any of the CVD pathologies (all 5 p values > 0.09). Main Effects of LB and CVD Pathology With Global Parkinsonism Proximate to Death Consistent with our previous study, 10 , 11 LB (estimate, 0.318; SE, 0.080; p < 0.001), atherosclerosis (estimate, 0.373; SE, 0.079; p < 0.001), arteriolosclerosis (estimate, 0.253; SE, 0.078; p = 0.001), and macroscopic infarcts (estimate, 0.333; SE, 0.077; p < 0.001) were associated with the severity of global parkinsonism ( Table 2 ). Microscopic infarcts and CAA were not related to global parkinsonism ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Association of LB and CVD Pathology With Parkinsonism Proximate to Death Open in a new tab Furthermore, we separately analyzed individual parkinsonian signs including parkinsonian gait, rigidity, tremor, and bradykinesia. Results showed that the presence of LB pathology was mainly associated with rigidity and tremor while CVD pathologies such as arteriolosclerosis, atherosclerosis, and macroscopic infarcts were mostly related with the severity of parkinsonian gait. There were no associations of CAA and microscopic infarcts with any parkinsonian sign scores ( Table 2 ). Interaction Between LB and CVD Pathology for the Severity of Global Parkinsonism Proximate to Death To examine whether macroscopic infarcts modified the association between LB and global parkinsonism, linear regression models included an interaction term between macroscopic infarcts and the presence of LB ( Table 3 , model 1A-1B). The interaction term was positive for more severe parkinsonism (estimate, 0.434; SE, 0.167; p = 0.009), suggesting that LB was associated with a 0.434-unit higher estimate for parkinsonism in people with macroscopic infarcts than for people without macroscopic infarcts. The result was consistent and robust after adjusting for other CVD pathologies and neurodegenerative pathologies. Table 3. Interaction Between CVD and LB Pathology for Parkinsonism Proximate to Death Open in a new tab Next, we included an interaction between atherosclerosis and LB in the model of global parkinsonism ( Table 3 , model 2A-2B). LB and atherosclerosis interacted in models of global parkinsonism (estimate, 0.336; SE, 0.172; p = 0.051) but did not reach the threshold for statistical significance after adjusting for demographics. The interaction remained after adjusting for CVD and neurodegenerative pathologies. The positive interaction term implies that the association between LB and parkinsonism is stronger in persons with moderate-to-severe atherosclerosis. In sensitivity analyses, we examined whether our significant interaction findings were driven by those individuals who had clinical PD. Exclusion of 66 participants with clinical PD did not change the interaction result of macroscopic infarcts and LB nor the interaction result of atherosclerosis and LB with more severe parkinsonism ( Table 3 ). To further investigate, instead of looking at 1 vascular pathology at a time, as summarized in Table 3 , we considered both vascular pathologies together and found that the effect of LB on parkinsonism is roughly double when both vascular pathologies are present (estimate, 1.258, SE, 0.185, p < 0.001) than when either vascular pathology alone is present (with atherosclerosis = estimate, 0.665, SE, 0.195, p < 0.001 and with macroscopic infarcts = estimate, 0.730, SE, 0.176, p < 0.001, Figure 1 ). We also ran 2 sets of secondary analyses. In the first set of analyses, we added both terms for the interaction of LBs and atherosclerosis and LBs and macroscopic infarcts in a fully adjusted model. The interaction term between LBs and macroscopic infarcts remained significant (estimate, 0.412; SE, 0.172; p = 0.017). However, the interaction term between atherosclerosis and LBs had no longer significance, with an estimated effect approximately 60% of the interaction with LBs and macroscopic infarcts (estimate, 0.248; SE, 0.177; p = 0.160), suggesting that the interaction effect is stronger between macroscopic infarcts and LBs than between atherosclerosis and LBs. In the second set of analyses, we added additional terms for the interaction of the 2 CVD pathologies with each other and the 3-way interaction with LBs: neither interaction is present ( p = 0.767 and 0.855, respectively). That suggests the 2 vascular pathologies act additively, with the association of macroscopic infarcts with parkinsonism being stronger when LBs are present (eFigure 2, links.lww.com/WNL/C920 ). By contrast, arteriolosclerosis, CAA, and microscopic infarcts did not modify the association between LB and global parkinsonism (eTable 1, links.lww.com/WNL/C920 ). Figure 1. Relation of LB and Large Vessel Pathologies to Parkinsonism Before Death. Open in a new tab These figures illustrate the synergy of LB findings with each of these vascular pathologies. The effect associated with LBs is very different, depending on which of these 2 vascular pathologies are seen. The effect associated with Lewy bodies is small when neither of the large vascular pathologies (atherosclerosis and macroscopic infarcts) are present (A), moderate when exactly one is present (B and C), and larger when both of these large vascular pathologies are present (D). Interaction Between LB and Atherosclerosis/Macroscopic Infarcts for Individual Parkinsonian Signs Because the global parkinsonian score was an average of scores for the 4 parkinsonian signs, we further examined whether macroscopic infarcts and atherosclerosis interacted with LB for individual parkinsonian signs ( Table 4 ). We found that macroscopic infarcts (estimate, 0.641; SE, 0.236; p = 0.006) and atherosclerosis (estimate, 0.48; SE, 0.24; p = 0.048) both had positive interactions with LB for more severe parkinsonian gait, after controlling for demographics. The interaction remained after controlling for other CVD and neurodegenerative pathologies. In sensitivity analyses, interaction results remained unchanged after excluding participants with clinical PD. Table 4. Interaction Between CVD and LB Pathology for the Severity of Parkinsonian Gait Proximate to Death Open in a new tab Because frailty and gait speed may account for the gait impairment in older adults, 21 we added 2 secondary analyses, one that controlled with physical frailty and another that controlled with gait speed. The interactions for more severe parkinsonian gait remained after controlling for physical frailty (atherosclerosis x LB: estimate, 0.493; SE, 0.223; p = 0.027; macroscopic infarcts x LB: estimate, 0.489; SE, 0.217; p = 0.024) and gait speed (atherosclerosis x LB: estimate, 0.505; SE, 0.259; p = 0.051; macroscopic infarcts x LB: estimate, 0.478; SE, 0.228; p = 0.038). Finally, there were no interactions between CVD pathology (macroscopic infarcts and atherosclerosis) and LB for bradykinesia, rigidity, or tremor (all 6 p s > 0.149; data not shown). Additional Analyses First, to test for the possibility that sex differences may modify the interaction between atherosclerosis and LB to further potentiate the severity of global parkinsonism, we used linear regression models with additional term for sex x LB x atherosclerosis. In the analysis, there were no interaction of sex with atherosclerosis and LB ( p = 0.567, data not shown). Furthermore, there were no interactions of sex with atherosclerosis and LB for parkinsonian gait ( p = 0.601, data not shown). Second, because the history of stroke can influence the association of macroscopic infarcts with gait impairment, 22 we performed additional analyses to examine whether the interaction between LB and macroscopic infarcts with gait impairment modified by history of stroke. The interaction of LB x macroscopic infarcts x history of stroke was not significant ( p = 0.853, data not shown) indicating that synergistic interaction between LB and macroscopic infarcts for more severe parkinsonian gait did not vary by history of stroke. Third, we conducted additional analyses to control for vascular risk factors such as hypertension, smoking, and diabetes with potential to affect independent association of LB and CVD pathologies with global parkinsonism. Consistent with our previous study, 10 the association of LB and CVD pathologies with global parkinsonism and individual parkinsonian domains were remained unchanged (data not shown). Furthermore, the interaction results between CVD pathologies (atherosclerosis and macroscopic infarcts) and LB to further potentiate global parkinsonism were also unchanged (data not shown). Fourth, we also considered infarct location and repeated the analyses by replacing macroscopic infarcts with cortical macroscopic infarcts and separately with subcortical macroscopic infarcts. Analyses showed that subcortical macroscopic infarcts had significant interactions with LB for more severe global parkinsonism (estimate, 0.401; SE, 0.181; p = 0.026) and for parkinsonian gait (estimate, 0.547; SE, 0.257; p = 0.033) after controlling for demographics and age-related neurodegenerative and vascular pathologies. There was no interaction found between cortical macroscopic infarcts and LB for global parkinsonism (eTables 2 and 3, links.lww.com/WNL/C920 ). Furthermore, because prior study 23 has indicated that macroscopic infarcts in the basal ganglia may strongly affect the parkinsonian gait than those in other brain areas, we ran secondary analyses by replacing the term subcortical macroscopic infarcts with basal ganglionic macroscopic infarcts. We also changed interaction term LB x subcortical macroscopic infarcts with interaction term LB x basal ganglionic macroscopic infarcts for parkinsonian gait as the outcome. There were no interactions between LB and basal ganglia infarcts ( p = 0.712, data not shown). Finally, to compare our results with neuroimaging data, we obtained available ex vivo white matter hyperintensity (WMH) data (n = 598). Of 598, 17% had none/mild WMH burden, 35% had moderate WMH burden, and 48% had severe WMH burden. In linear regression models adjusted for demographics, postmortem interval, and neurodegenerative and vascular pathologies, WMH burden was associated with greater parkinsonism (estimate, 0.291; SE, 0.075; p < 0.001) similar to our previous study. 24 Secondary analyses examining whether LB and WMH burden showed synergistic interactions revealed that there was no interaction between LB and WMH burden for global parkinsonism ( p = 0.526, data not shown). This result was similar with the lack of interaction between arteriolosclerosis and LB and was in line with the association of brain arteriolosclerosis, not atherosclerosis, with WMH. 25 Furthermore, this finding indicates that LB and brain small vessel diseases may not have synergistic effect on parkinsonism in contrast to LB and intracranial large vessel pathology. Discussion In this study of 1,753 community-dwelling older persons, we explored a potential interaction between CVD and LB pathology to understand whether the common copathologies of LB and CVD affects the severity of parkinsonism proximate to death. We found that both macroscopic infarcts, particularly subcortical macroscopic infarcts, and atherosclerosis have separate and synergistic effects with LB to potentiate the clinical expression of parkinsonism in older persons, and more specifically to potentiate more severe parkinsonian gait. These interactions remained significant in a model including postmortem indices of other neurodegenerative and cerebrovascular disease brain pathologies. Overall, these findings provide insight into the impact of the common presence of copathologies in aging, specifically CVD and LB pathologies, and highlight their synergistic role in the severity of parkinsonism. Prior studies indicate that LB and CVD pathologies each individually play crucial roles in the development of parkinsonism in older adults without a clinical diagnosis of PD. We and others have reported that LBs are associated with increased severity of parkinsonism but do not account for the full variation of parkinsonian motor impairment in older persons. 11 , 26 , 27 Many autopsy studies have reported that brain vascular pathology including vessel disease pathology and infarcts are associated with higher odds of parkinsonian signs. 3 , 10 , 28 - 30 Several brain imaging studies of older adults report that white matter hyperintensities and lacunar infarcts are associated with higher odds of parkinsonian signs in old age. 28 - 31 Furthermore, our recent postmortem study has reported that more than 3/4th of older adults have evidence of multiple brain pathologies and clinical expression of the progression of parkinsonism in older people is not specific for the presence of a single brain pathology; rather it is more associated with mixed pathologies, with the most common being CVD and PD pathology. 8 These studies raise the possibility that the presence of CVD pathology in the brain may act to further reduce reserve mechanisms, such as structural or network integrity or plasticity, in persons with LB-associated parkinsonism. We are not aware of other autopsy-based studies that have examined a potential interaction between CVD and LB pathology to potentiate late-life parkinsonism. The current clinical-pathologic study confirms our previous findings. 11 We confirm that LB and cerebrovascular disease pathologies add to the severity of parkinsonism. We expand on this observation in several ways. First, we show that macroscopic infarcts and atherosclerosis interact with LB to potentiate the severity of parkinsonism before death compared with the additive effect of both pathologies. Second, the association of LB with parkinsonism is stronger when both vascular pathologies are present than only one of them is present. Third, we show the specificity of this synergistic effect of cerebrovascular (atherosclerosis and macroscopic infarcts) and LB pathology on gait problems, while there is no interaction between cerebrovascular and LB pathology for other parkinsonian sign scores. Finally, the synergistic effect was robust even after controlling or excluding multiple potential confounders, for example, brain pathologies, vascular risk factors, and clinical PD. Overall, these findings have important implications for the etiology of parkinsonism in aging by unraveling complex relationships between LB and CVD pathology and specifically suggest that prevention of CVD pathology may reduce the magnitude of parkinsonism in the older population. This suggestion is recently supported by a study indicating that statins have a potential role in lowering the risk of parkinsonism by reducing the odds of brain atherosclerosis. 32 LB and CVD pathology seem to be important to potentiate the severity of late-life parkinsonism, but the interaction between LB and CVD pathology is likely complex. Our previous study indicates that most of the cases with moderate-to-severe nigral neuronal loss had LB, and the relation of LB with parkinsonism was mediated by nigral neuronal loss. 11 This adds complexity to separate the individual effects of LB from nigral neuronal loss in parkinsonism; however, in this study, the interaction between LB and CVD pathology remained significant after controlling for nigral neuronal loss. Thus, the structural integrity of the nigral neuronal population, which could perhaps be affected by vascular disease, is unlikely to account for the interaction. Indeed, the mechanism underlying this synergistic effect of CVD and LB pathology in developing worse parkinsonism will require further study. One potential hypothesis regarding the etiology of macroscopic infarcts is that they are considered a result of large vessel disease pathology, including atherosclerosis because of compromised blood flow, and the presence of these pathologies can drastically change the brain environment by producing increased oxidative stress and inflammation. 33 , 34 So it is possible that aggravation of oxidative stress and inflammation by CVD may make neurons more susceptible to the toxic effects of α-Syn and potentiate more severe parkinsonism. Alternatively, CVD and LB might affect separate pathways and having both pathologies could lead to decreased reserve or plasticity in the context of parkinsonism in older adults. A few studies support these hypotheses. An in vivo study using human SH-SY5Y neuroblastoma cells suggest that widespread insufficiency of blood flow can contribute to LB pathogenesis through accumulation of α-Syn, 35 with another human study demonstrating that increased vascular inflammation may act as a risk factor of PD progression. 36 Another study conducted on rodent brains showed that stroke can produce motor dysfunction through upregulating serine-129 (S129) phosphorylation of alpha-Syn (pα-Syn) and nuclear translocation of pα-Syn. 37 This study also reported that knockdown of α-Syn improved motor function after stroke. 37 These studies further support the idea that vascular changes can interact in an important way with neurodegenerative processes to potentiate the severity of parkinsonism. Our current study is cross-sectional; therefore, it is difficult to determine whether CVD pathology precedes LB or vice versa. Further studies specifically focusing on understanding mechanisms by which vascular and LB interact may thus be useful in the development and interpretation of future treatment. In addition, the identification of molecular and imaging markers that determine vascular damage and neurodegeneration will be imperative to understand the complex etiology of parkinsonism. Overall, findings from this study advance our knowledge regarding parkinsonism in the context of comorbid LB and CVD pathology. A major strength of this study is that findings are based on 3 large community-based studies, which enrolled individuals without known dementia and have high rates of clinical follow-up and autopsy. A detailed, uniform structured clinical procedure was used to assess parkinsonian signs across all 3 cohorts. Uniform structured neuropathologic evaluations were performed to assess cerebrovascular and neurodegenerative pathologies blinded to clinical data, specifically some of which are not feasible to detect by neuroimaging techniques. Some limitations are also noted in this study. Study participants were mostly non-Latino White adults and highly educated. Therefore, our findings should be replicated in populations with more diversity in race, ethnicity, and education. The participants were mostly the oldest old individuals (90% were older than 80 years at death), so these findings may not extend to younger older population. Other studies that have access to more autopsies of participants near age 70 years will be necessary to understand whether different associations exist between brain pathologies and parkinsonian signs. Despite the fact that multiple vascular pathologies were examined in this study, we did not examine other pathologies such as microhemorrhage or enlarged perivascular spaces. Arteriolosclerosis severity was assessed only in the anterior basal ganglia, which can underestimate the interaction between arteriolosclerosis and LB for parkinsonism. Finally, the study is cross-sectional; therefore, consequences and cause cannot be established. Acknowledgment The authors thank participants from the Rush Religious Orders Study, Rush Memory and Aging Project, and Minority Aging Research Study. The authors also thank investigators and staff at Rush Alzheimer Disease Center. Glossary AD Alzheimer disease CAA cerebral amyloid angiopathy CVD cerebrovascular disease IQR interquartile range LB Lewy body MAP Memory and Aging Project MARS Minority Aging Research Study PD Parkinson disease ROS Religious Orders Study Appendix. Authors Open in a new tab Footnotes Editorial, page 290 Study Funding This work was supported by National Institute of Health grants, R01AG022018, R01AG056352, R01AG047976, R01AG059732, R01AG017917, P30AG010161, P30AG072975, R01AG015819, and R01AG022018. Disclosure The authors report no relevant disclosures. Go to Neurology.org/N for full disclosures. References 1. Buchman AS, Leurgans SE, Yu L, et al. Incident parkinsonism in older adults without Parkinson disease. Neurology. 2016;87(10):1036-1044. doi: 10.1212/wnl.0000000000003059 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Buchman AS, Wilson RS, Shulman JM, Leurgans SE, Schneider JA, Bennett DA. Parkinsonism in older adults and its association with adverse health outcomes and neuropathology. J Gerontol A Biol Sci Med 