INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5341,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11487692','Is palatal cavity volume affected by maxillary sinus pathologies? A CBCT study.','Erturk M, Tassoker M, Aydın Kabakcı AD.','2024','mso itk mso msos itk pneumatic aesthetically palatum durum roof palatine dome vault parafunctional stomatognathic viana palatum durum antrolith periostitis orthodontic malocclusions bruxism rme vault lione bruxists orthodontic plaster polyposal mucoceles leveling hypothesizes necmettin erbakan mso polyposal leveling orthodontic orthodontic orthognathic newtom giano 160mm x180 100mm 130mm ostia frankfort mso msos ostia polyposal leveling polypoidal corticated radiopaque polyposal honeycomb itk cognitica itksnap leftmost itk heine msos msos ostia ostia ostia ostia polyposal polyposal polyposal mm3 msos mso mso mso vif r_mph r_mrc r_afl l_mh l_mph l_mrc l_afl vif r_mh r_mph r_mrc r_afl l_np l_mh l_mph l_mrc l_m l_afl polyposal afl leveling eigenvalue r_mh r_mph r_mrc r_afl l_np l_mh l_mph l_mrc l_m l_afl palatal_volume r_mh r_mph r_mrc r_afl l_np l_mh l_mph l_mrc l_m l_afl adjusts flattens breathers breathers mso inning osteomeatal osteomeatal nsd nsd arching vault dolichocephalic brachycephalic canines bruxist bruxist bruxists bruxists bourdiol perimeter arched plaster mm3 mm3 bruxism malocclusions severities polypoid orthodontists busra nisa mso rme polyposal afl leveling nsd necmettin erbakan stipulations ınterests aiello arx lozanoff arx lozanoff netter netter ziylan aktoren cocuklarda agız solunumunun kraniyofasiyal gelisime agız saglıgına etkileri dellavia orthodontics lione casts orthodontics marinelli mariotti defraia arches malocclusion dentition bruxist dentition agacayak breathers viana alhaija unilaterally canines temur maksiller patolojilerinin osteomeatal kompleksin radyolojik olarak degerlendirilmesi arsiv kaynak tarama dergisi heiser nonextraction orthodontics dentofacial gracco dentition orthodontist gohl enciso vault orthodontically orthodontics dentofacial saadeh ghafari malocclusions orthodontics bourdıol vault shahen casts orthodontist prasanna mamatha buzi icex bhagyalakshmi renukarya rajangam berwig dimensoes palato duro respiradores nasais orais etiologias jornal fonoaudiologia pachipulusu govindraju berwig codas e20170216 vault dentition orthodontist esteves bommarito profundidade palato dimensoes arco dentario individuos oclusao tipos faciais ortodontia ortopedia primozic crossbite dentition orthodontics yassaei monga orthodontics bruxist dentition baghdady kecik orthodontist','Abstract Aim This study uses cone beam computed tomography (CBCT) to determine whether pathology in the maxillary sinus (MS) affects the volume of the palatal cavity. Methods 188 individuals, 95 women and 93 men, aged between 17 and 63, were included in the study. MS pathology in the patients and the open-closed status of the maxillary sinus ostium (MSO) were recorded. Palatal volume measurements were performed using open-access ITK-SNAP via CBCT images. Statistical analysis of the study was conducted using SPSS v.21 software (IBM. Chicago. IL. USA), and p <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results The average palatal volume was 1375.29±313.38 mm 3 in male patients and 1235.33±250.40 mm 3 in females, and it was found to be statistically significant between genders ( p= 0.001). MS pathology was detected in 114 (60.6%) of the patients. It was determined that the most frequently observed pathology in both the right ( n = 58, 30.9%) and left ( n = 65, 34.6%) side MS of individuals was mucosal hypertrophy. It was determined that the mean palatal volume was higher when the MSO was closed ( p = 0.000). As a result of the correlation analysis, it was shown that the presence of MS pathology had a positive effect in explaining palate volume by 38.6% ( R 2 = 0.386). Conclusion Palatal cavity volume was affected by maxillary sinus pathologies. Palatal cavity volume increases in the presence of MS pathologies and when MSOs are closed. Keywords: Maxillary sinus, Palatal cavity volume, Pathology, CBCT, ITK-Snap, Volumetric analysis Introduction The facial skeleton comprises orbital cavities, nasal and pneumatic cavities, oral cavity, jaws, and temporomandibular joint (TMJ) [ 1 ]. These structures support the face functionally and aesthetically. One of the essential elements of the maxillofacial structures that contribute to the facial skeleton is the hard palate (palatum durum). The hard palate, which serves critical functions such as chewing and speaking, forms the roof of the oral cavity and the floor of the nasal cavity [ 2 ]. The hard palate consists of the maxillary bone''s palatal process and the palatine bone''s horizontal lamina [ 3 ]. The height of the palate, also called the palatal dome in the oral cavity, is affected by various parameters. Especially in mouth-breathing individuals, the morphological characteristics of the palatal vault also change, and a more extended palatal height is observed [ 4 ]. Palate size is affected by many factors other than mouth breathing, such as tongue size, tooth inclination, occlusion, and parafunctional habits [ 5 – 8 ]. The central location of the hard palate and its close relationship with the adjacent nasal cavity, oral cavity, and maxillary sinus (MS) increase the likelihood that any morphological change in these structures will affect other components of the stomatognathic system [ 9 ]. Miranda-Viana et al. [ 10 ] reported in their study that there is a relationship between the width and height of the hard palate and the volume of the maxillary sinuses, and the greater the width and height of the hard palate, the greater the volume of the maxillary sinuses. Pyramid-shaped MSs are the first paranasal sinuses to develop and have a mean volume of 6-8 cm 3 at birth [ 11 ]. MS and palatum durum show two development phases between 0-3 and 7-12 years. The mean volume of MS, which reaches adult size between the ages of 14 and 18, is approximately 15 cm 3 [ 12 ]. While the intrinsic diseases of MS include mucositis, sinusitis, mucus retention cyst, polyp, mucocele, and antrolith, the extrinsic diseases include periostitis, benign odontogenic cysts, tumors, and fibrous dysplasia [ 13 ]. In the literature, morphometric changes related to the palatal cavity are primarily compared before and after orthodontic treatment [ 14 – 16 ]. In addition, there are studies evaluating the relationship between malocclusions [ 17 ], tongue position [ 18 ], mouth breathing [ 6 ], bruxism [ 8 ], and palatal cavity morphometry. Palatal volume has been reported to increase with rapid maxillary expansion (RME) [ 14 ]. It has been determined that tongue volume has a significant positive correlation with palatal vault height [ 18 ]. Lione et al. [ 6 ] have stated that individuals with prolonged mouth breathing have different palatal morphology compared to those with normal breathing and that palatal volume is reduced in these individuals. Another study revealed that bruxists had a statistically significant more extended palate in the sagittal plane than controls [ 8 ]. However, to our knowledge, no study has investigated the effect of maxillary sinus pathologies on palatal cavity volume. While the literature offers insights into the morphometric changes of the palatal cavity related to orthodontic treatment [ 14 – 16 ] and other oral conditions [ 6 , 8 , 17 , 18 ], the potential impact of MS pathologies on palatal structure remains unknown. Addressing this knowledge gap contributes to early diagnosis and intervention of potential orofacial complications. It may also enable the development of improved treatment plans for patients with MS pathologies and palatal abnormalities. Furthermore, it is crucial to understand the potential impact of pathological conditions in structures adjacent to the hard palate on craniofacial growth and development. Plaster models and 3D imaging techniques can be used when measuring the volume of the palatal cavity, which forms the superior border of the oral cavity [ 15 , 19 ]. This study aims to determine whether the palatal volume is affected by the presence of specific maxillary sinus (MS) pathologies—such as mucosal hypertrophy, mucosal polyposal hypertrophy, mucus retention cysts, mucoceles, and air-fluid leveling—using cone beam computed tomography (CBCT). The study hypothesizes that these MS pathologies may contribute to changes in palatal volume, potentially leading to an increase in volume due to the impact of the pathologies on surrounding structures. This research seeks to clarify the relationship between MS pathologies and palatal volume alterations. Methods Sample and study design The necessary approval for the study was received from Necmettin Erbakan University Dentistry Non-Drug and Medical Device Research Ethics Committee (approval no: 2023/350, meeting date: 28.12.2023). All procedures followed were by the ethical standards of the committee responsible for human experimentation (institutional and national) and with the 1975 Helsinki Declaration as revised in 2008. All data was anonymized, and a waiver of informed consent was obtained from the ethics committee. In the study, cone beam computed tomography (CBCT) records of 188 individuals, 95 women and 93 men, were retrospectively examined. The age range of the participants is 17-63 years. A blinded operator measured All palatal volumes twice in 60 patients (30 controls and 30 patients with maxillary sinus pathology). Intraclass correlation coefficients were highly reliable (palatal cavity volume ICC=0.992, 95% CI:0.979-0.998, p< 0.001). Data in which the right/left (MS) and right/left maxillary sinus ostium (MSO) regions were visible were included in the study. Patients with mucosal hypertrophy, mucosal polyposal hypertrophy, mucus retention cyst, mucocele, and air-fluid leveling were included in the study. Other infectious and inflammatory conditions and tumors were excluded from the study. We excluded patients who had undergone orthodontic treatment, particularly involving rapid maxillary expansion, orthodontic tooth movements, or orthognathic surgery. Cleft lip and palate patients, those who had upper jaw and/or maxillary sinus (MS) surgery, and patients with a history of head or neck trauma were also excluded. Furthermore, records with artifacts, including metal, motion, or noise artifacts, were not considered. Image acquisition CBCT images were acquired by NewTom GiANO HR devices (Verona, Italy, 90 kV and 10 mA, 18 seconds rotation time, voxel 0.15 mm, 160mm x180 mm, and 100mm x 130mm fields of view). CBCT data were saved in dicom file format. Radiological examination All evaluations were performed by the same oral radiologist (ME) with two years of oral radiology experience. MS ostia were evaluated using coronal CBCT sections, and the presence of pathologies was assessed by examining each of the three planes (axial, coronal, and sagittal) separately. The axial plane was standardized by aligning it with the Frankfort horizontal plane, and the coronal and sagittal planes were adjusted in relation to the midline structures of the skull. This standardization minimized potential variations and ensured uniformity in assessing pathologies and anatomical features. To evaluate MSO, images in the coronal section are examined; the natural opening of the MS was observed at the superior of the medial wall and inferior to the orbital floor (Fig. 1 ) [ 20 ]. Right/left MSOs were evaluated separately for each patient and recorded as open or closed. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Right/left MS Ostia Maxillary sinus pathologies were classified as present/absent. The pathological conditions observed are mucosal hypertrophy, mucosal polyposal hypertrophy, mucus retention cyst, mucocele, and air-fluid leveling in the sinus (Fig. 2 ). Their diagnostic criteria are given below: Mucosal Hypertrophy: Sinus mucosa shows thickening more significant than 4 mm, often symmetric. Mucosal Polypoidal Hypertrophy: a well-defined, non-corticated, circular, or globular radiopaque area within the sinus. It is characterized by thickening and expansion of the sinus mucosa. It can often spread throughout the sinus and grow inward from the sinus walls, narrowing the sinus cavity. Mucus Retention Cyst: Well-circumscribed, fluid-filled cystic structure adherent to the sinus wall. They are usually located at the sinus''s base and confined within the sinus cavity. Mucocele: Expanded sinus cavity with smooth, homogenous soft tissue mass, potentially causing bony erosion. Air-Fluid Level: Horizontal air-fluid level within the sinus, often indicating acute sinusitis or trauma. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab A mucosal hypertrophy, B polyposal hypertrophy, C mucus retention cyst in right/left MS, D mucus retention cyst appearance in axial section, E air-fluid level (honeycomb appearance), F mucocele shows the destructive area created by the mucocele surrounded by yellow color Volumetric assessment Palatal volume measurements were performed with open access ITK-SNAP (version 4.0.1) software (Cognitica, Philadelphia, PA, USA) ( https://www.itksnap.org ). Three points were determined to achieve standardization in palatal volume measurement [ 21 ]. These are the most anterior midline points of the maxillary alveolar process and the inferior points at the right and leftmost distal parts of the maxillary alveolar crest, respectively (Fig. 3 ). The inferior border of the measurement is determined by the anterior midline of the maxillary alveolar process, and the superior border is determined by the posterior nasal spine (PNS) point, the most posterior point of the palatal bone. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab A, the boundaries determined in the axial section: 1, the most anterior midline point of the maxillary alveolar process; 2, the most distal and inferior point of the right maxillary arch; 3, the most distal and inferior point of the left maxillary arch, B the inferior point of the palatal volume (the most anterior point of the maxillary alveolar process on midline sagittal view), C PNS (superior border point of palatal volume) After the ROI borders were determined, the palatal region was segmented, and the region''s volume was converted to mm 3 , which was recorded for each patient (Fig. 4 ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Volumetric analysis of the palatal region in ITK-SNAP software (version 4.0.1) Statistical analysis Statistical analysis of the study was performed using SPSS v.21 software (IBM Corp., Armonk, NY, USA). The suitability of the parameters to normal distribution was evaluated with the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test and Skewness and Kurtosis values. The data obtained were evaluated descriptively (mean value, standard deviation, maximum and minimum values, percentages) and quantitatively. The differences between the volumetric measurements of palatal cavities of male and female patients were compared using an independent sample t-test. Correlation analysis was used to show how adequate MS pathology was in explaining palate volume, and the chi-square test was used to analyze the relationship between categorical variables. Multiple regression analysis was used to determine how much the palatal cavity volume is affected by one or more pathologies in the maxillary sinus. p< 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results The study''s power for the independent sample t-test was calculated based on the sample size of two groups and an effect size equal to 0.5 (11). The power(1-β) was 0.90 at an alpha level of 0.05 (G*Power 3.1, Heinrich-Heine-Universität Düsseldorf, Germany). Retrospectively, CBCT images of 95 female and 93 male individuals recorded between 2020 and 2023 were analyzed. The ages of the study participants ranged between 17 and 63 (mean 29.35) years. The categorical data analysis results The changes in palatal cavity volume were determined separately for the right and left sides in cases where the MSOs were open or closed. Of the individuals'' right-side MSOs, 120 (63.8%) were open, and 68 (36.2%) were closed, while on the left side, 104 (55.3%) were open, and 84 (44.7%) were closed (Fig. 5 ). It has been determined that the closed or open status of the maxillary sinus ostia on the right side (X 2 =4.995, p= 0.052) of individuals is related to gender, but is independent of gender on the left (X 2 =2.554, p= 0.110) side (Table 1 ). Fig. 5. Open in a new tab The incidences of open and closed maxillary sinus ostia (%) Table 1. The significance of right and left side maxillary sinus ostia between genders (X 2 : The Chi-square value, df: the degree of freedom, p: the significance value, n: the number of patients, %: frequency) Maxillary sinus ostia Right Left Sex Type n % X 2 df p n % X 2 df p Male Closed 41 44.1 4.995 1 0.033 47 50.5 2.554 1 0.142 Open 52 55.9 46 49.5 Total 93 100 93 100 Female Closed 27 28.4 37 38.9 Open 68 71.6 58 61.1 Total 95 100 95 100 Open in a new tab MS pathology was detected in 114 (60.6%) of the patients included in the study. The study determined mucosal hypertrophy was the most frequently observed pathology in the right ( n =58, 30.9%) and left ( n =65, 34.6%) side MS of patient individuals. The least common MS pathology on the right side was mucus retention cyst ( n =6, 3.2%), and on the left side was mucocele ( n =2, 1.1%) (Table 2 ). The study showed that the presence of maxillary sinus pathologies was statistically related to gender (X 2 =5.158, p= 0.023). In our research, it was determined that maxillary sinus pathology types caused a significant difference in male individuals (X 2 =12.242, p= 0.016), but this was not significant in female individuals (X 2 =7.06, p= 0.219) (Table 3 ). Table 2. The percentages of maxillary sinus pathology types between the right and left sides (n: the number of patients, %: frequency) Maxillary sinus pathology Right Left n % n % No pathology 95 50.5 86 45.7 Mucosal hypertrophy 58 30.9 65 34.6 Mucosal polyposal hypertro