ding to right/left MSO (p: significance value) Palatal cavity volume (mm 3) Right MSO Left MSO Open Closed Open Closed Min value 824,00 858,00 814,00 925,00 Max value 2187,00 2217,00 2187,00 2217,00 Mean value 1244,00 1410,8382 1218,3077 1411,3690 p=0.000 p=0.000 Open in a new tab Correlation analysis results In our study, correlation analysis was performed to evaluate how much maxillary sinus pathologies affect the palatal cavity volume. The correlation analysis showed that MS pathology effectively explained palate volume by 38.6% ( R 2 = 0.386) (Fig. 6 ). Fig. 6. Open in a new tab The correlation analysis graph of palatal cavity volume and the presence of MS pathology Multiple linear regression analysis results Multiple regression analysis was used to detect changes in the volume of the palatal cavity in the presence of one or more pathologies located in the maxillary sinus. The multiple regression analysis examined whether there was a multicollinearity problem between the independent variables. The independent variables'' VIF, tolerance, and condition index data were analyzed, and it was determined that there was no multicollinearity problem between the variables (Tables 2 and 3 ). The effect of right and left-side MS pathologies on palatal cavity volume was examined by regression analysis. As a result of the study, R_MPH ( p= 0.021), R_MRC ( p= 0.02), R_AFL ( p= 0.000), L_MH ( p= 0.000), L_MPH ( p= 0.000), L_MRC ( p= 0.002) and L_AFL ( p= 0.000) parameters were found to have a positive effect on palatal cavity volume (Tables 5 and 6 ). As a result of multiple regression analysis, the regression equation according to the parameters affecting the palatal cavity volume is given as follows: Table 5. The multiple linear regression analysis results for factors affecting palatal cavity volume Unstandardized Coefficients Standardized Coefficients t Sig. 95,0% Confidence Interval for B Correlations Collinearity Statistics B Std. Error Beta Lower Bound Upper Bound Zero-or