 ,10 ,36 a. Dependent Variable: Palatal_Volume Open in a new tab Palatal cavity volume=1090,842+63.420* R_MH+154.226* R_MPH+296,235* R_MRC+297,508* R_AFL-137,428* L_NP+244,928*L_MH+310,244* L_MPH+285,506* L_MRC+291,738* L_M+413,829* L_AFL As can be seen from the formula, just one unit increase in the left air-fluid level causes an increase of 413,829 mm 3 in the volume of the palatal cavity. The presence of other maxillary sinus pathologies causes an increase in the palatal cavity volume at varying rates. Discussion The hard palate, an essential aid to speech, is an understudied area in anatomy studies [ 22 ]. Due to the close relationship of the hard palate with the maxillary sinus and nasal cavity, these structures may affect changes in its morphology. The hard palate adjusts shape and position in response to airway obstruction [ 10 ]. This adaptive response highlights the functional plasticity of craniofacial anatomy in the context of airway dynamics, which may play a critical role in managing obstructive respiratory conditions. Maxillary sinus pathologies are common and were detected in 60.6% of the individuals included in the study. This study investigated the effect of maxillary sinus pathologies and ostium closure on palatal cavity volume. The functional matrix theory suggests that breathing significantly influences craniofacial growth. Nasal breathing can encourage the lateral expansion of the maxilla, which flattens the palate and impacts the breathing process [ 6 ]. It has been shown that mouth breathers have a more profound and narrower hard palate than nasal breathers [ 23 ]. It can be inferred that chronic respiratory obstruction may affect current health and have long-term morphological consequences, potentially altering the course of craniofacial development. The clinical implications are profound, as interventions to restore nasal breathing could prevent or mitigate such structural changes, reducing the risk of developing related complications. This study showed that the mean palatal volume was higher when the MSO was closed and that the presence of the MS path positively explained inning palate volume by 38.6%. Osteomeatal obstruction can impair maxillary sinus ventilation and increase the risk of pathology. As a result, mouth breathing may be the primary means of air intake. This highlights a critical need for early identification and management of osteomeatal complex dysfunction, which could prevent secondary changes in palatal morphology and potentially reduce the risk of associated respiratory issues. A recent study [ 24 ] showed that palatal arch depth and width were higher in individuals with nasal septal deviation. The absence of linear measurements in the axial, coronal, and sagittal planes limits our ability to determine which specific dimension primarily contributes to changes in palatal volume. In future studies, performing linear measurements in these planes would offer a more comprehensive understanding of the dimensional changes contributing to the overall volume. Shetty et al. [ 25 ] demonstrated that the presence of nasal septal deviation (NSD) and concha bullosa (CB) have a significant effect on the palatal dimensions. These results were attributed to the fact that nasal airway passage blockage caused by CB and NSD may affect the growth and downward arching of the palate. The present study observed that men''s palatal cavity volumes were more significant than women''s. Previous studies on palatal cavity height and width have reported that men show higher values [ 10 , 26 , 27 ]. Tsai et al. [ 28 ] found that the height of the palatal vault increases with age. In addition to age and gender, various parameters that may affect the palatal dimension have been investigated in the literature. Dolichocephalic patients tend to exhibit vertical craniofacial growth, resulting in a narrower and deeper hard palate. In contrast, brachycephalic patients typically show horizontal craniofacial growth, with a broader and more regular hard palate [ 29 ]. These craniofacial patterns suggest that head shape may significantly determine palatal morphology. Additionally, unilateral posterior cross-bite was associated with low palatal cavity volume [ 30 ]. A recent study found that impacted canines were significantly associated with volume reduction on the affected side [ 31 ]. In another study, unilateral and bilateral cleft lip groups were reported to be smaller than controls [ 32 ]. Based on this, these patients were excluded as it could lead to bias in our study group. Restrepo et al. [ 33 ] compared the palatal dimensions of bruxist and non-bruxist children and reported that bruxists had larger values. They stated that this may be due to bruxists'' higher muscular activity. Bourdiol et al. [ 18 ] reported a strong correlation between tongue volume, maxillary inter-molar width, and palatal perimeter. These findings emphasize the importance of functional factors in craniofacial development. Another study [ 22 ] found that the hard palate in children with Down syndrome was high-arched and narrow. In Turner syndrome [ 34 ], palatal height was higher than in the average control population. Syndromic variations in palatal morphology underscore the importance of personalized approaches in clinical practice. Individuals with genetic conditions may require tailored interventions to address their unique anatomical and functional needs. Moreover, further research into the genetic factors influencing palatal development could enhance our understanding of these conditions and improve diagnostic and therapeutic approaches. Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a disease characterized by attacks of upper airway obstruction during sleep, resulting in recurrent hypoxia. In the study using plaster models of the maxillary arch, the palatal volume was measured as 6755.63 mm3 in the OSA group and 7254.67 mm3 in the control group. Palatal area measurements were significantly smaller in OSA [ 35 ]. Limitations The most significant limitation of our study is its retrospective design, which inherently restricts the ability to establish causality. This methodology precludes rigorous control over confounding variables and potential biases. While considering the impact of skeletal class and growth pattern on palatal morphology, we must acknowledge that other unmeasured confounders may have influenced our findings. Nasal breathing patterns, genetic predisposition, and functional factors such as tongue posture, mouth breathing, and bruxism are potential confounders that could affect palatal dimensions. Additionally, skeletal facial type, dental malocclusions, and tooth inclination may play a role. The absence of data on these variables limits the ability to fully assess their impact on the observed relationship between maxillary sinus pathologies and palatal morphology. Future prospective studies with standardized inclusion criteria, including detailed assessments of skeletal class, growth patterns, and potential confounders, are warranted to address these limitations. Suggestions for future research This study showed that the presence of MS pathology positively affected palate volume by 38.6%. By addressing the following questions, researchers can better understand the underlying mechanisms and clinical implications of the observed relationship between maxillary sinus pathologies and palatal changes. Future studies should explore the factors contributing to the remaining 61.4% palate volume variation. Specifically, the role of genetic, environmental, and developmental factors (such as skeletal class and facial growth patterns) in interaction with MS pathology should be investigated. It is recommended to examine whether different types of maxillary sinus pathologies (e.g., chronic sinusitis, fungal sinusitis, inverted papilloma) lead to distinct changes in palatal cavity volume. To better understand potential relationships, it is suggested that the correlation between the severity and duration of maxillary sinus pathologies and palatal cavity volume be investigated. Evaluating the effects of treating maxillary sinus pathologies on the progression of palatal changes would benefit understanding treatment outcomes. Roadmap for future research Longitudinal studies may be initiated to monitor palatal changes over time in patients with different severities of various sinus pathologies. Based on the findings, clinical guidelines can be developed for the early detection and management of palatal changes in patients with maxillary sinus pathologies. Conclusion This study demonstrates a positive association between increased palatal cavity volume and MS pathologies such as mucosal thickening, mucosal polypoid hypertrophy, mucus retention cyst, and air-fluid levels within the maxillary sinuses. These findings reinforce the study hypothesis, suggesting that MS pathologies may contribute to palatal morphological changes. Clinicians, particularly dentists and oral surgeons, should remain alert to potential skeletal changes in the palatal region. This is crucial when treating patients with MS pathologies or airway obstructions. Early identification of these changes could prompt timely referrals to specialists, such as otolaryngologists or orthodontists, for comprehensive evaluation and management. This proactive approach may help prevent or mitigate complications associated with altered palatal morphology. Future research should explore the interaction between MS pathologies and other contributing factors to uncover the mechanisms driving palatal changes. This knowledge could lead to the development of more effective preventive measures and therapeutic interventions, ultimately improving patient outcomes. Acknowledgments I extend my special thanks to PhD Candidate Büşra Nisa Yilmaz for her professional editing of the manuscript in accordance with English language rules. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Abbreviations TMJ Temporomandibular 