INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5343,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12140547','Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones in Resident Evaluation: Reliability, Validity, and Correlation with Objective Examination Scores: A Cross-sectional Study.','Ruan X, Xu X, Pei L, Yi J, Yu C, Yu X, Zhu B, Quan X, Li X, Jv H, Zhang Y, Huang Y.','2024','recategorized emulation mkts lll pbli restructured relocated mkts pbli lll relocate 4ccc guidebook manikins deduction rerate nonnormally categorial olkin kmo kmo srmr gfi tli normed nfi srmr gfi tli nfi uncorrelated srmr gfi tli nfi subcompetency subcompetency discriminability mkts mkts desctools lavaan semtools subcompetency amalgamating subcompetency f58 subcompetency subcompetency prof1 prof2 conscientiousness demeanor prof3 prof4 transited prof5 mkts1 mkts2 transited mkts3 transited mkts4 preanesthetic pc3 pc4 pc5 pc6 pc7 ics1 ics2 ics3 ta1 popularization lll1 transited lll2 subcompetency pgys subcompetency prof1 prof2 prof3 prof4 prof5 mkts1 mkts2 mkts3 mkts4 pc3 pc4 pc5 pc6 pc7 ics1 ics2 ics3 ta1 lll1 lll2 lll mkts subcompetency kmo sphericity srmr gfi tli nfi prof1 prof2 prof3 prof4 prof5 mkts1 mkts2 mkts3 mkts4 pc3 pc4 pc5 pc6 pc7 ics1 ics2 ics3 ta1 lll1 lll2 lll mkts literal reassigned comprehensible subcompetency mkts accustomed discriminability lll postgraduation yifei xhzj2407 2021zlgc0115 nemergut reprints nasca philibert harsy ambardekar ccoet bewildering guidebook acgmeclinical competencycommitteeguidebook psychometrika nora bentler multidiscip sivo witta willse herdman signorell aho alfons desctools andrisignorell desctools rosseel lavaan softw pornprasertmanit schoemann semtools simsem semtools gamer harman rathmell bienstock shivraj hamstra cuddy jurich vandergrift lipner vandergrift kimbrough thrush roby beaman','Abstract BACKGROUND: Evaluating competency acquisition during residency training is crucial. The Anesthesiology Milestones have been implemented in the United States. The China Consortium of Elite Teaching Hospitals for Residency Education has also developed the Chinese Resident Core Competency Milestone Evaluation System. Despite this, Milestones tailored for anesthesiology have yet to be implemented in China. To address this gap, we have developed Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones. This study aims to assess the reliability and validity of the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones and their correlation with objective examinations. METHODS: In this single-center cross-sectional study, we included anesthesia residents enrolled in the standardized residency training program at our hospital during the academic year 2021 to 2022. The Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones were developed based on the American version of Anesthesiology Milestones 2.0 and the Chinese Resident Core Competency Milestone Evaluation System using the Delphi method. The Delphi panel comprised a diverse group, including education administrators, faculty from teaching hospitals, and anesthesia residents. Five attending anesthesiologists independently assessed the levels achieved by each anesthesia resident based on the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones. Subsequently, they collaboratively discussed the ratings for each resident until a consensus was reached. The interrater reliability, internal consistency, and construct validity were assessed using Kendall’s coefficient, Cronbach’s α coefficient/ composite reliability, and average variance extracted, respectively. Higher values indicated better reliability or validity. The correlation between Milestone ratings and objective examination scores, including written examinations and Objective Structured Clinical Examinations, were analyzed using Pearson correlation. RESULTS: The Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones encompassed 6 competencies, including professionalism, medical knowledge and technical skills, patient care, interpersonal and communication skills, teaching ability, and life-long learning. Milestone evaluation data were available and analyzed from 66 residents. The Kendall’s coefficient of concordance among raters ranged from 0.799 (95% confidence interval [CI], 0.793–0.918) to 0.942 (95% CI, 0.934–0.982). The average variance extracted, composite reliability, and Cronbach’s α coefficient ranged from 0.782 to 0.920, 0.935 to 0.980, and 0.916 to 0.978, respectively. Correlations between objective examination scores and related Milestone subcompetencies were as follows: written examinations: r = 0.52 (95% CI, 0.22–0.71), technical skills stations: r = 0.51 (95% CI, 0.21–0.71), the oral test station: r = 0.66 (95% CI, 0.45–0.79), and the standardized patient station: r = 0.61 (95% CI, 0.36–0.76). CONCLUSIONS: The Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones demonstrated satisfactory interrater reliability, internal consistency, construct validity, and correlation with objective examination scores within our hospital. KEY POINTS. Question: What is the reliability and validity of the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones? Findings: The Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones demonstrated satisfactory interrater reliability, internal consistency, construct validity, and correlation with objective examination scores within our hospital. Meaning: These results underscore the effectiveness and credibility of the milestone tool in describing competency acquisition during residency training. Residency training programs worldwide are undergoing a transition from traditional time-based curricula to competency-based medical education. Assessing competency acquisition and measuring educational outcomes are pivotal in the new era. 1 The Milestones system, launched by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) in collaboration with the American Board of Medical Specialties in 2013, introduced 6 domains of competencies and corresponding subcompetencies. 2 Subsequently, specialty-specific Milestones systems were developed and implemented in the United States, including the Anesthesiology Milestones. 2 , 3 The Milestones System offers a comprehensive and structured descriptions of progressive competency goals that residents are expected to achieve throughout their training. Assessments of the milestones achieved by residents in the Milestones System can provide an understanding of their strengths, shortcomings, and goals for improvement, ultimately contributing to the delivery of high-quality patient care by well-trained health care practitioners. Given the advantage of the Milestones system, the Chinese Resident Core Competency Milestone System was recently developed through the utilization of Delphi and focus group methodologies by the China Consortium of Elite Teaching Hospitals for Residency Education. 4 This consortium, supported by the China Medical Board, comprised 9 teaching hospitals led by Peking Union Medical College Hospital. This Milestones system is general and encompasses 6 competencies, similar to the ACGME Milestones. However, these competencies have been tailored, subcompetencies recategorized, and language adjusted to align with China’s health care system and cultural context. The Chinese Milestones have undergone piloting and validation across various standardized residency training programs. 4 Nonetheless, a Chinese version of a specialty-specific Milestones system for anesthesiology has yet to be established, validated, or implemented. The scale and quality of anesthesiology workforce face challenges in meeting the growing surgery demands in China. 5 Medical education programs in China display considerable diversity, offering degrees such as Bachelor of Medicine, Master of Medicine, or Doctor of Medicine. 6 This variance contributes to significant differences in the competency levels of anesthesia residents. Therefore, there is a compelling need to implement a competency-based, staged evaluation system that corresponds to the current landscape of postgraduate medical education in the field of anesthesia within China. The ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones offer a valuable framework for emulation. Acknowledging these gaps, we have formulated Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones by integrating the ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones 2.0 with the Chinese Milestone Evaluation System. The objectives of this study were to assess the reliability and validity of these Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones and to evaluate their correlation with objective examinations. METHODS Ethics and Study Design We conducted a single-center cross-sectional study in Peking Union Medical College Hospital, a general tertiary teaching hospital in Beijing, China. The institutional review board of Peking Union Medical College Hospital classified our study as exempt and waived the need for informed consent. This article adheres to Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) guideline. Selection and Description of Participants We included anesthesia residents who were enrolled in the standardized residency training program in our hospital in the academic year 2021 to 2022. The anesthesiology residency training program in China extends over 3 years. It comprises a 9-month rotation in anesthesia-related departments, encompassing medical, surgical, and intensive care units and a 27-month rotation in various subspecialties of anesthesiology and pain medicine. It’s important to highlight that in China, anesthesia and critical care medicine residency training programs are separate entities. Therefore, rotations in intensive care units last for only 3 months for anesthesia residents in China, which is a major difference from the US training programs. Development of the Chinese Version of Anesthesiology Milestones The Chinese version of Anesthesiology Milestones were developed based on the ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones 2.0 3 and the Chinese Resident Core Competency Milestone Evaluation System. 4 The ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones 2.0 3 was initially translated into Chinese by 2 attending anesthesiologists proficient in medical English. The 6 competencies were defined in the Chinese Milestones system, namely professionalism (PROF), medical knowledge and technical skills (MKTS), patient care (PC), interpersonal and communication skills (ICS), teaching ability (TA), and life-long learning (LLL). 4 These differ from the competencies outlined in the ACGME version, which include PROF, PC, ICS, medical knowledge (MK), system-based practice (SBP), and practice-based learning and improvement (PBLI). To maintain consistency with the competencies in the Chinese version, we restructured the translated ACGME version. Specifically, we relocated “application and interpretation of monitors,” “airway management,” “regional anesthesia,” and “point-of-care ultrasound” from PC to MKTS, modified the name of “PBLI” to “LLL,” and introduced a new competency of TA. Additionally, given the challenges in seamlessly adapting the content of SBP to the Chinese medical system, we opted to relocate 3 subcompetencies originally included in SBP to PROF and PC. In the next step, we modified the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones using the Delphi methods. The Delphi panel comprised a diverse group of experts from 2 general teaching hospitals in Beijing. The panel members included a faculty member from the National Postgraduate Medical Education Anesthesiology Specialty Committee, a faculty representative from the China Consortium of Elite Teaching Hospitals, a Vice Dean from the Education Department, a Vice Dean from the Anesthesiology Department, 4CCC faculty members, and 4 anesthesia residents (2 in PGY 2 and 2 in PGY 3). We requested panel members to provide revision suggestions on the structure, content, and language of the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones back-to-back. We concluded the Delphi method once more than 90% of the panel members reached consensus on all subcompetencies. Implementation of the Chinese Version of Anesthesiology Milestones We involved 5 attending anesthesiologists from our Clinical Competency Committee to evaluate the milestones achieved by the included residents based on the Chinese version of Anesthesiology Milestones. All of them had over 15 years of experience in postgraduate education. Their expertise spanned diverse subspecialties including cardiac, thoracic, obstetric, pediatric, and regional anesthesia, allowing for comprehensive assessment of residents across various subcompetencies. They were enthusiastic about clinical education and provided daily supervision to residents. They were also provided with supervisor’s evaluations and objective examination scores, including the 4 written examinations and an OSCE in the current year, to ensure their familiar with the performance of each resident. They initially conducted independent evaluation of each anesthesia resident to analyze interrater reliability. Subsequently, in alignment with the process described in the ACGME Clinical Competency Committee Guidebook, 7 they collectively discussed the ratings for each resident until a consensus was reached. These consensus scores were then used to analyze internal consistency, construct validity, and correlation with objective examination scores. Objective Examinations All residents who complete the 3-year residency training must take a provincial final examination, which consisted of a written examination and an Objective Structured Clinical Examination (OSCE). Passing this examination is a prerequisite for obtaining a license to practice anesthesia independently as an attending physician. To facilitate our residents to prepare for this provincial examination, we arranged practice examinations in our center. These practice examinations followed the same format as the provincial examination, with case-based single- and multi-select questions for the written portion, and an OSCE setup including an oral test station, a standardized patient (SP) station, and 5 technical skills stations (endotracheal intubation, central line placement, arterial line placement, cardiopulmonary resuscitation, and epidural anesthesia). In the oral test, residents were asked about the preoperative evaluation, anesthesia plan, and management of perioperative complications of a clinical case. The SP station was designed to assess communication skills, with residents completing preoperative visit and informed consent on a SP. In the technical skills stations, residents performed the technical skills on manikins. The difficulty of the oral test and the SP station increased from PGY1 to PGY3, while the technical skills stations remained consistent across all grades. We have rigorous quality control for our practice examinations. The question bank of written examinations was composed of nearly 9000 real examination questions in previous years and mock examination questions. It covered all the subspecialties required for residency training, and was continuously updated. Each question was labeled with its difficulty level and subspecialty. Considering the manpower required for creating and supervising examinations, we organize a written examination every 3 months. The examination content was aligned with the training level. For instance, PGY-1 residents were assessed on anesthesia-related physiology and pharmacology, and principles of general anesthesia and intrathecal anesthesia. In comparison, PGY-3 residents were assessed on cardiac anesthesia, thoracic anesthesia, and pain medicine. We randomly selected questions from different difficulty levels according to a fixed ratio within the planned subspecialty range, ensuring a consistent overall difficulty level for each examination. The OSCE was held once a year. The examiners and SPs for the OSCE underwent regular standardized training. There were 2 examiners at each station. Each independently gave ratings based on a scoring standard sheet, and their average ratings were ultimately used. If the differences between their ratings were greater than 10% of their average ratings, they would discuss the deduction points and rerate until the difference was less than 10%. Outcomes The primary outcomes of this study were the reliability and validity of the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones, as well as their correlation with objective examinations. Statistical Analysis The distribution of the continuous variables was examined using Shapiro-Wilk test. Normally distributed continuous variables, nonnormally distributed continuous variables, and categorial variables were described as mean ± standard deviation, median [interquartile range], and number (percentage), respectively. The interrater reliability was assessed using the Kendall’s coefficient of concordance, which showed the degree of agreement among raters. 8 The internal consistency was measured using the Cronbach’s α coefficient, reflecting how closely related the subcompetencies are within each competency. 9 Construct validity was evaluated using confirmatory factor analysis (CFA), provided that the Kaiser-Meyer-Olkin (KMO) test and Bartlett’s test showed the suitability of factor analysis. A KMO value closer to 1 demonstrated adequate sample size and amount of common variance, while a significant Bartlett’s test indicated significant relationships among the variables. 10 In the CFA framework, the 6 competencies were defined as latent variables. 11 An evaluation of each resident by each rater was considered as an observation, adjusting the clustering effects of evaluations of the same resident by different raters. The goodness of fit of the CFA framework was assessed using the χ 2 test, root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA), standardized root mean square residual (SRMR), goodness of fit index (GFI), comparative fit index (CFI), Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI), normed fit index (NFI), and incremental Fit Index (IFI). Among them, RMSEA measured how well the CFA framework fits the population covariance matrix. SRMR represented the standardized difference between the observed and predicted correlations. GFI assessed the relative amount of variance and covariance explained by the framework. CFI, TLI, NFI, and IFI all compared the fit of the framework to a null model where all variables were uncorrelated. 9 An RMSEA and SRMR ≤0.08, and GFI, CFI, TLI, NFI and IFI ≥0.90 indicated acceptable fit. 12 , 13 The average variance extracted (AVE) and composite reliability (CR) of each competency were calculated based on the standardized factor loading of each subcompetency. AVE measured construct validity, assessing the variance a construct explained relative to measurement error. 14 CR, similar to Cronbach’ α, measured internal consistency but accounted for varying contributions of each subcompetency. 15 Kendall’s coefficient, Cronbach’s α coefficient, AVE, and CR all ranged from 0 to 1, with values ≥0.7 indicating acceptable reliability. Confidence intervals (CI) of these indices were calculated by bootstrapping estimates of 2000 bootstrap samples. In the correlation analysis, each resident was considered as a unique observation. To standardize discriminability across subcompetencies or examinations, we standardized the scores of the Milestones, written examinations, and OSCE in the academic year 2021 to 2022 using the Z-score. This score measured the distance of an individual’s score from the mean score of all those undergoing the same evaluation in standard deviation units. The correlation was assessed by the Pearson correlation coefficients and 95% CIs in the following 4 comparisons: (1) the average rating of MKTS 1 to 2 and PC 5 to 6, and the average score of 4 written examinations; (2) the average rating of MKTS 3 to 4 and the average score of 5 technical skills stations; (3) the average rating of PC 1 to 4 and the oral test score; and (4) the average rating of ICS 1 to 3 and the score of the SP station. To analyze the differences in correlations among training levels, we conducted a post hoc subgroup analysis in which residents were categorized by their grades, and analyzed Pearson correlations within each grade. A 2-sided P < .05 was considered statistically significant. We did not conduct multiple comparison adjustment in subgroup analyses; hence, these findings were regarded as exploratory results. The statistical analysis was performed using R (version 4.2.1, R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria, 2022) along with the packages DescTools, 16 lavaan, 17 semTools, 18 and irr. 19 RESULTS We received feedbacks from all of the 12 Delphi panel members. In the initial round, they proposed restricting the scope of the “critical care” subcompetency to focus specifically on the “anesthetic management of critically ill patients,” as in China, anesthesia and critical care medicine residency training programs are separate entities, and graduates from anesthesia residency training programs do not engage in critical care medicine. Additionally, they suggested amalgamating the “medical knowledge” and “clinical reasoning” subcompetencies, as well as merging the “regional anesthesia” and “point-of-care ultrasound” subcompetencies, due to the overlapping foundational abilities they assessed. In the subsequent round, they refined the milestone details. For instance, they advised retaining milestones in PROF that were present in the Chinese Milestones system but absent in the ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones, such as milestones related to integrity, honesty, empathy, compliance with the law, and respect for patient privacy within the subcompetency of “professional behavior and ethical principles.” Furthermore, they made revisions to milestones that were deemed to exceed the requirements for residents in China. For instance, the milestone “functions as an expert in an airway crisis for complicated airways” in ACGME Milestones was adjusted to “functions as a consultant in an airway crisis during in-hospital emergency.” Panel members also provided revision to make the language easier to understand. For instance, examples were provided to explain advanced monitors and advanced airway equipment. After 2 rounds, consensus was achieved by 92% of the panel members on all subcompetencies. In the final iteration, a total of 22 subcompetencies were categorized into the 6 competencies of the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones. The distinctions between the Chinese and ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones and explanations of modifications were delineated in Table 1 . The complete Chinese version and English translation were provided in Supplemental Digital Content 1, Supplementary Table 1, http://links.lww.com/AA/F58 . Each subcompetency had 5 milestone levels, accompanied by narrative descriptions for each level. Level 1 was characterized as “critical deficiencies,” and level 5 as “readiness for unsupervised practice.” Ratings were possible at or between levels on a 1 to 10 scale (1: not reaching level 1, 2: equivalent to level 1, 3: between levels 1 and 2, 4: equivalent to level 2... 10: equivalent to level 5). Table 1. Difference Between Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones and ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones 2.0 Chinese version Difference between the ACGME version Competency Abbreviation Subcompetency Professionalism PROF1 Professional behavior and ethical principles Similar, added milestones related to integrity, honesty, empathy, compliance with the law, and respect for patient privacy PROF2 Accountability/ Conscientiousness Similar, added milestones related to conscientious and responsible demeanor at work PROF3 Well-being Similar, added milestones related to resilience to stress PROF4 Patient safety and quality improvement Transited from “Systems-based practice” PROF5 Health care efficiency and cost savings Adapted from “Physician role in health care systems,” and combined with milestones related to care for specific population in “System Navigation for Patient-Centered Care” in “Systems-based practice” Medical knowledge and technical skills MKTS1 Foundational knowledge and clinical reasoning Adapted from 2 distinct subcompetencies: “Foundational knowledge” and “Clinical reasoning” in “Patient care” MKTS2 Application and interpretation of monitors Transited from “Patient care” MKTS3 Airway management Transited from “Patient care” MKTS4 Regional anesthesia and point-of-care ultrasound Adapted from 2 distinct subcompetencies “Regional anesthesia” and “Point-of-care ultrasound” in “Patient care” Patient care PC1 Preanesthetic evaluation Similar PC2 Perioperative care and management Similar PC3 Intraoperative Care Similar, added milestones related to anesthetic record in “Communication within Health Care Systems” in “Interpersonal and Communication Skills” PC4 Situational awareness and crisis management Similar PC5 Postoperative care Similar PC6 Anesthetic management of critically ill patients Adapted from “Critical care” in “Patient Care” PC7 Patient transition and hand-off Adapted from “System navigation for patient-centered care” in “Systems-based practice” Interpersonal and communication skills ICS1 Patient- and family-centered communication Similar ICS2 Interprofessional and team communication Similar ICS3 Coordinated management of medical resources Adapted from “System Navigation for Patient-Centered Care” in “Systems-Based Practice” Teaching ability TA1 Clinical teaching and medical science popularization Added Life-long learning LLL1 Evidence-based and informed practice Transited from “Evidence-Based and Informed Practice” in “Practice-Based Learning and Improvement,” added milestones related to clinical research ability LLL2 Reflective practice and commitment to personal growth Adapted from “Reflective Practice and Commitment to Personal Growth” in “Practice-based learning and improvement” Open in a new tab Abbreviation: ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education. The Milestone ratings and objective examination scores were available and analyzed from a total of 66 residents who were under standardized residency training in the Department of Anesthesiology in our hospital in the year 2021 with 24 (36.4%) in PGY 1, 22 (33.3%) in PGY2, and 20 (30.3%) in PGY3. The average age was 26 ± 2 years. Of them, 18 (26.9%) were males and 48 (73.1%) were females. The average ratings given by 5 raters of each subcompetency among residents in different PGYs were described in Table 2 . Table 2. Chinese Version of Anesthesiology Milestones of Residents in Each Postgraduate Year and Kendall’s Coefficient of Concordance Competency Subcompetency Ratings (mean ± standard deviation) a Kendall’s coefficient b (N = 66) PGY1 (n = 24) PGY2 (n = 22) PGY3 (n = 20) Estimate 95% CI Professionalism PROF1 5.91 ± 0.42 6.67 ± 1.40 7.42 ± 1.04 0.869 0.854–0.949 PROF2 5.79 ± 0.48 6.34 ± 1.50 7.00 ± 1.32 0.819 0.793–0.930 PROF3 5.28 ± 0.35 6.07 ± 1.05 6.73 ± 0.81 0.854 0.851–0.940 PROF4 5.20 ± 0.53 5.83 ± 1.26 6.90 ± 1.26 0.889 0.881–0.957 PROF5 5.61 ± 0.48 6.59 ± 1.42 7.51 ± 1.11 0.864 0.856–0.945 Medical knowledge and technical skills MKTS1 5.52 ± 0.64 6.35 ± 1.10 7.97 ± 1.36 0.942 0.934–0.982 MKTS2 4.88 ± 0.47 5.50 ± 0.73 6.26 ± 0.79 0.843 0.842–0.932 MKTS3 5.13 ± 0.46 5.70 ± 0.82 7.04 ± 0.75 0.896 0.896–0.956 MKTS4 4.52 ± 0.43 5.11 ± 0.64 5.70 ± 0.41 0.799 0.793–0.918 Patient care PC1 5.64 ± 0.52 6.50 ± 1.03 7.70 ± 0.91 0.903 0.896–0.962 PC2 4.97 ± 0.27 5.61 ± 0.83 6.76 ± 0.61 0.879 0.879–0.950 PC3 5.41 ± 0.50 6.15 ± 1.11 7.54 ± 0.89 0.909 0.909–0.963 PC4 5.01 ± 0.61 5.80 ± 1.11 7.18 ± 0.94 0.908 0.904–0.965 PC5 5.16 ± 0.52 5.79 ± 1.05 7.07 ± 0.95 0.907 0.904–0.963 PC6 4.62 ± 0.47 5.43 ± 1.24 6.88 ± 1.04 0.898 0.890–0.959 PC7 4.89 ± 0.60 5.42 ± 1.10 6.58 ± 0.96 0.889 0.883–0.955 Interpersonal and communication skills ICS1 5.24 ± 0.46 6.13 ± 1.03 7.23 ± 0.93 0.870 0.856–0.949 ICS2 5.24 ± 0.52 5.89 ± 1.15 6.90 ± 1.05 0.859 0.845–0.956 ICS3 4.42 ± 0.49 5.24 ± 1.12 6.36 ± 1.06 0.889 0.880–0.955 Teaching ability TA1 5.65 ± 0.50 6.14 ± 1.28 7.12 ± 1.13 0.858 0.858–0.937 Life-long learning LLL1 5.17 ± 0.80 5.64 ± 1.65 6.48 ± 1.63 0.891 0.881–0.956 LLL2 5.08 ± 0.80 5.48 ± 1.61 6.22 ± 1.62 0.899 0.886–0.967 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: CI, confidence interval; ICS, interpersonal and communication skills; LLL, life-long learning; MKTS, medical knowledge and technical skills; PC, patient care; PGY, postgraduate year; PROF, professionalism; TA, Teaching ability. a Average ratings given by the 5 raters. b Kendall’s coefficient of concordance of 5 raters. The Kendall’s coefficient of concordance among the 5 raters ranged from 0.799 (95% CI, 0.793–0.918] to 0.942 (95% CI, 0.934–0.982) of each subcompetency, indicating a good interrater reliability (Table 2 ). Among the 330 evaluations (5 evaluations per resident), the KMO index was 0.98 and the Bartlett’s test of sphericity was significant ( P < .001), indicating the suitability for CFA. The fit indices of the CFA framework were as follows: χ 2 / degree of freedom 2.50 ( P < .001), RMSEA 0.067, SRMR 0.023, GFI 0.964, CFI 0.958, TLI 0.950, NFI 0.939, and IFI 0.953. Despite the significant χ 2 test that might be affected by the sample size, the other indices showed an acceptable goodness-of-fit. The ranges of factor loadings, AVE, CR, and Cronbach’s α coefficient were 0.854 to 0.966, 0.782 to 0.920, 0.935 to 0.980, and 0.916 to 0.978, respectively (Table 3 ). All these indices were >0.7, suggesting satisfactory construct validity and internal consistency. Table 3. Reliability and Validity of the Chinese version of Anesthesiology Milestones in Confirmatory Factor Analysis (N = 330) Competency Sub- competency Rating a Factor loading AVE CR Cronbach’s α Estimate 95% CI Estimate 95% CI Estimate 95% CI Professionalism PROF1 6.6 ± 1.2 0.910 0.826 0.796–0.849 0.960 0.951–0.966 0.959 0.950–0.964 PROF2 6.4 ± 1.3 0.909 PROF3 6.0 ± 1.0 0.854 PROF4 5.9 ± 1.3 0.938 PROF5 6.5 ± 1.3 0.932 Medical knowledge and technical skills MKTS1 6.6 ± 1.5 0.872 0.782 0.753–0.807 0.935 0.924–0.944 0.916 0.900–0.928 MKTS2 5.5 ± 0.9 0.883 MKTS3 5.9 ± 1.0 0.917 MKTS4 5.1 ± 0.7 0.865 Patient care PC1 6.6 ± 1.2 0.943 0.870 0.849–0.888 0.980 0.975–0.982 0.978 0.974–0.981 PC2 5.7 ± 1.0 0.876 PC3 6.3 ± 1.2 0.955 PC4 6.0 ± 1.3 0.948 PC5 5.9 ± 1.2 0.944 PC6 5.6 ± 1.3 0.950 PC7 5.6 ± 1.1 0.910 Interpersonal and communication skills ICS1 6.2 ± 1.2 0.923 0.868 0.843–0.888 0.952 0.942–0.959 0.951 0.941–0.959 ICS2 6.0 ± 1.2 0.937 ICS3 5.3 ± 1.2 0.934 Teaching ability TA1 6.3 ± 1.2 / / / / / / Life-long learning LLL1 5.7 ± 1.4 0.952 0.920 0.897–0.939 0.958 0.946–0.968 0.958 0.945–0.968 LLL2 5.6 ± 1.4 0.966 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: AVE, average variance extracted; CI, confidence interval; CR, composite reliability; ICS, interpersonal and communication skills; LLL, life-long learning; MKTS, medical knowledge and technical skills; PC, patient care; PROF, professionalism; TA, Teaching ability. a Ratings were expressed as mean ± standard deviation. Among the 66 residents, the correlation between objective examinations and related Milestone subcompetencies were as follows: written examinations: r = 0.52 (95% CI, 0.22–0.71), technical skills stations: r = 0.51 (95% CI, 0.21–0.71), the oral test station: r = 0.66 (95% CI, 0.45–0.79), the SP station: r = 0.61 (95% CI, 0.36–0.76) (Table 4 ). When categorizing residents by grades, we found that the correlations were strongest in PGY2: written examinations: r = 0.66 (95% CI, 0.16–0.86), technical skills stations: r = 0.77 (95% CI, 0.43–0.90), the oral test station: r = 0.66 (95% CI, 0.17–0.86), the SP station: r = 0.68 (95% CI, 0.22–0.87) (Table 4 ). Table 4. Correlation Between the Chinese Version of Anesthesiology Milestones and Objective Examinations Comparison Milestone Objective examinations Pearson correlation coefficient Raw score a Standardized score b Raw score a Standardized score b Estimate 95% CI All residents (n = 66) With written examinations 4.5 ± 1.0 0 ± 0.91 73.5 ± 6.0 0 ± 0.67 0.52 0.22–0.71 With technical skills stations 4.5 ± 0.9 0 ± 0.95 39.4 ± 1.8 0 ± 0.72 0.51 0.21–0.71 With oral test station 5.1 ± 1.2 0 ± 0.96 30.0 ± 5.0 0 ± 1.00 0.66 0.45–0.79 With SP station 4.9 ± 1.2 0 ± 0.97 40.6 ± 3.1 0 ± 1.00 0.61 0.36–0.76 PGY-1 residents (n = 24) With written examinations 3.8 ± 0.4 0 ± 0.71 72.7 ± 5.0 0 ± 0.66 0.25 –0.37 to 0.64 With technical skills stations 3.8 ± 0.5 0 ± 0.91 39.1 ± 1.2 0 ± 0.60 0.30 –0.68 to 0.70 With oral test station 4.2 ± 0.5 0 ± 0.84 28.9 ± 4.5 0 ± 1.00 0.45 –0.26 to 0.76 With SP station 4.0 ± 0.5 0 ± 0.87 39.4 ± 2.8 0 ± 1.00 0.24 –0.85 to 0.68 PGY-2 residents (n = 22) With written examinations 4.4 ± 0.9 0 ± 0.93 73.2 ± 7.2 0 ± 0.70 0.66 0.16–0.86 With technical skills stations 4.4 ± 0.7 0 ± 0.94 39.4 ± 2.3 0 ± 0.79 0.77 0.43–0.90 With oral test station 5.0 ± 1.0 0 ± 0.94 29.1 ± 6.0 0 ± 1.00 0.66 0.17–0.86 With SP station 4.8 ± 1.1 0 ± 0.97 40.4 ± 2.5 0 ± 1.00 0.68 0.22–0.87 PGY-3 residents (n = 20) With written examinations 5.5 ± 0.8 0 ± 0.84 74.7 ± 5.6 0 ± 0.69 0.58 –0.23 to 0.88 With technical skills stations 5.4 ± 0.5 0 ± 0.88 39.8 ± 1.9 0 ± 0.83 0.77 0.41–0.91 With oral test station 6.4 ± 0.9 0 ± 0.94 32.0 ± 3.8 0 ± 1.00 0.44 –0.37 to 0.79 With SP station 5.9 ± 0.9 0 ± 0.95 42.3 ± 3.5 0 ± 1.00 0.58 –0.10 to 0.84 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; PGY, postgraduate year; SP, standardized patient. a For each resident, raw scores were calculated as follows: the average ratings of related Milestone subcompetencies, the average score of the 4 written examinations, the average score of the 5 technical skill stations, the score of the oral test station, and the score of the SP station. The full scores for the written examinations, technical skill stations, oral test, and SP station were 100, 50, 50, and 50, respectively. b Standardized scores for each resident were computed by subtracting the mean score of all residents enrolled in the same evaluation from the resident’s raw score and then dividing by the standard deviation. In the subgroup analysis, residents were categorized based on their grades, and standardized scores were calculated within each grade. Scores were expressed as mean ± standard deviation. DISCUSSION In this cross-sectional study, we found that the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestone demonstrated satisfactory interrater reliability, internal consistency, construct validity, and correlation with objective examination scores in our institution. These findings supported the utility of this Milestones system in assessing Anesthesiology-specific competency acquisition in China. To the best of our knowledge, this was the first study to report the development of Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones. Given the considerable differences in medical practice between China and the United States, the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones should not only be a simple literal translation of the ACGME version. For example, aligning with the Chinese Resident Core Competency Milestone Evaluation System, 4 we disaggregated the SBP competency and reassigned its subcompetencies into PROF and PC, as the concept of SBP was ambiguous within Chinese culture. In addition, given the fact that a large percentage of doctors work in university-affiliated hospitals after their residency training in China, TA was separated from ICS as an individual competency. After these adjustments, it was essential to assess whether the language was comprehensible and whether the items were logically organized. Our study provided robust evidence regarding these issues. The high interrater reliability suggests that clear and unambiguous descriptions of the Milestones facilitated consistent interpretation by raters. Moreover, all subcompetencies were strongly correlated with their respective competencies in the CFA (Table 3 ), indicating that our modifications to the ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones System led to a coherent internal structure. Therefore, both residents and faculty members can have confidence that Milestone ratings offer formative feedback on a resident’s progress in each subcompetency and reflect a program’s comprehensive training across all competencies. Furthermore, as one of the leading residency training programs in China, we provided regular Milestone ratings, written examinations and OSCEs for anesthesia residents. The integrity and accuracy of these data enabled us to analyze the correlation between different competencies and related objective examinations separately. We found that the scores of written examinations and oral tests were correlated with the related Milestone ratings in MKTS, PC, and ICS (r > 0.5, Table 4 ). These findings were consistent with previous studies assessing the ACGME Milestones or other clinical performance assessments of Anesthesiology, Obstetrics and Gynecology, Emergency Medicine, and Internal Medicine. 20 – 25 Interestingly, the correlation between the Milestone ratings and objective examination scores was not very strong, indicating that each has unique advantages, and they cannot be substituted for each other. Objective examinations were conducted in simulated standardized clinical situations and could be deliberately prepared. In contrast, Milestone ratings were assigned primarily based on residents’ behavior in daily work, and thus reflected their natural state. It is worth noting that the correlation in PGY2 was stronger than in PGY1 or PGY3. This may be because PGY1 residents spent most of the year rotating through other departments and were therefore unfamiliar to faculty. Conversely, PGY3 residents were highly accustomed to the objective examination formats, which may have hindered the discriminability of the examinations. Despite the strengths mentioned above, our study had the following limitations. First, we analyzed data from a limited number of residents from a single institution, which may not represent all the anesthesia residents across China. Given the promising findings of our study, a multi-center validity study including a diverse cohort of anesthesia residents is warranted in the future. Second, despite our efforts in the quality control of our objective examinations, they were not provincial or nationwide standardized objective examinations, which may further impact our generalizability. Third, we were unable to analyze the correlation between PROF, TA, and LLL ratings and objective examination scores, as our center lacked objective examinations that assessed these specific competencies. This underscored the importance of developing Anesthesiology Milestones, which offered a detailed description of residents’ progress in competencies that were difficult to quantify through objective examinations. Finally, the Milestone ratings of our residents were relatively lower than the ratings of anesthesia residents in the United States reported in previous studies. 26 , 27 There were 3 possible reasons. First, residents under training were not allowed to independently deliver anesthesia care according to Chinese law. Therefore, faculty lacked opportunities to evaluate whether residents reached this level of unsupervised practice. This might explain why most of our PGY3 residents did not reach this level. This gap could be addressed by assessing residents’ behavior in simulation training, during which PGY-3 residents played roles as attending anesthesiologists. Second, the duration of anesthesiology residency training is 3 years in China, which is shorter than the 4-year duration in the United States. Actually, a significant number of anesthesia residents in China continue to work under supervision for more than 1 year after they completed from the residency training program, before being their promotion to attending physicians. Third, the Milestones system has seldom been used among anesthesia programs in China before. Some subcompetencies, though important, might not receive enough training. For example, many faculty members in our center, except for CCC members, were unfamiliar with the requirements of reflective practice or coordinated management of medical resources. Therefore, they did not deliberately cultivate or assess these subcompetencies when supervising residents, possibly leading to low ratings in these subcompetencies. We hope this article can improving this situation by providing faculty with a comprehensive and deep understanding of Anesthesiology Milestones, and further enhances clinical teaching for residents in China. In conclusion, our research represented a pioneering endeavor in creating a structured and systematic competency framework tailored to the needs of Chinese anesthesia residents, aligning with the current postgraduation medical education situation in the field of anesthesia in China. This framework stands poised to aid clinical educators and residents in navigating incremental training objectives, thereby augmenting the caliber of anesthesia residency training nationwide. Furthermore, our findings presented a blueprint for adapting the ACGME Anesthesiology Milestones to diverse international contexts, facilitating broader adoption of the Milestone paradigm. With the continuous progress of medical practice, the Chinese Milestones system also needs to be constantly adjusted. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We extend our gratitude to Dr Ai Hu, Dr Yifei Zhao, Dr Di Jin, and Dr Jing Zhan from the Department of Anesthesiology, Peking Union Medical College Hospital, for their valuable feedback during the revision of the Chinese Anesthesiology Milestones. DISCLOSURES Conflicts of Interest: None. Funding: This work was supported by National Natural Science Foundation of China (72304281), Peking Union Medical Foundation (XHZJ2407), Beijing Resident Standardized Training Quality Improvement Project (2021041), and Teaching Reform Project of Undergraduate Course in Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences (2021zlgc0115). This manuscript was handled by: Edward C. Nemergut, MD. Supplementary Material ane-141-190-s001.pdf (682.7KB, pdf) Open in a new tab Footnotes Reprints will not be available from the authors. Conflicts of Interest, Funding: Please see DISCLOSURES at the end of this article. Supplemental digital content is available for this article. Direct URL citations appear in the printed text and are provided in the HTML and PDF versions of this article on the journal’s website. X. Ruan and X. Xu contributed equally to this work. REFERENCES 1. Nasca TJ, Philibert I, Brigham T, Flynn TC. The next GME accreditation system—rationale and benefits. N Engl J Med. 2012;366:1051–1056. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. McLean S, Edgar L, Harsy B. The milestones 2.0 development process. J Grad Med Educ. 2021;13:4–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ambardekar AP, Walker KK, McKenzie-Brown AM, et al. The anesthesiology milestones 2.0: an improved competency-based assessment for residency training. Anesth Analg. 2021;133:353–361. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Hospitals CCoET. Establishment of the resident core competency milestone evaluation system in China consortium of elite teaching hospitals. Med J Peking Union Med College Hosp. 2022;13:7. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Zhang C, Wang S, Li H, Su F, Huang Y, Mi W; Chinese Anaesthesiology Department Tracking Collaboration Group. Anaesthesiology in China: a cross-sectional survey of the current status of anaesthesiology departments. Lancet Reg Health West Pac. 2021;12:100166. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Shan D. China’s bewildering medical education pathways. Lancet. 2023;401:999–1000. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. ACGME. Clinical competency committees: a guidebook for programs. Accessed November 1, 2024. https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/acgmeclinical-competencycommitteeguidebook.pdf . [ Google Scholar ] 8. Kendall MG, Smith BB. The problem of m rankings. Ann Math Stat. 1939;10:275–287. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Cronbach LJ. Coefficient alpha and the internal structure of tests. Psychometrika. 1951;16:297–334. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Schreiber J, Nora A, Stage F, Barlow E, King J. Reporting structural equation modeling and confirmatory factor analysis results: a review. J Educ Res. 2006;99:323–338. [ Google Scholar ] 11. Cole DA. Utility of confirmatory factor analysis in test validation research. J Consult Clin Psychol. 1987;55:584–594. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Hu L, Bentler PM. Cutoff criteria for fit indexes in covariance structure analysis: Conventional criteria versus new alternatives. Struct Equation Modeling: A Multidiscip J. 1999;6:1–55. [ Google Scholar ] 13. Sivo S, Fan X, Witta E, Willse J. The search for “optimal” cutoff properties: fit index criteria in structural equation modeling. J Exp Educ. 2006;74:267–288. [ Google Scholar ] 14. Carlson K, Herdman A. Understanding the impact of convergent validity on research results. Organizational Res Methods. 2012;15:17–32. [ Google Scholar ] 15. Peterson RA, Kim Y. On the relationship between coefficient alpha and composite reliability. J Appl Psychol. 2013;98:194–198. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Signorell A, Aho K, Alfons A, et al. DescTools: tools for descriptive statistics. Accessed November 1, 2023. https://andrisignorell.github.io/DescTools/ . [ Google Scholar ] 17. Rosseel Y. lavaan: an R package for structural equation modeling. J Stat Softw. 2012;48:1–36. [ Google Scholar ] 18. Jorgensen T, Pornprasertmanit S, Schoemann A, et al. semTools: useful tools for structural equation modeling. Accessed November 1, 2023. https://github.com/simsem/semTools/wiki . [ Google Scholar ] 19. Gamer M, Lemon J. Various coefficients of interrater reliability and agreement. Accessed November 1, 2023. https://www.r-project.org . [ Google Scholar ] 20. Baker K, Sun H, Harman A, Poon KT, Rathmell JP. Clinical performance scores are independently associated with the American Board of Anesthesiology certification examination scores. Anesth Analg. 2016;122:1992–1999. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Bienstock JL, Shivraj P, Yamazaki K, et al. Correlations between Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Obstetrics and Gynecology Milestones and American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology qualifying examination scores: an initial validity study. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2021;224:308.e1–308.e25. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Hamstra SJ, Cuddy MM, Jurich D, et al. Exploring the association between USMLE scores and ACGME milestone ratings: a validity study using national data from emergency medicine. Acad Med. 2021;96:1324–1331. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Hauer KE, Vandergrift J, Lipner RS, Holmboe ES, Hood S, McDonald FS. National internal medicine milestone ratings: validity evidence from longitudinal three-year follow-up. Acad Med. 2018;93:1189–1204. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Hauer KE, Vandergrift J, Hess B, et al. Correlations between ratings on the resident annual evaluation summary and the internal medicine milestones and association with ABIM certification examination scores among US Internal Medicine Residents, 2013-2014. JAMA. 2016;316:2253–2262. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Kimbrough MK, Thrush CR, Barrett E, Bentley FR, Sexton KW. Are surgical milestone assessments predictive of in-training examination scores? J Surg Educ. 2018;75:29–32. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Tanaka P, Park YS, Roby J, et al. Milestone learning trajectories of residents at five anesthesiology residency programs. Teach Learn Med. 2021;33:304–313. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Ross FJ, Metro DG, Beaman ST, et al. A first look at the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education anesthesiology milestones: implementation of self-evaluation in a large residency program. J Clin Anesth. 2016;32:17–24. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',482,'2025-10-15 11:10:06','2025-10-15 11:10:06');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5344,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12317895','Return on investment of rapid ICU workforce upskilling: an economic and cost-effectiveness analysis.','Ebm C, Istrate M, Van Gelder F, Szőllősi GJ, Alexandre J, Azoulay E, Kesecioglu J, Cecconi M.','2025','upskilling c19_space upskilling qahw upskilling upskilling upskilling upskilling reconfigure reallocating upskilling upskilling c19_space upskill upskilling upskilling misallocation mismanagement overstretched upskilling c19_space upskilling misallocation c19_space c19_space c19_space vre qahw qahw conducing upskilled outlays diverting strained pessimistic triangular c19_space ppl qahw qahw qahw upskilling recouped upskilling upskilling c19_space c19_space upskilling centralize diverted upskilling upskilling geopolitical upskilling upskilling c19_space qahw gjs maurizio balcom gulliver witherspoon ckab165 hick hanfling cantrill grasselli pesenti ranney griffeth e41 nejmp2006141 hettle allanby bakewell dhonye handford upton vilenchik skilling e41 e44 fhj camilleri jeans monkhouse chisnall vindrola padros e050038 horney ricchetti masterson pdm fueling s10900 schaller mellinhgoff c19_space c19space szollosi istrate duska schaller boulanger mellinghoff waldauf girbes arj derde waele kesecioglu c19_space hippe umoren bresnahan tolsgaard tabor wulff dyre ringsted nørgaard thusini milenova nahabedian soukup moffatt hefner mekhjian mcalearney latimer scheck mcalearney medcal villafuerte zamora towse linearities alloc s12962 zamora chalkidou cubi molla leech towse s40258 sculpher claxton jval reinap piggott laidmae pold falavigna gibbens neamtiu parmelli jameleddine pyke verstijnen tugwell saz kuchenmuller moja e378 e390 caky offiah ekpotu sharifuddin adk condron georgiadou standby gjplr vol11n517 7a7afb35 accessed20june2025 healthcare_personnel_statistics_ _physicians sohrabi aalaa systematized jamp healthcare_personnel_statistics_ _nursing_and_caring_professionals source_data_for_tables_and_graphs waterlow jombart procter kapinos hohmann podichetty e2350145 edbrooke bjaceaccp mkl030','Abstract Purpose Although healthcare crises are infrequent, they may place extraordinary stress on Intensive Care Units (ICUs), often exposing critical weaknesses in workforce planning and resulting in acute staffing shortages. This study presents a comprehensive economic evaluation of large-scale, rapid ICU workforce upskilling as a strategic response to such pressures. Specifically, we assess the cost-effectiveness, economic sustainability, and resilience-building potential of these interventions during crisis conditions. Methods C19_SPACE, a Europe-wide upskilling initiative led by the European Society of Intensive Care Medicine (ESICM), was implemented across 24 countries between 2020 and 2021. A societal economic evaluation and return on investment (ROI) was calculated through deterministic modeling and validated using probabilistic sensitivity analysis across a range of plausible scenarios, including variations in patient throughput, training efficacy, and healthcare system parameters. Results The total societal investment in the program was €20.1 million, translating to an average cost of €1146 per participant and €1720 per Quality-Adjusted Healthcare Worker (QAHW). Deterministic modeling estimated an ROI of 478%, with program costs fully recovered in just 5.1 days. Probabilistic sensitivity analysis confirmed the robustness of these findings, with a mean ROI of 455% (95% CI 130–1029%) and a median break-even point of 5.4 days (95% CI 2.66–13.04 days). Conclusion Rapid, structured ICU workforce upskilling initiatives deliver substantial economic returns and significantly expands healthcare capacity. Strategic investment in emergency workforce upskilling is economically sound and crucial for healthcare system resilience during future crises. Keywords: Economic evaluation, Crisis management, Cost-effectiveness, Workforce training, Upskilling Take-home message This study provides an economic evaluation of a large-scale ICU upskilling program implemented in a crisis context. While prior research has primarily examined clinical outcomes or training effectiveness, this analysis quantifies financial returns and system-level benefits, offering empirical evidence for rapid workforce training as a strategic investment. The findings equip policymakers with actionable insights on cost-effectiveness and resource prioritization in emergency preparedness and response. Open in a new tab Introduction Critical care systems are frequently tested by large-scale crises that generate a sudden and sustained increase in demand for intensive care services [ 1 ]. Whether driven by infectious disease outbreaks, environmental disasters, or other high-impact events, such scenarios expose structural vulnerabilities and stretch intensive care unit (ICU) capacity to its limits [ 2 ]. In these contexts, system resilience depends not only on material resources but also on the ability to rapidly mobilize and reconfigure the healthcare workforce to meet emerging needs [ 3 ]. Conventional strategies for crisis response have largely focused on logistical measures—such as increasing ICU bed numbers, repurposing wards, and reallocating equipment [ 4 ]. However, these efforts are often constrained by a more fundamental bottleneck: the availability of skilled personnel. Even in high-income countries, the number of physicians and nurses with critical care expertise is limited and cannot be easily expanded through traditional workforce pipelines, which rely on lengthy, resource-intensive training pathways [ 5 ]. To address this challenge, healthcare systems have explored flexible workforce models that support rapid deployment in times of crisis [ 5 , 6 ]. One approach involves the targeted upskilling of non-ICU professionals—physicians and nurses from other specialties—who can be temporarily integrated into critical care teams [ 7 ]. This strategy aims to enhance surge capacity while trying to maintain continuity of care. Importantly, it represents a broader shift from reactive measures to proactive preparedness, embedding upskilling as a core element of health system resilience. A prominent example of this approach is the COVID-19 Skills Preparation Course for Europe (C19_SPACE), funded by the European Union (EU) Commission, and developed by the European Society of Intensive Care Medicine (ESICM). Designed for broad implementation across multiple countries, the program provided standardized, competency-based training to upskill non-ICU healthcare professionals, enabling them to effectively support critical care services during periods of heightened demand [ 8 , 9 ]. While the clinical rationale for such programs is increasingly accepted, the economic impact of large-scale, rapid-response upskilling initiatives remains poorly characterized. Implementing such programs requires significant investment—both direct costs (e.g., materials, coordination, and personnel) and indirect costs (e.g., time away from clinical duties). Previous estimates of critical care upskilling programs suggest wide variability in per-person expenditures, ranging from €3100 to €24,000 depending on intensity and delivery model [ 10 , 11 ]. Given these significant expenditures, understanding the financial efficiency and return-on-investment (ROI) of such initiatives is essential. In contrast to standard healthcare investments, crisis-driven interventions demand heightened efficiency; resource misallocation or mismanagement not only results in financial losses but also risks compromising patient care and exacerbating pressures on an already overstretched healthcare system [ 6 ]. Consequently, systematic economic evaluation of crisis-driven upskilling initiatives is essential to optimize resource allocation, manage financial constraints effectively, and safeguard care quality [ 12 ]. By accurately assessing the cost-effectiveness and economic impacts such as the ROI of such programs, healthcare systems can minimize waste, maintain operational effectiveness, and prevent additional strain on limited healthcare personnel [ 13 ]. This study provides a comprehensive economic evaluation of the C19_SPACE program, examining its direct and indirect costs, resource utilization, and broader system impact. By integrating clinical and economic perspectives, this study provides data-driven insights into the cost-effectiveness of the crisis response strategy. The findings aim to guide policymakers in optimizing resource allocation for rapid large-scale workforce training, ensuring that such upskilling initiatives deliver maximum value with minimal risk of misallocation. Methodology Economic analysis framework This study aims to evaluate the economic impact of the C19_SPACE program using a comprehensive, three-step approach that balances in-depth analysis with the limitations imposed by data availability. Our methodology integrates cost analysis, cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA), and scenario-based simulations to assess financial efficiency and ROI. The analysis utilizes data from the C19_SPACE program, which provided training to 17,494 professionals; 12,086 of these participants were certified, with support from 2400 trainers. Competency test scores increased from a pre-training average of 63.1% to a post-training average of 83.6% (p < 0.005) [ 8 ]. Phase 1: cost analysis The first phase of the evaluation focuses on determining the cost per trained healthcare professional, aimed to provide an understanding of the program’s financial efficiency. The cost of the training program was calculated by incorporating both direct and indirect costs, as detailed in Tables 1 and 2 . Table 1. Input parameters derived from the C19_SPACE study, including all course design, implementation, and opportunity costs* (defined as time spent away from clinical duties) Category Parameter Value (range) Training program budget Training development cost Management coordination IT PLATFORM (SETUP & MAINTENANCE) Virtual reality environment (VRE) content development 3D glasses: purchase & transport Video lectures €2,485,050 Training implementation Faculty time per training event 10 h Number of faculty members 75 people Training time per participant 12 h Trainees Total trainees Doctors trained Nurses trained 17,494 people 8213 people 9281 people Open in a new tab Table 2. Input parameter taken from international literature; when data were not available expert opinion was obtained Value Min Max References Knowledge status 0.82 0.63 1.00 [ 8 ] Working hours per week (hours) 40 36 48 [ 21 ] FTE (full-time equivalent) shifts per month 22 20 24 [ 21 ] Salary of a healthcare worker (HCW) per hour (€) 60 35 150 [ 22 ] Patients per doctor ratio 8 6 10 [ 23 ] Patients per nurse ratio 2 1 4 [ 24 , 25 ] ICU length of stay (LOS) 8 5 13 [ 26 ] Cost of ICU stay ICU patient 12,500 5500 18,000 [ 27 , 28 ] Bed utilization rate 75% 60% 85% [ 22 ] Expert opinion Open in a new tab Phase 2: cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA) Cost analysis in isolation is insufficient to capture the full value of the program, as its intended impact lies in improving patient care. Given the ethical constraints inherent to crisis settings, direct measurement of patient outcomes was not recorded. To address this limitation, we developed the Quality-Adjusted Healthcare Worker (QAHW) index—defined as a healthcare professional attaining ≥ 80% proficiency in ICU-relevant skills, as determined by expert consensus—to serve as a proxy for training effectiveness. The cost per QAHW was subsequently calculated, providing a pragmatic estimate of cost-effectiveness in the absence of direct outcome data. Phase 3: return-on-investment supported by scenario-based simulations In Phase 3, we refine the analysis conducing an ROI with scenario-based simulations that estimate the program’s impact based on intermediate outcome related to increased capacity. By combining empirical data from the study with published international estimates, we develop a model for the post-training clinical phase. Key metrics include the increase in patient treatment capacity—reflecting the ability to manage a higher patient volume through an expanded and upskilled workforce—and the corresponding rise in ICU capacity, measured by the number of additional beds that could be safely staffed and operated. Although a significant number of physicians were trained, the primary limitation in scaling ICU capacity stemmed from nursing availability—particularly in light of shift scheduling and nurse-to-bed ratio requirements. Consequently, despite physicians comprising 50% of the participants, our core calculations focused on nursing resources. Return on investment (ROI) The ROI is calculated as the net profit from the investment divided by its initial cost ROI % ∗ = Monetary Benefit - Investment Costs / Investment Costs × 100 . *An ROI (%) greater than 1 indicates a positive return, while an ROI less than 1 indicates a negative return. All costs were reported in Euro (EUR), and no currency conversion was necessary, as all financial data originated from institutions and sources within the European Union. The chosen time horizon for the analysis was 1-month post-training. This short timeframe was selected to capture immediate outcomes, including direct implementation costs, workforce availability, and operational adjustments within healthcare institutions. Although short term, the analysis also reflects early indicators of medium-term benefits—such as improved workforce capacity and enhanced system resilience—which are essential in evaluating the initial return-on-investment of the training program. Given the 1-month analysis period, discounting of costs and outcomes was not necessary. Input data Direct costs These include implementation costs, equipment costs, personnel costs, and operating costs. We used published international literature to estimate hourly wages, workforce capacity (full-time equivalent (FTE)), and staffing ratios. The economic value generated per patient was estimated by assuming that the average cost incurred to treat a patient reflects the economic value attributed to the patient’s full stay. Tables 1 and 2 summarize the input parameter and assumptions used in the model. Indirect costs and opportunity costs From a societal perspective, we account for indirect costs, including opportunity costs—the value of healthcare professionals’ time spent in training rather than providing clinical care. Although opportunity costs do not involve direct financial outlays, they represent a significant societal investment, particularly in programs where time is the primary resource. Including these costs captures the full value of mobilized human capital, crucial during the pandemic when diverting physicians strained already limited resources. We calculated opportunity costs by multiplying trainees’ hourly compensation by the time spent attending and preparing for the training. Capacity calculation Capacity and throughput were estimated based on key assumptions (and tested within their min–max range): each newly trained nurse works 48 h per week, totaling approximately 22 shifts per month, with a bed-to-nurse ratio of 2:1. We calculated monthly “nurse shifts”, multiplied this by the patient-to-nurse ratio to determine total “patient shifts”, and then divided by the required shifts per patient to estimate the number of patients managed per nurse per month. Sensitivity and uncertainty analysis Deterministic scenario analysis To establish a clear and interpretable foundation for evaluating economic implications, we initiated the analysis using a deterministic framework. This approach allows for the direct comparison of predefined scenarios based on fixed input values, offering transparent “what-if” benchmarks for stakeholders and decision-makers. Three scenarios were defined to reflect likely, optimistic, and pessimistic operational environments derived from explicit values, as highlighted in Table 2 : Scenario I (probable): 75% bed utilization, 8-day length of stay, 2 patients per nurse, €12,500 cost per patient Scenario II (best case): 85% utilization, 5-day stay, 4 patients per nurse, €18,000 value per patient Scenario III (worst case): 60% utilization, 13-day stay, 1 patient per nurse, €5500 value per patient Probabilistic sensitivity analysis To complement the point estimates derived from the deterministic framework and better capture real-world variability, we conducted a probabilistic sensitivity analysis using Monte Carlo simulation with 10,000 iterations. This methodology facilitates robust estimation under uncertainty by incorporating variability in the key input parameters. Specifically, we modeled the length of stay (LOS) in ICU, patient-to-nurse ratios, training costs, and per-patient ICU costs using triangular probability distributions drawn from literature. This probabilistic approach allows for continuous sampling across the plausible ranges of each input parameter, generating a distribution of potential economic outcomes. Results Participants and outcome Cost analysis Table 3 presents the cost breakdown of the total investment in the training program. From a societal perspective, the overall cost of the program amounted to € 20,055,235. When divided by the number of trainees, this results in a cost of € 1146 per trainee for the full program. Table 3. Cost-effectiveness analysis of the C19_SPACE training program Cost category Cost per person (€) People (Ppl) Hours (h) per person Total Cost (€) Training program budget – – – € 2,485,050 Subject matter experts 250 75 10 € 187,500 Trainer costs 55 2400 14 € 1,848,000 Opportunity costs for trainees Doctors 60 8213 19 € 9,362,820 Nurses 35 9281 19 € 6,171,865 Cost of the training program – 2400 – € 20,055,235 Number of trainees – 17,494 – Cost per trainee – – – € 1146 Cost-effectiveness ANALYSIS Trainees meeting predefined performance standards a 12,086 Cost per quality-adjusted trainee € 1659 Open in a new tab QAHW Quality-Adjusted Healthcare Worker, ROI return on investment a The predefined standards are to achieve a test score of > 80% of all desired tasks and knowledge The introduction of the Quality-Adjusted Healthcare Worker (QAHW) metric allowed for intermediate measurement of training effectiveness, showing that 12,086 trainees met predefined competency standards (≥ 80% test score). The resulting average cost per QAHW was calculated at € 1659. The direct budget allocated to the training development amounted to €2,485,050 (12%), covering core program expenses. Additionally, €187,500 (1%) was dedicated to engaging subject matter experts, while €1,848,000 (9%) covered local trainer compensation. In addition to direct costs, the analysis accounts for the opportunity costs of trainees, estimated at €15,534,685 (77%), representing the economic value of the time trainees spent away from clinical duties during the program. Healthcare capacity and ROI analysis-Base Case analysis Based on previously described capacity calculations, coupled with further assumptions on system efficacy, which are detailed in Table 2 , the program enabled the activation of approximately 2537 additional ICU beds across participating hospitals. With an average ICU length of stay of 8 days, this translated into capacity for 9281 additional patients per month. Applying a standard cost value of €12,500 per ICU admission, the workforce trained by the program generated an estimated societal value of €116 million monthly. With an overall program cost of €20.1 million, these figures correspond to a return-on-investment (ROI) of 478%, with financial break-even achieved just 5.1 days after deployment. Deterministic “What-if” scenario analysis The “probable” scenario showed outcomes closely aligned with the point estimates described in the above mentioned “base case”, with only slight variations resulting from adjustments to staffing variables—which emerged as the most influential factors affecting the final results. In the best-case scenario, the return-on-investment (ROI) was particularly strong, with a break-even point achieved in just over a day, underscoring the high value of the upskilling program. Scenario analysis further demonstrated that even in the worst-case scenario, the initial investment was recouped within just over a month—highlighting that upskilling initiatives can deliver rapid and resilient returns (Table 4 ). Table 4. Deterministic simulation scenario outcomes Scenario Total patients/month Total economic value Monthly ROI Break-even time I. Probable 9,866 123,332,920 € 514% 4.95 days II. Best 23,598 424,764,000 € 2020% 1.4 days III. Worst case 3,310 18,205,000 € –9.2% 33 days Open in a new tab Probabilistic Monte Carlo simulation A probabilistic Monte Carlo simulation was conducted to account for uncertainty in key input parameters. The analysis yielded a mean return-on-investment (ROI) of 455%, with the central 90% of simulated outcomes ranging from 130 (5th percentile) to 1029% (95th percentile). The mean time to break-even was 5.4 days, with 90% of simulations achieving cost recovery between 2.7 and 13.0 days. These results, as seen in Fig. 1 , indicate a consistently favorable economic profile across a wide range of plausible scenarios. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Distribution of the ROI in the probabilistic Monte Carlo Simulation analysis Discussion This study provides robust evidence that large-scale, time-limited upskilling interventions can serve as effective and economically sound responses to healthcare system strain during crises. The C19_SPACE program, implemented at unprecedented speed and scale across 24 European countries, achieved an ROI of 478%, with full program cost recovery in just 5.1 days. Probabilistic modeling further validated these results, showing a mean ROI of 455% (95% CI 130–1029%) and cost neutrality in 90% of simulations within 2 weeks of deployment. In crisis settings, where time and resources are scarce, such outcomes are highly relevant. Policymakers must make swift, evidence-informed decisions about resource allocation. Embedding ROI modeling into program evaluation offers a critical, yet underutilized, tool for identifying high-yield strategies. While prior ICU-related economic studies have largely focused on capital infrastructure—such as ventilators or beds—this analysis brings the healthcare workforce to the forefront, quantifying its economic impact through operational metrics such as patient throughput and system-wide value generation [ 4 ]. The scale of the C19_SPACE program was central to its economic efficiency. Involving thousands of healthcare professionals, the program’s reach enabled fixed costs (e.g., coordination and digital infrastructure) to be distributed across a large base. This reflects both the intense demand for ICU support during crisis periods and the opportunity to mobilize workforce capacity at scale. In contrast to smaller continuing medical education initiatives—where ROI is often modest or even negative [ 14 ] the high-leverage nature of crisis-responsive upskilling directly translated into expanded care delivery and accelerated system performance. Although not directly measured, the strategic choice to centralize virtual content and deliver practical training near clinical sites likely reduced logistical costs and enhanced staff availability. A critical strength of our analysis is the explicit inclusion of opportunity costs, which accounted for 77% of the total program costs. These reflect the substantial value of healthcare professionals’ time diverted from clinical duties—a particularly relevant factor given the program’s rapid scale-up and the need to double training slots to meet unprecedented demand. While often excluded from hospital-based economic evaluations, opportunity costs are essential for capturing the full societal investment required by large-scale public health interventions [ 15 , 16 ]. Excluding opportunity costs would underestimate the true societal investment and fail to reflect the full economic value of large-scale, crisis-responsive upskilling initiatives. Accounting for human capital mobilization provides a more comprehensive, policy-relevant understanding of return-on-investment in healthcare resilience [ 17 ]. The analysis was intentionally designed from a conservative, system-level perspective, focusing exclusively on the contribution of nurses—widely recognized as the principal constraint in ICU capacity expansion across European healthcare systems. This modeling choice aimed to provide a lower-bound estimate of program benefits, reflecting realistic implementation constraints and avoiding overestimation of impact. Under worst-case assumptions, wherein newly trained nurses were assigned a productivity level equivalent to only 10% of a fully trained ICU nurse, the model still demonstrated full cost recovery within 1 month. These results underscore the critical importance of tailoring workforce upskilling strategies to specific system bottlenecks to maximize efficiency and resilience. Beyond the economic metrics, his study underscores the need for integrated evaluation frameworks that capture both financial and systemic dimensions of large-scale health interventions. Sound policy decisions in crisis settings must consider not only feasibility, but also economic viability, scalability, and logistical implementation [ 18 ]. By incorporating all these elements, this analysis offers a practical, data-driven foundation for guiding emergency resource allocation. As future crises—be they pandemics, climate-related events, or geopolitical conflicts—continue to challenge ICU capacity, rapid and strategic workforce development should be viewed not as an ancillary activity, but as a core pillar of preparedness. Investments in human capital, when executed at scale and guided by data, yield not only financial returns but also safeguard the continuity and quality of patient care when it is needed most. Strengths and limitations This study has several methodological strengths. First, it integrates multiple levels of economic evaluation—including cost analysis, cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA), and ROI—offering a comprehensive perspective that extends beyond cost-per-trainee metrics. Second, by adopting a societal perspective, it captures broader economic implications, such as increased hospital throughput and system responsiveness. This aligns with best practices in health economic evaluation and enhances the transferability of findings across settings [ 19 ]. Finally, the use of scenario-based simulation allows for the accommodation of uncertainty inherent in emergency program implementation. Probabilistic Monte Carlo and deterministic scenario analyses confirmed the program’s economic viability even under cautious assumptions such as reduced bed utilization or lower economic value per patient. Several limitations should be acknowledged. Most notably, the analysis lacks direct clinical outcome data—such as patient-level improvements, quality of care indicators, or mortality rates—primarily due to logistical and operational constraints during the emergency implementation phase. As a result, it is not possible to establish direct causal links between the training program and clinical outcomes. While the purpose of this analysis was to evaluate the knowledge gain, future research could aim to link upskilling initiatives with clinical performance metrics using integrated health system data. Furthermore, data on workforce-level effects, particularly in areas such as staff well-being and mental health, were not collected. These dimensions are particularly relevant, as intensive training interventions may influence stress levels, job satisfaction, and burnout—factors that are critical to long-term healthcare system resilience but were beyond the scope of this initial evaluation. Furthermore, the analysis adopts a short-term perspective, likely underestimating the broader and longer-term benefits of the program. These include sustained skill retention, changes in clinical practice, and the diffusion of knowledge among peers, which were not captured in the current model. While our ROI estimates are conservative, evidence from prior studies suggests that the impact of crisis training can persist for up to a year, with measurable improvements in clinical performance [ 20 ]. Future longitudinal studies should evaluate the durability of acquired competencies and their effect on workforce development and patient care. Despite these limitations, our findings offer significant implications for policymakers and healthcare administrators. The demonstrated economic efficiency strongly advocates for strategic investments in emergency healthcare workforce training. Policymakers should incorporate systematic economic evaluations into crisis preparedness frameworks, thereby optimizing resource allocation and fostering greater healthcare system resilience. Conclusion This study provides strong economic evidence supporting the value of crisis-driven upskilling programs. The C19_SPACE initiative demonstrated that a well-structured, large-scale training effort can generate immediate financial and operational benefits, with a rapid ROI and a significant increase in ICU capacity. While emergency training requires a substantial initial investment, the results clearly show that such programs are not only cost-effective but also essential for strengthening healthcare system resilience. Future research should build upon these findings by incorporating longitudinal assessments of skill retention, direct patient outcome data, and comparative analyses across different healthcare settings. Abbreviation ICU Intensive care unit ROI Return on investment CEA Cost-effectiveness analysis QAHW Quality-adjusted healthcare worker FTE Full-time equivalent ESICM European Society of Intensive Care Medicine EUR Euro LOS Length of stay HCW Healthcare worker Author contributions CE, MI, FV, JA and MC made substantial contributions to the conception and design of the study, as well as to the analysis and interpretation of the data. CE and GJS led the statistical analysis, CE took primary responsibility for drafting the manuscript. MC guided the evaluation process. MC, EA, and JK played a central role in conceptualizing the initial study and overseeing its implementation. Their input was instrumental in defining the scientific relevance and informing the design of the economic model. MC, JK, EA, and CE reviewed all revisions and provided in-depth scientific analysis, significantly enhancing the manuscript''s quality. All authors contributed their expertise, critically revised the content, and approved the final version of the manuscript. Funding No financial contribution has been received for this analysis, and the authors report no sponsorship involvement in the research that could have influenced the outcome of this work. Availability of data and materials We used anonymized data, currently stored at the ESICM Data Management system. The datasets used and analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Conflicts of interest No conflicts or competing interests regarding this study have been declared. The authors certify no conflicts of interest with any financial organization regarding the material discussed in the manuscript. Maurizio Cecconi is a Section Editor for Intensive Care Medicine journal. He has not taken part in the review or selection process of this article. Consent for publication Not applicable. Ethical standards This study involved only secondary economic data analysis and did not include human subjects; therefore, ethical review and approval were not required. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Gupta N, Balcom S, Gulliver A, Witherspoon R (2021) Systematic review of health workforce surge capacity during COVID-19 and other viral pandemics. Eur J Public Health. 10.1093/eurpub/ckab165.034 [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hick JL, Hanfling D, Cantrill SV (2012) Allocating scarce resources in disasters: Emergency department principles. Ann Emerg Med 59(3):177–187. 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2011.06.012 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Grasselli G, Pesenti A, Cecconi M (2020) Critical care utilization for the COVID-19 outbreak in Lombardy, Italy: early experience and forecast during an emergency response. JAMA 323(16):1545–1546. 10.1001/jama.2020.4031 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ranney ML, Griffeth V, Jha AK (2020) Critical supply shortages—the need for ventilators and personal protective equipment during the COVID-19 pandemic. N Engl J Med 382:e41. 10.1056/nejmp2006141 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Hettle D, Sutherland K, Miles E, Allanby L, Bakewell Z, Davies D, Dhonye Y, Handford V, Upton R, Vilenchik V, Wood R (2020) Cross-skilling training to support medical redeployment in the COVID-19 pandemic. Future Healthc J 7(3):e41–e44. 10.7861/fhj.2020-0049 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Vera San Juan N, Clark SE, Camilleri M, Jeans JP, Monkhouse A, Chisnall G, Vindrola-Padros C (2022) Training and redeployment of healthcare workers to intensive care units (ICUs) during the COVID-19 pandemic: A systematic review. BMJ Open 12(1):e050038. 10.1136/bmjopen-2021-050038 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Horney JA, Davis MK, Ricchetti-Masterson KL, MacDonald PDM (2014) Fueling the public health workforce pipeline through student surge capacity response teams. J Community Health 39(1):35–39. 10.1007/s10900-013-9750-5 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Schaller SJ, Mellinhgoff J, Cecconi M, on behalf of the C19_Space Taskforce members E (2022