INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5349,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12118615','Practice Variation in Urine Collection Among Emergency Department Providers in Pre-toilet-trained Children With Suspected Urinary Tract Infection.','Wilson LM, Klassen TP, Crombie T, Gangwani A, Wai Lai VK, Gripp K, Doyle E, Beer D, Hogue K, Florescu O, Knisley L, Aregbesola A.','2025','transurethral postmedical suprapubic bagged suprapubic bagged methodologist trekk perc perc winnipeg b350 perc perc perc perc perc postmedical sainte justine newfoundland sainte justine izaak killam halifax janeway pattison saskatoon stollery winnipeg winnipeg suprapubic ctas circumcision transurethral circumcision bagged postmedical bagged hadjipanayis suprapubic suspecting bagged perc methodologist trekk perc rady endowered startup startup lucy lucy ucalgary usask tighe crombie tcrombie cheo apoorva gangwani agangwani chrim veronica veronica umanitoba gripp kgripp elisabete edoyle darcy darcy umanitoba kaitlin hogue khogue cancercare oana florescu oflorescu knisley lknisley chrim aregbesola aregbesola umanitoba morone bost shulhan featherstone westwood etoubleau reveret brouet diviney jaswon tosif j1341 ravichandran farooqi labrosse autmizguine e20160573 crombie slinger barrowman midstream knisley hartling jabbour trekk perc perc cousin ryckewaert jorna lecouvey dineen hadjipanayis hoeffe desjardins zuckerbraun benjamens moumni e75 e81 kharbanda e182430','Abstract Background: Urinary tract infections are a common cause of acute illness among children presenting to the emergency department (ED). Many techniques exist to collect urine specimens in pre–toilet-trained children. There is wide practice variation regarding the most appropriate collection method. This variation also appears to exist across national health organizations and societies. To date, little is known about the extent of practice variation in urine collection methods or the influence of patient and health care provider characteristics on the choice of collection method. Materials and Methods: A cross-sectional survey was designed and comprised of 3 sections: pediatric emergency medicine physician demographics, pediatric ED demographics, and case scenarios designed to assess the context surrounding urine collection method choice. The survey was disseminated to pediatric emergency medicine physicians across Canada from February 2023 to April 2023. A descriptive analysis of the characteristics of pediatric emergency medicine physicians and the EDs in which they worked was performed. Multivariate logistic regression models were used to examine pediatric emergency medicine physicians and ED factors that influence urine collection methods. Results: Of 235 surveys, 96 were returned (41% participation rate). Most respondents were aged 40 to 49 (n=31, 35.6%), female (60.5%), completed residency in Ontario (18.4%) and Quebec (17.2%), and worked at the Children’s Hospital of Eastern Ontario (16%). There was variation in urine collection methods among pediatric emergency medicine physicians with a preference for transurethral catheterization and bladder stimulation versus other methods. Factors such as the length of wait time of patients to be seen in the ED (odds ratio=3.03, 95% CI=1.14-8.09) and year postmedical school (odds ratio=1.67, 95% CI=1.07-2.60) were associated with increased choice of urinary catheterization when selecting a urine collection method. Conclusions: The data suggests there is practice variation in urine collection methods among Canadian pediatric emergency medicine physicians. This practice variation is influenced by both individual providers and the demographics of EDs. Key Words: urinary tract infection, UTI, urine sample, pediatrics, survey Urinary tract infections (UTI) are a common cause of illness in children under 2 years of age. 1 Many of these children present to the emergency department (ED) acutely unwell, with an unexplained fever and other nonspecific symptoms such as vomiting or irritability. The diagnostic workup for these individuals includes obtaining a urine specimen. For individuals who are deemed high risk for having a UTI, a screening test consisting of a urinalysis or urine dipstick can be performed. However, to definitively diagnose a UTI a urine culture must be performed. In children who are not yet toilet trained, there is a high risk for contamination. Therefore, it is important to choose an appropriate urine collection method. There are many techniques for urine specimen collection in this age group, and methods can include invasive methods such as catheterization or suprapubic aspiration, and noninvasive methods including bagged urine or pad sampling. 2 Invasive methods have lower rates of contamination but are more painful for the child and thus more stressful for the parents. 3 – 5 Noninvasive methods are less painful for the child but have higher contamination rates. 5 , 6 In addition, new techniques such as bladder stimulation and lumbar massage are becoming increasingly used in clinical practice. 7 – 10 These methods aim to improve collection effectiveness as well as patient experience. Currently, there appears to be wide practice variation in urine collection choices among pediatricians. This practice variation is also found across national health organizations and societies. The Canadian Paediatric Society (CPS) position statement recommends that catheterization or suprapubic aspiration should be used to diagnose UTIs in pre–toilet-trained children. 11 The use of bagged urine collection is permissible in a screening urinalysis; however, invasive methods must be used to diagnose a UTI. 11 However, little is known to date about the extent or magnitude of practice variation in urine collection methods, or the association between the severity of symptoms and the demographics of patients and how this choice impacts urine collection method choice among Canadian health care practitioners working at a pediatric ED. The aims of this study were (1) to determine if there is variation in the practice of urine collection among pediatric emergency medicine physicians in Canadian Pediatric EDs and (2) to investigate factors influencing the choice of urine collection methods. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study Design A cross-sectional survey was designed using the CHERRIES structure reporting guidelines and with the help of a survey methodologist. 12 The survey was developed with input from physicians who work in the pediatric ED and from members of the Translating Emergency Knowledge for Kids (TREKK) network, a national knowledge mobilization organization in pediatric emergency care. 13 The demographic section was taken from previous surveys disseminated using the Pediatric Emergency Research Canada (PERC) platform ( https://perc-canada.ca/ ). 14 Similar studies which investigated UTIs in pre–toilet-trained children either targeted other provider demographics (eg, outpatient-focused physicians), did not encompass the same scope (eg, only focused on specific collection methods without assessing reasons for these choices), or were predominantly case scenario-based. 15 – 17 Following development, the survey was piloted with a convenience sample of pediatric emergency medicine physicians working at the Winnipeg Children’s Hospital to ensure clarity and practicality of all questions, as well as the survey''s usability. The survey was subsequently refined based on feedback from the pilot study. The final survey was comprised of 3 sections: pediatric emergency medicine physicians demographics, pediatric ED demographics, and a series of case scenarios designed to assess the context surrounding urine collection method choice (see Supplementary file Appendix 1, Supplemental Digital Content 1, http://links.lww.com/PEC/B350 for full survey details). In total, 4 case scenarios were presented, with the first 3 scenarios written to assess urine collection method choice based on increasing severity of illness of the child. The fourth case scenario was a mix of the previous case scenarios and was used to assess whether shared decision-making with parents was used when pediatric emergency medicine physicians were deciding on the urine collection method. Data Collection A convenience sampling was utilized, comprised of physicians working at one of the 15 pediatric EDs in the PERC network. The PERC national database is comprised of ∼235 members. The study was disseminated via Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) to pediatric emergency medicine physicians across Canada from February 2023 to April 2023. PERC members were sent 3 emails inviting them to participate in the survey, and a QR code was also presented at the 2023 PERC Annual Conference. All data was collected and managed using REDCap and Excel. 18 – 20 Data Analysis Data was analyzed using Stata (v. 17.0; StataCorp, College Station, TX). Descriptive statistics were performed for pediatric emergency medicine physicians'' demographics and pediatric ED demographics, with categorical variables presented as counts and percentages, and continuous variables presented as means and SDs when appropriate. Logistic regression was used to analyze the case scenarios. A dummy variable was created for each urine collection method answered in the scenario. For answers with high response rates, univariate regression models were run to see which of the pediatric emergency medicine physicians and ED demographic variables significantly impacted urine collection choice. Variables tested for significance included: age of respondent (years), number of years of respondent work experience, location of Pediatric Emergency Medicine (PEM) fellowship training (country), pediatric ED wait times, and ED annual census (number of patients seen at an ED). A 2-sided P -value <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Ethics Approval Institutional ethics approval was obtained from the University of Manitoba Health Research Ethics Board. Operational approval was also obtained from the PERC committee. RESULTS Of 235 surveys, 96 were returned with a participation rate of 41%, and 87 (37%) surveys were completed. Pediatric Emergency Medicine Physician Demographics Full details of survey respondent characteristics are presented in Table 1 . Most respondents were aged 40 to 49 years (n=31), female (60.5%), and did some part of their training in Canada. Overall, 85.1% of participants did their medical school training in Canada, and 89.7% did their residency training in Canada. Of those who did their residency in Canada (n=78), most were trained in Ontario (18.4%) or Quebec (17.2%). Most respondents completed a Pediatric Emergency Medicine fellowship (72.4%), with general pediatricians making up the second largest group of respondents (12.6%). Fifty (57.5%) of the respondents cited over 20 years of postmedical school clinical experience. When asked which health care centers pediatric emergency medicine physicians worked in during 2022, respondents identified Children’s Hospital of Eastern Ontario (n=14) and CHU Sainte-Justine in Montreal (n=10) as the most common centers of practice. TABLE 1. Characteristics of Survey Respondents Characteristics N (%) Age range 30-39 20 (23.0) 40-49 31 (35.6) 50-59 29 (33.3) 60 and above 7 (8.0) Sex Male 34 (39.5) Female 52 (60.5) Qualifications Emergency medicine physician 6 (6.9) Family medicine 1 (1.1) Family physician+emergency 3 (3.4) General pediatrician 11 (12.6) Pediatric emergency medicine 63 (72.4) General pediatrician/pediatric emergency medicine 3 (3.4) Medical school training location Canada 74 (85.1) United States 6 (6.9) Other 7 (8.0) Residency training location Canada 78 (89.7) Ontario 16 (18.4) Quebec 15 (17.2) Manitoba 8 (9.2) Alberta 7 (8.0) British Columbia 5 (5.7) Saskatchewan 3 (3.4) Newfoundland 2 (2.3) Nova Scotia 4 (4.6) Others 18 (20.7) United States 8 (9.2) Outside North America 1 (1.1) Post medical school-experience 05-09 9 (10.3) 10-15 18 (21.0) 15-19 10 (11.5) 20 and above 50 (57.5) Health care centers * Alberta Children’s Hospital (Calgary) 8 (9.3) British Columbia Children’s Hospital (Vancouver) 7 (8.1) CHU Sainte-Justine (Montreal) 10 (11.6) Laval University (Quebec City) 2 (2.3) Children’s Hospital of Eastern Ontario (Ottawa) 14 (16.3) Izaak Walton Killam Health Centre (Halifax) 4 (4.7) Janeway Children’s Health and Rehabilitation Centre (St. John’s) 4 (4.7) Jim Pattison Children’s Hospital (Saskatoon) 4 (4.7) Kingston Health Sciences Centre (Kingston) 2 (2.3) McMaster Children’s Hospital (Hamilton) 2 (2.3) Montreal Children’s Hospital (Montreal) 6 (7.0) Stollery Children’s Hospital (Edmonton) 6 (7.0) The Children’s Hospital of Winnipeg (Winnipeg) 8 (9.3) The Hospital for Sick Children (Toronto) 7 (8.1) Children’s Hospital; London Health Sciences Centre (London) 2 (2.3) Open in a new tab * Health care centers practiced for a minimum of 3 months between January and December 2022. Pediatric ED Demographics When examining the characteristics of Canadian pediatric ED departments, 39 respondents (45.5%) indicated their annual census was >75,000 patients. The mean minimum number of staff during a shift was 1.2 (SD: 0.6), and the maximum was 4.7 (SD: 2.0). The majority of EDs had policies that allowed for nurses to collect and analyze urine samples (73.3%) and the majority of respondents indicated only nurses collect the urine samples (83.9%). However, only 44.4% of pediatric emergency medicine physicians stated that nurses were allowed to decide on the collection method. Of the 14 pediatric emergency medicine physicians who indicated that physicians collect urine samples, suprapubic aspiration was the only method for which physicians collected the majority of samples (53.9%) as compared with nurses. Just over half of pediatric emergency medicine physicians stated they were using bladder stimulation, an emerging urine collection method. Of those who indicated it was not being incorporated into clinical practice, nursing constraints (n=11) and time constraints (n=14) were the most commonly cited reasons. Five respondents indicated this method was not performed due to a lack of familiarity or awareness of the method. When asked which factors affected urine collection choice, only the patient’s Canadian Triage and Acuity Scale (CTAS) score, a validated tool used across Canadian EDs to effectively group patients based on the severity of their presenting complaint and clinical condition, was noted to have an impact (60.9%). Other factors, including patient sex, circumcision status, analgesic availability, bladder scanning ability and volume, and wait time in the center, did not affect the choice of the collection method. Full details are available in Table 2 . TABLE 2. Emergency Department (ED) Characteristics ED characteristics N (%) Average annual census in ED <50,000 17 (19.8) 50,000-75,000 30 (34.9) 75,000 and above 39 (45.4) Staff members in ED Minimum staff 1.2 (0.6) * Maximum staff 4.7 (2.0) * ED nurses allowed to collect/analyze urine specimen? Yes 63 (73.3) No 18 (20.9) Don’t know 5 (5.8) If yes, ED nurses allowed to decide the method? Yes 28 (44.4) No 35 (55.6) Is the urine specimen collected by nurses only? Yes 73 (83.9) No 14 (16.1) Methods in which physicians are involved Transurethral catheterization Yes 3 (21.4) No 11 (78.6) Bladder stimulation Yes 3 (23.1) No 10 (76.9) Supra pubic aspiration Yes 7 (53.9) No 6 (46.2) New technique of infant bladder stimulation incorporated? Yes 47 (54.7) If yes, then maximum attempts <5 45 (95.7) 5-10 1 (2.1) 10 and above 1 (2.1) No 39 (45.4) If no, barriers to the technique Nursing constraints 11 (30.6) Time constraints 14 (38.9) Concern of contamination 5 (13.9) Familiarity and awareness 5 (13.9) Facility constraints 1 (2.8) Factors affecting collection techniques Canadian Triage and Acuity Scale score Yes 53 (60.9) No 34 (39.1) Sex Yes 13 (14.9) No 74 (85.1) Circumcision status Yes 9 (10.3) No 78 (89.7) Analgesics and sedatives Yes 36 (41.4) No 51 (58.6) Bladder scan Yes 26 (29.9) No 61 (70.1) Volume of patients Yes 23 (26.4) No 64 (73.6) Wait time of patients Yes 36 (41.4) No 51 (58.6) Open in a new tab * Mean and SD. Case Scenarios When examining variation in urine collection method choice, catheterization was the most common method among pediatric emergency medicine physicians in Canadian pediatric EDs in all 4 case scenarios. Bladder stimulation and bagged urine were the next most common urine collection method choices, respectively. The proportion of providers who selected catheterization as their preferred urine collection method increased with the increasing clinical severity of the case. Interestingly, shared decision-making was utilized the most in the fourth case scenario, which was a combination of the 3 previous case scenarios. Unfortunately, shared decision-making could not be tested for significance as it was underpowered. In the logistic regression models, we found factors such as the length of wait time for patients to be seen in the ED (odds ratio=3.03, 95% CI=1.14-8.09) and year postmedical school (odds ratio=1.67, 95% CI=1.07-2.60) to be significantly associated with the increased choice of catheterization when selecting a urine collection method. DISCUSSION The results of this study suggest that there is variation in the practice of urine collection among Canadian pediatric emergency medicine physicians, which is influenced by some physician and ED demographics. This is reflected in the urine collection method choice in case scenarios where the patient was seen as having lower acuity or in cases where a UTI was not the clear diagnosis. In these cases, not only was there more heterogeneity in the collection method chosen, but shared decision-making was more often utilized. In situations where children were presented as acutely unwell, providers more often opted to choose an invasive urine collection method, which is often quicker and has been shown to have lower contamination rates. 6 Bladder stimulation and bagged urine samples were the most frequently chosen collection methods in the absence of catheterization. These urine collection techniques are not the methods recommended to diagnose a UTI by the CPS. While the position statement recommends invasive methods, novel methods are becoming increasingly used in clinical practice. The bladder stimulation technique is a method that has only emerged over the last 10 years. Additional assist techniques, including utilizing ultrasound to assess the bladder before catheterization, are also becoming more common. 21 These methods have been shown to have improved clinical outcomes but are not yet captured in any clinical guidelines. This is suspected to be a contributor to the practice variation seen among pediatric emergency medicine physicians in Canadian pediatric EDs. This variation seen among clinicians is not unique to Canada. Previous studies conducted in Europe and the United Kingdom have found similar variations among practitioners and discrepancies between clinical practice guidelines and what is clinically performed. 15 – 17 Hadjipanayis et al 17 found large variations among general pediatricians, both within the same country and across Europe, and felt that there was discrepancy in what guidelines recommended and what was practical for physicians in a clinical setting. A study conducted in France also found discrepancies between national guidelines and the preferred choice of collection method. They sent out a survey to 26 hospitals across France and found that catheterization and suprapubic aspiration were underutilized despite being the recommended tests of choice by the French Society of Pediatrics. 15 Another study that examined the current practices of general practitioners in the United Kingdom compared with the National Institute for Health and Child Excellence (NICE) guidelines found that the preferred method of obtaining a specimen was urine collection bags, despite the evidence-based guidelines recommending a clean catch urine specimen. 16 The second objective of this study was to examine what factors influenced the choice of urine collection method. In this study, the length of wait time for patients to be seen in the ED and the number of years since completing medical school training were found to be statistically significant. If patients have been waiting to be seen by a physician for several hours, or if there is limited bed availability, it is unsurprising that physicians would be more inclined to proceed with catheterization rather than other urine collection methods due to its efficiency and low contamination rates. Years of work following medical school training were also significantly associated with catheterization use. Canadian guidelines have previously recommended catheterization as the preferred collection method when suspecting UTI in pre–toilet-trained children. 11 Older practitioners typically are more familiar with this method and have adopted it into their clinical practice. They also may be less familiar with novel methods. In this study, 13.9% of respondents cited not using the bladder stimulation technique due to a lack of either awareness or familiarity with the technique. In turn, younger clinicians are more likely to have had exposure to novel techniques and skills, such as point-of-care ultrasound or the bladder stimulation method, during their training. 22 , 23 Therefore, they may be more inclined to use these methods in lieu of catheterization. A study by Schroeder et al 24 examined factors that influenced selecting catheterization as the urine collection method over bagged urine. The study found a number of variables associated with higher rates of catheterization, including practitioners under 40 years of age, and when evaluation occurred after usual business hours. They postulated that younger practitioners were more likely to adhere closely to guidelines or select catheterization due to evolving training practices. 24 The study did not examine the use of novel methods, but was conducted in 2005, which is before the emergence of new collection methods. This may have resulted in conflicting results between the years of experience of the practitioner and the choice of catheterization. In addition, the study found that clinicians assessing patients after hours were more likely to select catheterization over other collection methods. 24 There are typically fewer resources and staff available at night, which can lead to time constraints. These findings could be similar to our study’s finding that longer ED wait times were associated with increased rates of catheterization. Overall, there are several strengths and limitations to this study. To our knowledge, this is the first study done in Canada examining practice variation in urine specimen collection choice among Canadian pediatric ED physicians. Utilizing the PERC network, we were able to recruit respondents from across the country, making it a national study. The survey was created in collaboration with a research methodologist and informed by a systematic review that investigated the current literature surrounding the choice of urine collection methods. 25 In addition, feedback was sought early on from members of the TREKK network. 13 Limitations to the study include a small sample size which restricted the ability to perform sensitivity analysis. In addition, there was a low response rate of 41%. However, this is in keeping with response rates typically found in other online surveys and with recent PERC studies. 26 Finally, surveys were sent out to complete on a voluntary basis. Each of these factors could contribute to selection bias. There are a number of emerging urine collection techniques that are being introduced into clinical practice but have not been adopted into clinical practice guidelines. Conducting randomized controlled trials (RCTs) using these methods will allow for direct comparison between these novel methods and those currently recommended by various pediatric societies across the world. There is also a potential to examine the role of shared decision-making in the choice of urine collection methods and how this can improve patient care experience. 27 These RCTs could influence future clinical practice guidelines and will allow for improved delivery of care. In conclusion, the data suggests there is practice variation in urine collection methods among Canadian pediatric emergency medicine physicians. This practice variation is influenced by both individual providers and the demographics of EDs. Supplementary Material SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL pec-41-475-s001.pdf (96.4KB, pdf) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors thank the administrative staff of The Children’s Hospital Research Institute of Manitoba for their support and contributions to their research work. Footnotes L.M.W. was supported by the Max Rady College of Medicine Medical Student Summer Research Program. T.P.K. was supported by a Canada Research Chair. A.A. was supported by the Endowered Chair, Pediatric Emergency Medicine, Robert Wallace Cameron, the Children’s Hospital Research Institute of Manitoba Startup funds, and the University of Manitoba Department of Pediatrics Startup funds. Disclosure: The authors declare no conflict of interest. Supplemental Digital Content is available for this article. Direct URL citations are provided in the HTML and PDF versions of this article on the journal’s website, www.pec-online.com . Contributor Information Lucy M. Wilson, Email: lucy.wilson@ucalgary.ca. Terry P. Klassen, Email: terry.klassen@usask.ca. Tighe Crombie, Email: tcrombie@cheo.on.ca. Apoorva Gangwani, Email: agangwani@chrim.ca. Veronica Ka Wai Lai, Email: Veronica.Lai@umanitoba.ca. Karen Gripp, Email: kgripp@hsc.mb.ca. Elisabete Doyle, Email: edoyle@hsc.mb.ca. Darcy Beer, Email: Darcy.Beer@umanitoba.ca. Kaitlin Hogue, Email: khogue@cancercare.mb.ca. Oana Florescu, Email: oflorescu@manitoba-physicians.ca. Lisa Knisley, Email: LKnisley@chrim.ca. Alex Aregbesola, Email: Alex.Aregbesola@umanitoba.ca. REFERENCES 1. Shaikh N, Morone NE, Bost JE, et al. Prevalence of urinary tract infection in childhood: a meta-analysis. Pediatr Infect Dis J. 2008;27:302–308. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. American Academy of Pediatrics, Committee on Quality Improvement, Subcommittee on Urinary Tract Infection . Practice parameter: the diagnosis, treatment, and evaluation of the initial urinary tract infection in febrile infants and young children. Pediatrics. 1999;103:843–852. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Gates A, Shulhan J, Featherstone R, et al. A systematic review of parents’ experiences and information needs related to their child’s urinary tract infection. Patient Educ Couns. 2018;101:1207–1215. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Whiting P, Westwood M, Watt I, et al. Rapid tests and urine sampling techniques for the diagnosis of urinary tract infection (UTI) in children under five years: a systematic review. BMC Pediatr. 2005;5:1–13. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Etoubleau C, Reveret M, Brouet D, et al. Moving from bag to catheter for urine collection in non-toilet-trained children suspected of having urinary tract infection: a paired comparison of urine cultures. J Pediatr. 2009;154:803–806. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Diviney J, Jaswon MS. Urine collection methods and dipstick testing in non-toilet-trained children. Pediatr Nephrol. 2021;36:1697–1708. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kaufman J, Fitzpatrick P, Tosif S, et al. Faster clean catch urine collection (Quick-Wee method) from infants: randomised controlled trial. BMJ (Online). 2017;357:j1341. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ravichandran Y, Parker S, Farooqi A, et al. Bladder stimulation for clean catch urine collection improved parent and provider satisfaction. Pediatr Emerg Care. 2022;3