INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5367,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11848169','Pathological angiogenesis was associated with cerebrovascular lesion and neurodegeneration in Alzheimer''s disease.','Gao F, Zhan Y, Wang Q, Zhang M, Dai L, Shen Y.','2025','candi svegfr2 angiopoietin2 serpin svegfr1 stie2 il8 svegfr1 stie2 svegfr2 angiopoietin2 serpin svegfr1 stie2 il8 pdgfrβ qalb unfunctional pericyte angiopoietin svegfr1 svegfr2 stie2 angiopoietin2 serpin serpin bfgf il8 gifts p50ag025588 furtherly a32961 dounce homogenizer tbsx rocking guhcl guhcl ph7 rocking aliquoted meninges hbss polytron homogenizer vigorously deoxycholate ethylenediaminetetraacetic a32961 sonicated amplitudes vigorously vortexed furtherly bicinchoninic bca e112 vazyme denatured np0008 dodecyl polyvinylidene pvdf pvdf tbst tbst tbst 3528s αsma ma1 pdgfrβ cd13 proteintech tuj1 t2200 deparaffinization unmasking boiling 3528s cd13 proteintech pbst fluor pbst antifade lsm 40x cd13 plex k15200e guhcl plex pt181 guhcl claudin novus nbp2 claudin tbsx pecam quantikine dcd310 tbsx occludin 3f10 ab31721 occludin novus h00004950 p01 sulfo r32ab l45sa quickplex guhcl candi candi 2023ky simoa quanterix quanterix ehalb bromocresol advia pdgfr ehpdgfrb spdgfrβ hmag luminex luminex svegfr2 angiopoietin2 serpin il8 k15190d svegfr1 stie2 bfgf qalb 750w mm3 freesurfer bianca log10 lme δ_mmse δ_cortical_thickness δ_hippo_volume δ_amy_volume v26 s1a pericyte cd13 αsma tuj1 s1b αsma pdgfrβ s2a cd13 s2b αsma pdgfrβ pericyte αsma pdgfrβ αsma pdgfrβ tbsx guhcl ultracentrifugation occludin claudin tbsx occludin claudin guhcl guhcl tbsx occludin claudin occludin claudin tbsx occludin claudin claudin s2d expectedly candi ttau lh_mean pdgfrβ qalb mm3 candi pdgfrβ ttau stie2 stie2 il8 svegfr1 svegfr2 angiopoietin2 serpin bfgf candi svegfr1 svegfr2 stie2 angiopoietin2 serpin bfgf il8 bfgf il8 serpin serpin stie2 svegfr vegfr ttau svegfr1 stie2 bfgf ttau svegfr2 angiopoietin2 serpin ttau ttau svegfr1 stie2 stie2 svegfr1 ttau svegfr1 stie2 angiopoietin2 bfgf stie2 angiopoietin2 bfgf svegfr1 stie2 svegfr1 stie2 candi pdgfrβ qalb svegfr1 pdgfrβ svegfr2 stie2 serpin il8 qalb svegfr1 serpin il8 stie2 pdgfrβ pdgfrβ qalb pdgfrβ qalb svegfr1 stie2 angiopoietin2 stie2 corelated stie2 il8 svegfr1 stie2 angiopoietin2 bfgf serpin il8 bfgf il8 serpin serpin stie2 vegfr δ_mmse δ_cortical_thickness δ_hippo_volume δ_amy_volume stie2 bfgf stie2 angiopoietin2 stie2 βδ_cortical_thickness βδ_hippo_volume βδ_amy_volume angiopoietin2 βδ_amy_volume il8 βδ_amy_volume il8 βδ_cortical_thickness δ_mmse δ_cortical_thickness δ_hippo_volume δ_amy_volume candi svegfr1 svegfr2 angiopoietin stie2 quiescent reactivate svegfr1 stie2 angiopoientin2 bfgf serpin svegfr1 stie2 bfgf svegfr1 stie2 popp svegfr1 occludin claudin pericyte pdgfrβ αsma serpin il8 qalb svegfr2 stie2 angiopoietin1 tie2 pericyte stie2 serpin il8 svegfr2 svegfr1 pericyte pdgfrβ svegfr1 renhai svegfr1 pericyte pericyte spiraling svegfr1 il8 oskar svegfr1 il8 stie2 angiopoietin2 angiopoietin2 antagonistically angiopoietin1 tie2 pericyte 2023ky e14521 e14521 e14521 deepest w2411069 xdb39000000 2020yfa0509304 2021yfa0805300 e14521 yaxi fenggao7 ustc yongshen ustc montagne cuadrado godia dwivedi spdgfrbeta montagne skoog wallin fredman vagnucci jefferies biron fenninger dickstein compass carvey hendey ulmann staufenbiel krucker betensky e1289 e1298 aky akay murdock multiregion tsartsalis sleven fancy monsky monsky fukumura gohongi transvascular hatakeyama ninomiya kanazawa lindqvist francardo walchli bisschop carmeliet heckman boulay saubamea decleves e53208 hartz sokola schindelin arganda carreras frise candi bace1 griffanti zamboni bianca dumitrescu popp oikonomidi tautvydaite pericytes vegfr1 falkenhain njiru stalls acker wiessner allegrini angiopoietin angiopoietin yancopoulos campochiaro angiopoietin gertje westen e1812 e1824 mazzone jonckx carmeliet flt1 vegfb hedlund vegfr1 pericyte challa thore anstrom e36893 mcaleese miah ashby wellington stomrud e867 e77 thurston alitalo angiopoietin rosenstein krum ruhrberg organogenesis e14521 e14521 e14521','Abstract INTRODUCTION We investigated the specific factors driving abnormal angiogenesis in Alzheimer''s disease (AD) and its role in cerebrovascular lesions and neurodegeneration. METHODS We assessed cerebrovascular pathologies, amyloid‐beta (Aβ), and tau pathologies in post mortem human brains and detected 12 angiogenic factors in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the China Aging and Neurodegenerative Disease Initiative (CANDI) cohort. RESULTS We observed severe blood‐brain barrier damage and elevated levels of the vascular marker CD31 in human AD brains, which had a stronger correlation with tau pathology than Aβ pathology. Consistently, only CSF pTau181 showed positive associations with CSF angiogenesis factors (soluble vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 [sVEGFR2], VEGF‐C, VEGF‐D, placental growth factor [PLGF], Angiopoietin2, and Serpin E1), but not CSF Aβ42/40. Additionally, higher levels of CSF sVEGFR1, soluble Tyrosine‐protein kinase receptor 2 [sTIE2], PLGF, and interleukin 8 [IL8], as well as lower levels of CSF urokinase‐type plasminogen activator [uPA], were associated with worsen cerebrovascular pathologies and neurodegeneration. DISCUSSION Our findings indicate that tau pathology may play a critical role in pathological angiogenesis, contributing to cerebrovascular lesions and neurodegeneration in AD. HIGHLIGHTS BBB damage and elevated vascular marker CD31 in human AD brains had a stronger correlation with tau pathology than Aβ pathology. CSF pTau181 mediated the effect of CSF Aβ42/40 on CSF sVEGFR1 and sTIE2. Only CSF pTau181 showed positive associations with sVEGFR2, VEGF‐C, VEGF‐D, PLGF, Angiopoietin2, and Serpin E1. Higher sVEGFR1, sTIE2, PLGF, and IL8, and lower uPA in CSF, were associated with cerebrovascular pathologies and neurodegeneration. Keywords: Alzheimer''s disease, angiogenesis, cerebrovascular lesion, neurodegeneration 1. BACKGROUND Alzheimer''s Disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia, and its pathologic features include extracellular amyloid‐beta (Aβ) deposition forming plaques and neurofibrillary tangles in brain parenchyma. 1 Research has demonstrated that cerebral vascular abnormalities manifest early in the progression of AD and are associated with accelerated brain atrophy and cognitive decline. 2 Notably, neuroimaging studies have shown that individuals with AD commonly exhibit various forms of small vessel disease, such as white matter hyperintensities (WMH), enlarged perivascular spaces, and microbleeds. 3 Additionally, the structure and function of the blood‐brain barrier (BBB) are impaired in AD patients, as indicated by elevated levels of platelet‐derived growth factor receptor β (PDGFRβ) 2 , 4 , 5 or increased ratios of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) albumin to serum albumin (Qalb). 6 More and more evidence suggests the occurrence of abnormal angiogenesis, characterized by increased levels of angiogenic factors and higher small vessel density in the post mortem brains of AD patients 7 , 8 , 9 and AD mouse models. 10 , 11 Notably, Rachel et al. observed increased blood vessel density and alterations in angiogenesis‐related genes in aged tau mice, whereas such changes were not found in APP/PS1 mice. 11 Additionally, analysis of single nuclear RNA sequencing from autopsied brains of AD patients has confirmed that endothelial cells in the AD brain display a pro‐angiogenesis phenotype. 12 , 13 , 14 Abnormal angiogenesis in the adult brain can lead to excessive unfunctional small blood vessel formation and impairments in endothelial wall integrity, pericyte coverage, and basement membrane function. 15 , 16 This phenomenon has been proposed as a contributing factor to BBB dysfunction observed in various neurological disorders, including stroke, 17 glioma, 18 and Parkinson''s disease. 19 However, the relationship between abnormal angiogenesis and amyloid versus tau pathology in AD patients, as well as its role in cerebrovascular pathologies, remains unclear. In this study, we firstly quantified insoluble Aβ and pTau181 concentrations, and investigated their relationship with abnormal angiogenesis in the post mortem human brains. The process of angiogenesis involves complex interactions between various factors and receptors, mainly involving two pathways, that is the vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) pathway and the angiopoietin—tyrosine kinase receptor (TIE) pathway. 20 Thus, we comprehensively detected a panel of angiogenic factors in CSF from a clinical AD cohort (Table S1 ), including soluble VEGF receptor 1 and 2 (sVEGFR1 and sVEGFR2); VEGF‐A, C, and D; placental growth factor (PLGF); soluble TIE 2 (sTIE2); Angiopoietin2; urokinase‐type plasminogen activator (uPA); serpin family E member 1 (Serpin E1); basic FGF (bFGF); and interleukin 8 (IL8). Subsequently, we investigated whether the levels of angiogenic factors were associated with CSF Aβ42/40 or pTau181. Additionally, we analyzed the relationship between these angiogenic factors and cerebral vascular pathologies, neurodegeneration, and cognitive function. 2. METHODS 2.1. Human brain studies 2.1.1. Human post mortem brain samples Frozen post mortem frontal cortex tissues were gifts from the Brain Bank and Neurodegenerative Disorder Research Center of University of Science and Technology of China ( n = 28) and Emory Alzheimer''s Disease Center (P50AG025588) ( n = 12). This study included 31 cases in total (30 AD and 10 non‐ADD), based on neuropathological diagnosis according to National Institute of Aging—Alzheimer''s Association (NIA‐AA) guidelines. 21 And the diagnosis of all cases was furtherly confirmed by quantitative analysis of insoluble Aβ42, Aβ40, and pTau181. Details of demographic and quantitative neuropathological characteristics were described in the Table S2 . All study participants provided written informed consent. 2.1.2. Isolation of membrane protein and insoluble protein from human brain Prefrontal cortex dissected from human brain was used to isolate different component proteins by a three‐step extraction as previously describe. 22 In brief, 90‐120 mg of frozen brain tissue was homogenized in ice‐cold 1 mL Tris buffered saline (TBS) buffer containing protease inhibitor cocktail (A32961, Thermo) by Dounce homogenizer for 100 times. After centrifugation at 100,000 × g for 30 min at 4°C, the resultant supernatant was referred to as soluble protein. The pellet was re‐homogenized in TBSX buffer (1% Triton X‐100 in TBS buffer) for 20 times and incubated with gentle rocking for 1 h at 4°C. After centrifugation as above, the resultant supernatant was referred to as membrane protein. The pellet was re‐homogenized in GuHCl buffer (5 M GuHCl in TBS buffer, pH7.6) for 20 times and incubated with gentle rocking overnight at 4°C. After centrifugation as above, the resultant supernatant was referred to as insoluble protein. And all these component proteins were aliquoted and stored at −80°C for further protein quantification assays. 2.1.3. Isolation of cerebral vessels Cerebral vessels were extracted from prefrontal cortex of human brain. The protocol was adapted from previous study. 23 , 24 After removal of meninges, 1 g of frozen brain tissue was cut into small pieces (about 1 mm 3 ) and homogenized in 10 mL ice‐cold B1 buffer (1 × HBSS + 10 mM Hepes) by polytron homogenizer for 30 times. After centrifugation at 2,000 × g for 10 min at 4°C, the pellet was resuspended in 20 mL B2 buffer (15% Dextran‐70 in B1 buffer) by vigorously shaking the tube manually for 1 min. After centrifugation at 4,800 × rpm for 15 min at 4°C, the upper myelin and debris was carefully removed. The pellet was re‐suspended in 3 mL B3 buffer (1% bovine serum albumin [BSA] in B1 buffer) with low‐binding tips and transferred into 20‐µm filters on a filter holder to remove nucleus and debris. Then, the filters were transferred into 50 mL tubes containing 20 mL B3 buffer and the vessels were detached from filters by shaking gently. After centrifugation at 2,000 × g for 10 min at 4°C, enriched cerebral vessels were obtained and stored at −80°C. Protein of cerebral vessels were extracted using lysis buffer (50 mM Tris, 150 mM NaCl, 1% Triton X‐100, 1% sodium deoxycholate, 0.1% SDS, 1 mM ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid [EDTA]) containing protease inhibitor cocktail (A32961, Thermo). And cerebral vessels were sonicated for 3 × 5s at 50 amplitudes on ice and vigorously vortexed at 1400 × rpm at 4°C. After centrifugation at 10,000 × g for 10 min at 4°C, the supernatant was collected and stored at −80°C for further protein quantification assays. RESEARCH IN CONTEXT Systematic review : A PubMed review shows an angiogenic phenotype and increased vascular density in the brain of Alzheimer''s disease (AD), indicating an abnormal angiogenesis had occurred. However, the specific factors driving angiogenesis and its role in cerebrovascular lesions and neurodegeneration in AD remain unclear. Interpretation : Abnormal angiogenesis showed a stronger association with tau pathology than amyloid‐beta (Aβ) pathology in human AD brains. This is further supported by the correlation between cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pTau181 and CSF angiogenic factors in a clinical AD cohort study. Our results indicated that tau pathology may play a critical role in pathological angiogenesis, contributing to cerebrovascular lesions and neurodegeneration in AD. Future directions : The downstream mechanism of tau pathology‐induced pathological angiogenesis need to be furtherly investigated using AD mouse models and human AD brains. 2.1.4. Western blotting Proteins from human cerebral vessels were quantified using bicinchoninic acid (BCA) assay (E112‐02, Vazyme) and denatured in loading buffer (Invitrogen, NP0008) and heated at 100°C for 10 min. Equal amounts of proteins per sample were loaded on 8% or 10% acrylamide gel (sodium dodecyl sulfate‐polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis [SDS‐PAGE]). After electrophoresis, proteins were transferred to polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) membranes (240 mA, 90–120 min). Then, PVDF membranes were blocked with 5% milk in TBST (0.1% Tween‐20 in TBS) for 1 h at room temperature and incubated with primary antibodies in TBST overnight at 4°C. Membranes were then rinsed four times with 1 × TBST for 7 min and incubated with secondary antibodies (CST,1:5000) for 90 min at room temperature. The following primary antibodies were used: CD31 (CST, 3528S, 1:1000), αSMA (Invitrogen, MA1‐06110, 1:500), PDGFRβ (CST, 3169, 1:2000), CD13 (Proteintech, 66211‐1‐Ig, 1:3000), Tuj1 (Sigma, T2200, 1:1000). 2.1.5. Immunofluorescence After deparaffinization and rehydration, 5 µm paraffin sections were immersed in citrate unmasking solution (pH 6.0) and heated in a microwave at sub‐boiling temperature for 10 min. The sections were then blocked with blocking buffer (5% normal goat serum, 0.3% Triton ×‐100 in phosphate buffered saline [PBS]) for 1 h at room temperature and incubated overnight at 4°C with primary antibodies against CD31 (CST, 3528S, 1:200) and CD13 (Proteintech, 66211‐1‐Ig, 1:200). After washing with PBST (0.05% Tween‐20 in PBS), sections were incubated with Goat anti‐Mouse Alexa Fluor 647 (CST, 4410, 1:500) for 1.5 h at room temperature, followed by incubation with DAPI. The sections were then washed in PBST, rinsed in PBS, and mounted with Prolong Diamond Antifade Mounting Solution (Invitrogen). Images were acquired using a Zeiss LSM 800 microscope with a 40x oil objective. The positive area of CD31 and CD13 were quantified using the Analyze Particles plugin in Fiji, 25 applying the same threshold for each experiment. 2.1.6. Quantification of human brain proteins The level of human brain proteins of interest to this study was determined using the following assays: V‐PLEX Aβ Peptide Panel 1 (6E10) Kit (MSD, K15200E) for Aβ42/ Aβ40 in GuHCl fraction; S‐PLEX Human Tau (pT181) Kit for pTau181 in GuHCl fraction; Human Claudin‐5 ELISA Kit (Novus Biologicals, NBP2‐75332) for Claudin‐5 in TBSX fraction; Human CD31/PECAM‐1 Quantikine ELISA Kit (R&D, DCD310) for CD31 in TBSX fraction. The concentration of occludin was measured on MSD, using antibody pairs validated by previous study. 22 A mouse monoclonal antibody (Invitrogen, OC‐3F10, 1:500) was used as the capture antibody, a rabbit polyclonal antibody (Abcam, ab31721, 1:250) as the detection antibody, recombinant occludin proteins (Novus Biologicals, H00004950‐P01) as standard protein (range: 31.25 ‐ 2000 ng/mL), and SULFO‐TAG labeled anti‐rabbit antibody (MSD, R32AB, 1:500) was used for detection. Assays were done in duplicate for the same sample on a small spot plate (MSD, L45SA) on MESO QuickPlex SQ 120 MM (MSD). To assess the relationship between AD pathologies and CD31 levels, we categorized AD brains into a high pathological group if the levels of insoluble Aβ42, Aβ40, or pTau181 in the GuHCl fraction were greater than the median. 2.2. Cohort studies 2.2.1. Participants A total of 200 participants were recruited from China Aging and Neurodegenerative Disease Initiative (CANDI) between 2018 and 2022, including cognitively unimpaired individuals, patients with mild cognitive impairment (MCI), Alzheimer’ s disease or non‐AD dementia. The CANDI cohort was established at the First Affiliated Hospital of the University of Science in China between 2018 and 2024. All participants underwent a medical history, apolipoprotein E (APOE) genotyping and cognitive assessments, including the Mini‐Mental State Examination (MMSE) and Clinical Dementia Rating (CDR) scores. In all participants, Aβ and Tau status were defined using CSF Aβ42/40 ratio and CSF pTau181 with a previously established cut‐off value (A+: Aβ42/40 < 0.0642; T+: pTau181 > 54.68 pg/ml). 26 Patients with MCI were defined by CDR = 0.5, AD by CDR ≥ 1 & A+, non‐ADD dementia by CDR ≥ 1 & A‐. Based on the CDR score, we divided the participants into CDR = 0 group (with 44 individuals), CDR = 0.5 group (with 60 individuals) and CDR ≥ 1 group (with 96 individuals). Among them, 76 individuals had longitudinal cognitive assessments (follow up: mean [SD] = 24.2 [11.8] months), and 70 individuals had longitudinal structure MRI (follow up: mean [SD] = 23.2 [9.9] months). The detailed inclusion and exclusion criteria of participants were described in the previous study. 26 This study was approved by the ethics committee of the First Affiliated Hospital of the University of Science and Technology China (2023KY‐117). All patients (or immediate family members) provided written informed consent to participate in this study. 2.2.2. CSF and serum biomarkers CSF and serum samples were collected, processed and storage as previously describe. 26 The level of biomarkers of interest to this study was determined using the following assays: commercially available Simoa kits (Quanterix, 101995, 103714 and 103520) on HD‐X (Quanterix) for CSF markers (Aβ40, Aβ42, total tau, and pTau181) and serum markers (NFL and GFAP); Albumin Human ELISA Kit (Thermo, EHALB) for CSF albumin; a bromocresol green dye binding assay (ADVIA 1800; Siemens, Berlin, Germany) for serum albumin; Human PDGFR beta ELISA Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, EHPDGFRB) for CSF sPDGFRβ; hMag Luminex Assay kit (R&D) on Luminex 200 Analyzer for CSF sVEGFR2, Angiopoietin2, uPA, Serpin E1 and IL8; Angiogenesis Panel 1 (human) Kits (MSD, K15190D) for CSF sVEGFR1, sTIE2, VEGF‐A, VEGF‐C, VEGF‐D, PLGF, and bFGF. Qalb was calculated using the formula: (CSF albumin / serum albumin) × 1000. 2.2.3. MRI data MRI imaging acquisition was conducted in GE DISCOVER 750w 3.0T MRI scanner (GE Healthcare, US) (TR = 8.5 ms, TE = 3.2 ms, flip angle = 12, matrix = 256 × 256, NEX = 1, bandwidth = 31.25 Hz, Voxel = 1 × 1 × 1 mm3). Mean cortical thickness, hippocampus volume, and amygdala volume were estimated using Freesurfer (v7.2.0) as previously described. 27 T1‐weighted and T2 FLAIR data were included in the WMH analyses. For the purpose of defining regions of interest (ROIs) and other masks, participants are aligned into a standard space atlas using the T1‐weighted structural scan. Then, WMH volume was automated quantified on T2 FLAIR images using an automated, supervised method in BIANCA (Brain Intensity AbNormality Classification Algorithm). 28 2.2.4. Statistical analysis Data distributions were assessed using chi‐squared tests for categorical variables and Kolmogorov‐Smirnov tests for continuous variables. Log10‐transformations were used to the variables which were not normally distributed. Group differences were assessed using analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) adjusted age, sex, and APOE genotype, following by Bonferroni multiple comparison tests. The associations at baseline were determined using a linear regression adjusted by age, sex, and APOE genotype. Linear mixed‐effects (LME) models were used to assess the associations of CSF angiogenic factors with longitudinal cognitive decline (Δ_MMSE) and brain atrophy (Δ_Cortical_thickness, Δ_Hippo_volume and Δ_Amy_volume) including covariate adjustment for age, sex, and APOE genotype. A 5000‐bootstrap mediation analysis were conducted with PROCESS macro version 4.1 for SPSS to evaluate direct and indirect effects of angiogenic biomarkers with Aβ and Tau pathologies using age, sex, and APOE genotype as covariates. All statistical analyses were performed in SPSS v26.0 (IBM Corp) and Prism 9.0 (GraphPad). A two‐tailed p value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. 3. RESULTS 3.1. Worsened BBB integrity and elevated CD31 levels related to tau pathology in human AD brains In order to evaluate cerebrovascular pathologies, we extracted cerebral vessels from human cortex tissues (Supplementary Figure S1A ) and found that isolated blood vessels demonstrated higher levels of endothelial marker CD31, pericyte marker CD13, and smooth muscle marker αSMA, and very lower levels of neuronal marker Tuj1 (Supplementary Figure S1B ). Additionally, Western blot analyses showed that the AD group had significantly lower levels of αSMA and PDGFRβ compared to the non‐AD group, while there was no significant difference in CD31 levels in equal protein loading amount between the two groups (Figure 1A and Supplementary Figure S2A ). Immunofluorescence staining showed that no significant difference in CD13‐positive area was observed between the two groups (Supplementary Figure S2B and C ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Cerebral vessels lesion and elevated CD31 levels related to tau pathology in human AD brains. Representative western blots of the level of CD31 (endothelial marker), αSMA (smooth muscle marker), and PDGFRβ (pericyte marker) in separated cerebral vessels of human non‐ADD ( n = 9) and AD brains ( n = 20). Relative expression of αSMA and PDGFRβ normalized to CD31. (A) While both αSMA/CD31 and PDGFRβ/CD31 in cerebral vessels were reduced in human AD brains compared to non‐AD brains, CD31 levels showed no difference between the two groups. (B) Schematic of extraction of soluble (TBS fraction), membrane (TBSX fraction) and insoluble proteins (GuHCl fraction) from frozen human brains by a three–step ultracentrifugation. (C) Quantification of occludin, Claudin‐5, and CD31 in TBSX fraction by ELISA. Both occludin/CD31 and Claudin‐5/CD31 were reduced in human AD brains ( n = 7) compared to non‐ADD brains ( n = 24). (D) Quantification of Aβ42, Aβ40 and pTau181 in GuHCl fraction by MSD. The high pathological group is defined as having levels of Aβ42, Aβ40, or pTau181 greater than the median of the AD group. (E) Univariate or multivariate analyses was used to test associations between Aβ42, Aβ40, pTau181 in GuHCl fraction and CD31 in TBSX fraction with age, sex, and APOE genotype as covariates. Group differences were assessed using ANCOVA adjusted age, sex, and APOE genotype, following by Bonferroni multiple comparison tests. * p < 0.05, ** p < 0.01. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; ANCOVA, analysis of covariance; APOE, apolipoprotein E. To further assess the integrity of the BBB, we measured levels of the tight junction proteins occludin and Claudin‐5 in the membrane fraction of human brains (Figure 1B ). We observed that the levels of occludin/CD31 and Claudin‐5/CD31 in TBSX fraction (membrane protein) were lower in the brains of individuals with AD compared to those without AD (Figure 1C ). Moreover, we observed significant negative correlations between occludin/CD31 or Claudin‐5/CD31 and both Aβ pathology (levels of insoluble Aβ42 or Aβ40) and tau pathology (levels of insoluble pTau181) (all p < 0.05, Table S3 ), except for Claudin‐5/CD31 and insoluble Aβ40. These findings indicate disruption in the structure of the BBB in human AD brains. Furthermore, we investigated the association between vascular density and AD pathologies in human brains. Immunofluorescence staining revealed a significant increase in CD31+ vessel density in AD brains compared to non‐AD brains (Supplementary Figure S2D and E ). Given that there was no change in CD31 expression of cerebral vessels between the AD and non‐AD groups, we further assessed vascular density in brain tissue by quantified CD31 levels in the membrane fraction of human brains (Figure 1B ). We found that the CD31 was increased in higher AD pathology groups (Aβ42‐H, Aβ40‐H, and pTau181‐H) compared with the non‐AD group (Figure 1D ). In univariate analyses, membrane CD31 was positively associated with insoluble Aβ42 (β = 0.528, p = 0.002) and pTau181 (β = 0.628, p < 0.001), but not with insoluble Aβ40 (β = 0.257, p = 0.164) (Figure 1E ). However, in multivariable analyses, only pTau181 (β = 0.485, p = 0.008) showed a significant association with membrane CD31, while there were no associations for Aβ42 (β = 0.254, p = 0.185) and Aβ40 (β = 0.031, p = 0.847), indicating the critical role of tau pathology in the abnormal elevation of vessel density in AD brains. 3.2. Demographics of clinical cohort In cohort study, 44 individuals with a CDR score = 0, 60 individuals with a CDR score = 0.5 and 96 individuals with a CDR score ≥ 1 were selected. The demographic characteristics of the participants are summarized in Table 1 and Table S4 . Among 200 individuals, 126 (63.0%) were women, 67 (34.7%) were APOE4 carriers, 100 (51.9%) were Aβ positive, 95 (50.3%) were tau positive, and the mean of age was 61.9 years (SD = 8.7 years). There were no significant differences in age between CDR = 0 (mean [SD], 61.30 [8.67] years), CDR = 0.5 (62.65 [8.93] years) and CDR ≥ 1 (61.75 [8.65] years) groups. Expectedly, the CDR = 0.5 and CDR ≥ 1 group are more female ( p = 0.028), more APOE4 carriers ( p < 0.001), more Aβ positive ( p < 0.001) or tau positive individuals ( p < 0.001), and lower MMSE scores ( p < 0.001) than the CDR = 0 group. TABLE 1. Demographic characteristics and biomarkers levels in CANDI. Parameter CDR = 0 CDR = 0.5 CDR ≥ 1 p ‐Value Demographics, clinical characteristics Number 44 60 96 age, years 61.30 (8.67) 62.65 (8.93) 61.75 (8.65) 0.712 Female, n (%) 21 (48) 44 (73) 61 (64) 0.028 APOE4 carriers, n (%) 3 (7.3) 23 (39.0) 41 (44.1) <0.001 MMSE 26.8 (2.9) 22.17 (4.4) 12.25 (6.1) <0.001 A+, n (%) 4 (10.3) 30 (50.8) 66 (69.5) <0.001 T+, n (%) 2 (5.6) 27 (46.6) 66 (69.6) <0.001 AD biomarkers CSF Aβ42/40 0.0894 (0.0177) 0.0640 (0.0260) 0.0581 (0.0227) <0.001 CSF tTau (pg/ml) 81.1 (22.8) 141.8 (90.1) 210.0 (189.1) <0.001 CSF pTau181 (pg/ml) 35.1 (13.0) 74.6 (62.8) 121.8 (106.5) <0.001 Serum NFL (pg/ml) 11.3 (173.6) 16.4 (10.6) 34.7 (35.4) <0.001 Serum GFAP (pg/ml) 123.0 (62.5) 198.5 (124.0) 296.4 (149.5) <0.001 lh_Mean Cortical Thickness 2.360 (0.069) 2.329 (0.090) 2.244 (0.104) <0.001 Cerebrovascular damage factors CSF PDGFRβ (pg/ml) 201.9 (70.2) 276.3 (64.8) 234.8 (59.1) <0.001 Qalb 9.21 (4.65) 10.83 (7.73) 13.10 (9.65) 0.034 WMH volume (mm3) 7002 (6687) 5837 (4857) 11518 (16537) 0.052 Open in a new tab Note : Data are presented as Mean ± standard deviation or number (%). Analyses group differences by