0.098 bFGF (pg/ml) 0.828 (0.586) 0.984 (1.212) 1.225 (2.061) 0.748 IL8 (pg/ml) 52.3 (32.9) 42.0 (11.1) 46.6 (13.8) 0.036 Open in a new tab Data are presented as mean ± standard deviation or number (%). Analyses group differences by analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) adjusted with age, sex, and APOE genotype for continuous variables. Abbreviations: APOE, apolipoprotein E; bFGF, basic fibroblast growth factor; CDR,Clinical Dementia Rating; IL8, interleukin 8; PLGF, placental growth factor; Serpin E1, serpin family E member 1; sTIE2, soluble Tyrosine‐protein kinase receptor 2; sVEGFR, soluble vascular endothelial growth factor receptor; PA, urokinase type plasminogen activator; uPA, urokinase‐type plasminogen activator; VEGFR, vascular endothelial growth factor receptor. To explore the potential relationship between AD biomarkers and angiogenic factors, we examined the associations between angiogenic factors and CSF Aβ42/40, tTau, pTau181, and serum GFAP. In the total participant cohort, elevated levels of CSF sVEGFR1, sTIE2, and bFGF were associated with lower CSF Aβ42/40 and higher CSF tTau or pTau181 (all p < 0.05, Figure 2A ). Moreover, higher levels of CSF sVEGFR2, VEGF‐C, VEGF‐D, PLGF, Angiopoietin2, and Serpin E1 were significantly associated only with CSF tTau or pTau181 (all p < 0.05), rather than CSF Aβ42/40. Although higher levels of uPA were associated with lower serum GFAP (β = ‐0.178, p = 0.045), no significant associations were observed between other CSF angiogenic biomarkers and serum GFAP (all p > 0.05). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Associations between angiogenic biomarkers and AD biomarkers. Forest plots show the association between CSF angiogenic biomarkers and AD biomarkers (Aβ42/40, tTau, pTau181 and GFAP) in Total (left), APOE4‐ (middle), or APOE4+ population (right). (A) β (95% CI) and P values were calculated with linear regression analysis adjusting for age, sex and APOE genotype. A bootstrap mediation analysis revealed that CSF pTau181 almost entirely mediate the associations of Aβ42/40 with sVEGFR1 (B) and sTIE2 (C) . The negative coefficients of CSF Aβ42/40 and angiogenic factors are shown in the forest plots. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; APOE, apolipoprotein E; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; ELISA, enzyme‐linked immunosorbent assay; sTIE2, soluble Tyrosine‐protein kinase receptor 2; sVEGFR1, soluble vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 1. In subgroups stratified by APOE genotypes, the effect sizes of CSF Aβ42/40, tTau, and pTau181 on CSF angiogenic biomarkers (specifically, sVEGFR1, sTIE2, VEGF‐C, VEGF‐D, Angiopoietin2, and bFGF) were greater in the APOE4+ group (Figure 2A ). These findings suggest that the effects of CSF Aβ42/40 and pTau181 on CSF angiogenic biomarkers were modified by the APOE genotype. We further investigated the correlation between AD biomarkers and angiogenic biomarkers in subgroups stratified by CDR score. In the CDR > 0.5 group, we found that CSF Aβ42/40 levels were associated with VEGF‐C (β = −0.299, p = 0.003) and sTIE2 (β = ‐0.225, p = 0.019), while CSF pTau181 levels were associated with Angiopoietin2 (β = 0.238, p = 0.024) and bFGF (β = −0.258, p = 0.015) (Supplementary Figure S3 ). These associations were not observed in the CDR ≤ 0.5 group, suggesting a stronger relationship in the later stage of the disease. 3.4. Mediation analysis among CSF Aβ42/40, pTau181, and angiogenic factors To explore the relationship between CSF Aβ42/40 and CSF angiogenic biomarkers, a bootstrap mediation analysis was conducted to assess whether the effects of CSF Aβ42/40 were dependent on CSF pTau181. After adjusting for age, sex, and APOE genotype, it was observed that CSF pTau181 mediated the associations between CSF Aβ42/40 and sVEGFR1, as well as sTIE2, to a large extent (Indirect / Total Effect: sVEGFR1 = 84%; sTIE2 = 94%, Figure 2B and 1C ). Results from both the human brain tissue cohort and the CANDI cohort suggest that tau pathology may be a crucial contributor in regulating the expression of angiogenic factors. 3.5. Associations between CSF angiogenic factors and cerebrovascular injury Additionally, we examined the association between changes in angiogenic factors and pathological features of cerebrovascular injury, including elevated levels of CSF PDGFRβ, Qalb, and WMH volume. Initially, we employed a linear model with adjustments for age and sex. In the entire participant cohort, increased levels of sVEGFR1 (β = 0.216, p = 0.020) and decreased levels of VEGF‐C (β = −0.191, p = 0.046) were associated with higher PDGFRβ. Lower levels of uPA (β = −0.252, p = 0.002) and higher levels of sVEGFR2 (β = 0.304, p < 0.001), sTIE2 (β = 0.163, p = 0.041), PLGF (β = 0.425, p < 0.001), Serpin E1 (β = 0.167, p = 0.048), and IL8 (β = 0.232, p = 0.003) were associated with higher Qalb. Furthermore, lower levels of sVEGFR1 (β = ‐0.225, p = 0.029) and increased levels of PLGF (β = 0.202, p = 0.039), Serpin E1 (β = 0.203, p = 0.048), and IL8 (β = 0.240, p = 0.010) were associated with higher WMH volume (Supplementary Figure S4 ). To investigate whether the effects of angiogenic biomarkers on cerebrovascular damage were independent of APOE genotype, we conducted a secondary analysis including APOE genotype as a covariate. While there were slight alterations in effect sizes for sTIE2 with WMH volume (β = ‐0.213, p = 0.039) and VEGF‐C with PDGFRβ (β = ‐0.183, p = 0.069), the majority of associations were not been affected by APOE genotype (Figure 3A ). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Associations between angiogenic biomarkers and cerebrovascular damage biomarkers. Forest plots show the association between CSF angiogenic biomarkers and cerebrovascular damage biomarkers (PDGFRβ, Qalb and WMH volume). β (95%CI) and p values were calculated with linear regression analysis adjusting for age, sex and APOE genotype. APOE, apolipoprotein E; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; PDGFRβ, platelet‐derived growth factor receptor ß; Qalb, albumin to serum albumin; WMH, white matter hyperintensities. 3.6. CSF angiogenic factors, neurodegeneration, and cognitive function at baseline At baseline, we examined the associations between CSF angiogenic biomarkers and neurodegeneration (cortical thickness and serum NFL levels), and cognitive function (MMSE scores) (Table 3 ). In the entire cohort, lower levels of sVEGFR1 (β = −0.226, p = 0.012), sTIE2 (β = ‐0.188, p = 0.027), and Angiopoietin2 (β = ‐0.193, p = 0.025), and higher levels of VEGF‐C (β = 0.206, p = 0.015) and uPA (β = 0.207, p = 0.016) were significantly associated with increased cortical thickness. Consistently, lower levels of sTIE2 (β = −0.153, p = 0.030) and PLGF (β = ‐0.222, p = 0.003), and higher levels of uPA (β = 0.281, p < 0.001) were significantly corelated with higher MMSE scores. Moreover, uPA was negatively associated with (β = −0.194, p = 0.012), while sTIE2 (β = 0.157, p = 0.039), PLGF (β = 0.239, p = 0.003), and IL8 (β = 0.200, p = 0.010) were positively associated with serum NFL. TABLE 3. Associations between CSF angiogenic biomarkers and cognitive function. Parameter Mean cortical thickness MMSE NFL Standardized β p ‐Value Standardized β p ‐Value Standardized β p ‐Value sVEGFR1 − 0.226 0.012 −0.038 0.617 −0.094 0.255 sTIE2 − 0.188 0.027 − 0.153 0.030 0.157 0.039 VEGF‐A 0.206 0.015 −0.046 0.520 0.009 0.901 VEGF‐C −0.034 0.702 0.122 0.094 −0.139 0.076 VEGF‐D −0.126 0.142 −0.101 0.159 0.041 0.599 PLGF −0.015 0.865 − 0.222 0.003 0.239 0.003 Angiopoietin2 − 0.193 0.025 −0.030 0.668 0.059 0.445 bFGF 0.075 0.376 −0.052 0.467 0.083 0.278 uPA 0.207 0.016 0.281 0.000 − 0.194 0.012 Serpin E1 −0.061 0.535 −0.051 0.525 0.149 0.093 IL8 0.006 0.944 −0.023 0.748 0.200 0.010 Open in a new tab Note : β (standardized coefficient) and p values were calculated with linear regression analysis adjusting for age, sex, and APOE genotype. Abbreviations: APOE, apolipoprotein E; bFGF, basic fibroblast growth factor; IL8, interleukin 8; MMSE, Mini‐Mental State Examination; NFL, neurofilament light chain; PLGF, placental growth factor; Serpin E1, serpin family E member 1; sTIE2, soluble Tyrosine‐protein kinase receptor 2; uPA, urokinase‐type plasminogen activator; VEGF, vascular endothelial growth factor; VEGFR, VEGF receptor. 3.7. Associations of CSF angiogenic factors with and cognitive decline and brain atrophy Finally, we explored the associations of CSF angiogenic factors with cognitive decline (Δ_MMSE) and brain atrophy (Δ_Cortical_thickness, Δ_Hippo_volume and Δ_Amy_volume) over time. After adjusting for age, sex, and APOE genotype, higher sTIE2 (β = ‐0.254, p = 0.037) and bFGF (β = ‐0.300, p = 0.013) were associated with accelerated decline in MMSE score (Figure 4A ). In the longitudinal MRI analyses, higher sTIE2 and Angiopoietin2 were significantly associated with accelerated brain atrophy (sTIE2: [βΔ_Cortical_thickness = ‐0.245, p = 0.047], [βΔ_Hippo_volume = ‐0.316, p = 0.010], [βΔ_Amy_volume = ‐0.263, p = 0.036]; Angiopoietin2: [βΔ_Amy_volume = ‐0.270, p = 0.045]). By contrast, higher uPA and IL8 were significantly associated with less brain atrophy (uPA: [βΔ_Amy_volume = 0.249, p = 0.047]; ‐IL8: [βΔ_Cortical_thickness = ‐0.350, p = 0.006]) (Figure 4B‐D ). FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Associations of angiogenic biomarkers with cognitive decline and longitudinal neurodegeneration. Forest plots show the associations of CSF angiogenic biomarkers with Δ_MMSE (MMSE score change per month) (A) and brain atrophy (Δ_Cortical_thickness, Δ_Hippo_volume and Δ_Amy_volume) (B‐D) . β (95%CI) and p values were calculated with linear mixed‐effect models with age, sex and APOE genotype as covariates. APOE, apolipoprotein E; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; MMSE, Mini‐Mental State Examination. 4. DISCUSSION The present study investigated the relationships between pathological angiogenesis and cerebrovascular injury and neurodegeneration in post mortem human AD brains and a clinical AD cohort. Our findings revealed that elevated vascular marker CD31 exhibited a more robust correlation with tau pathology and BBB impairment in human brains. Meanwhile, higher levels of CSF angiogenic factors also showed a stronger association with CSF pTau181 in the CANDI cohort. Furthermore, the abnormal angiogenic biomarkers were found to be linked to brain atrophy and cognitive decline. Notably, only angiogenic factors linked to the VEGF pathway (CSF sVEGFR1, sVEGFR2, VEGF‐C, and PLGF) and the Angiopoietin‐TIE pathway (CSF sTIE2) were associated with both AD biomarkers (CSF Aβ42/40 or pTau181) and vascular injury, suggesting their predominant role in the pathogenic angiogenesis of AD. These results collectively indicated that abnormal CSF angiogenic factors, influenced by tau pathology, play a significant role in cerebrovascular injury and contribute to the development of AD. Physiological angiogenesis is typically highly active during brain development and becomes nearly quiescent in the adult brain. 20 However, certain neurological diseases like AD can reactivate pathological angiogenesis, 7 , 29 the exact mechanism of angiogenesis in AD remains unclear. In the present study, multivariate analysis indicated insoluble pTau181, rather than Aβ42 or Aβ40, displayed a predominantly relationship with CD31 in the AD brain. Moreover, data from the clinical AD cohort revealed that higher level of CSF angiogenic biomarker sVEGFR1, sTIE2, VEGF‐C, VEGF‐D, Angiopoientin2, bFGF, and Serpin E1 were associated with CSF pTau181, while only CSF sVEGFR1, sTIE2, and bFGF were positively associated with CSF Aβ42/40. Meanwhile, mediation analysis revealed that the relationships among CSF Aβ42/40, CSF sVEGFR1 (proportion: 84.4%), and sTIE2 (proportion: 94.0%) were largely mediated by CSF pTau181. Consistent with these findings, an increase in angiogenic factors and blood vessel density was observed in tau transgenic mice, but not in APP transgenic mice. 11 Furthermore, a study by Popp et al. 30 demonstrated that CSF levels of sVEGFR1 and VEGF‐D were correlated with CSF Tau and pTau181 levels, rather than the Aβ42/40 ratio. Therefore, tau pathologies appear to be the main contributor to the elevated levels of CSF angiogenic factors. Nevertheless, Aβ, as the earliest driving factor in AD, 31 triggers downstream tau pathology and may also plays a crucial role in the abnormal angiogenesis observed in AD. The abnormal growth of blood vessels has been suggested to be associated with cerebrovascular damage. 19 , 32 , 33 Presently, we also observed an increased endothelial cell marker CD31, in human AD brains, along with an abnormal BBB structure, characterized by decreased levels of occludin, Claudin‐5, the pericyte marker PDGFRβ, and the smooth muscle marker αSMA. Consistently, clinical cohort data displayed that an elevated level of PLGF, Serpin E1, and IL8 were linked to BBB leakage (measured with Qalb) and WMH volume. Moreover, we observed that higher level of sVEGFR2 and sTIE2 were associated with BBB leakage. The Angiopoietin1‐TIE2 pathway is believed to promote pericyte recruitment and vascular stabilization. 34 , 35 The increasing sTIE2 may interfere with this pathway and promote the BBB leakage. Recently, an increased concentration of PLGF was reported to be associated with greater WMH volume. 36 Therefore, not only PLGF but also Serpin E1, IL8, and sVEGFR2 were associated with cerebrovascular damage. An interesting finding of this study was the association between higher levels of sVEGFR1 and lower WMH, as well as worsened pericyte damage (measured with CSF PDGFRβ level). One possible explanation is that sVEGFR1 can hinder abnormal angiogenesis by trapping VEGF factors, 37 thereby mitigating the occurrence of WMH. Conversely, Renhai et al. 38 demonstrated that sVEGFR1 is primarily responsible for VEGF‐mediated pericyte ablation in the retinal vasculature, which, in turn, contributes to exacerbated pericyte damage. Pathological angiogenesis can induce morphological and functional abnormalities in capillaries, including spiraling morphologies, reduced vessel diameters, and impaired blood flow. 11 This is closely linked to another vascular pathological feature, string vessels, which are characterized by an extremely small diameter (1‐2 µm). Elevated string vessels in AD brains are considered dysfunctional capillaries. 39 , 40 Given the close relationship between string vessels and angiogenesis, we propose that the abnormal new vessels in AD brains are loss‐of‐function vessels that may evolve into dysfunctional string vessels as the disease progresses. Previous studies have identified an increase in dysfunctional string vessels in human AD brains, which was associated with Aβ pathology. However, the underlying mechanisms remain unclear. Interestingly, pathological angiogenesis also leads to abnormalities in vascular structure and function. Since both string vessels and angiogenesis primarily involve capillaries, we propose that the abnormal formation of new vessels in AD brains results in loss‐of‐function vessels, which may evolve into dysfunctional string vessels as the disease progresses. Abnormal angiogenesis leading to abnormalities in vascular structure and function may be related to other pathological cerebrovascular features in AD brains. For example, elevated string vessels in AD brains, defined as extremely thin diameter (1‐2 µm), were loss of functional capillaries. The abnormal angiogenesis‐mediated cerebrovascular lesion may further promote neurodegeneration and cognitive impairment. 41 , 42 In line with this, we observed that sVEGFR1, PLGF, and IL8 were associated with lower cortical thickness or greater serum NFL level at baseline. In longitudinal study, Oskar et al. 36 , 43 found sVEGFR1, PLGF, and IL8 were associated with cognitive decline in early AD. Thus, the increased CSF angiogenic factors were associated with worse neurodegeneration. Intriguingly, our cross‐sectional and longitudinal results showed that the higher levels of sTIE2 and Angiopoietin2 were also associated with brain atrophy, MMSE score decline or increased serum NFL, which may be caused by elevated Angiopoietin2 antagonistically impairing the Angiopoietin1‐TIE2 pathway to exacerbate vascular permeability. 44 Moreover, some angiogenic factors also play a role in non‐angiogenic process. For example, VEGF could promote neurogenesis and neurite growth in physiological condition, 45 while the pro‐angiogenic factor uPA may play a neuroprotective role by inhibiting soluble Aβ‐induced synaptic damage. 46 Consistently, we also observed that the VEGF‐A and uPA were positively associated with the cortical thickness. Thus, these angiogenic factors have distinct functions in cerebrovascular lesions and neurodegeneration. There are some limitations that need to be declared in this study. First, the simple ethnicity, limited size, and single district of the participants. Second, additional validated cohorts and longitudinal studies are required to investigate whether aberrant angiogenic factors at baseline might increase the risk of cerebrovascular lesions and neurodegeneration. Thirdly, despite observing an association between tau pathology and aberrant angiogenic factors in the current cohort, the downstream mechanism of tau pathology‐induced pathological angiogenesis is not clear. Finally, while elevated CD31 levels suggest endothelial cell proliferation in human AD brains, a more thorough assessment is required to accurately determine vascular density in the future. In conclusion, the findings of this study support the notion that tau pathology, rather than Aβ pathology, plays a significant role in influencing the expression of angiogenic factors, leading to abnormal angiogenesis in patients with AD. Furthermore, we discovered a close association between aberrant CSF angiogenic factors and cerebrovascular damage, including pericyte injury, BBB leakage, WMH, and neurodegeneration. These observations suggest that pathological angiogenesis may represent a novel mechanism contributing to cerebrovascular damage and neurodegeneration in the AD. This highlights the potential therapeutic value of inhibiting excessive blood vessel growth or promoting the maturation of immature blood vessels in AD. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Author disclosures are available in the Supporting Information . CONSENT STATEMENT This study was approved by the ethics committee of the First Affiliated Hospital of the University of Science and Technology China (2023KY‐117). All patients (or immediate family members) provided written informed consent to participate in this study. Supporting information Supporting Information ALZ-21-e14521-s002.pdf (212.3KB, pdf) Supporting Information ALZ-21-e14521-s003.docx (25.5KB, docx) Supporting Information ALZ-21-e14521-s001.pdf (841.4KB, pdf) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We extend our deepest thanks to all the patients who participated in this study. This work was supported by the National Natural Sciences Foundation of China (82030034, W2411069 and 82371418), the Chinese Academy of Sciences (XDB39000000), the National Key Plan for Scientific Research and Development of China (2020YFA0509304 and 2021YFA0805300). Gao F, Zhan Y, Wang Q, Zhang M, Dai L, Shen Y. Pathological angiogenesis was associated with cerebrovascular lesion and neurodegeneration in Alzheimer''s disease. Alzheimer''s Dement. 2025;21:e14521. 10.1002/alz.14521 Feng Gao and Yaxi Zhan contributed equally to this work. Contributor Information Feng Gao, Email: fenggao7@ustc.edu.cn. Yong Shen, Email: yongshen@ustc.edu.cn. REFERENCES 1. Long JM, Holtzman DM. Alzheimer Disease: an update on pathobiology and treatment Strategies. Cell. 2019;179:312‐339. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Nation DA, Sweeney MD, Montagne A, et al. Blood‐brain barrier breakdown is an early biomarker of human cognitive dysfunction. Nat Med. 2019;25:270‐276. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cuadrado‐Godia E, Dwivedi P, Sharma S